{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are a Flint ingest atomizer. Task: atomize *Supertraining* (Siff and Verkhoshansky, 6th Expanded Edition) Chapter 7 \"Strength Training Methods\", batch 7a: the chapter lead-in (from PDF idx 404) and section 7.1 \"Bodybuilding and Other Strength Training Methods\", PDF idx 404-413 (printed 393-402). Stop where 7.2 begins (PDF 414). Another atomizer is working at the same time on 7.2-7.6 (PDF 414-431); do not atomize its pages.\n\n## Read first, in full\n1. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md` (every rule is checked by a reviewer; Windows paths in it, use the Mac translations below).\n2. `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/ingest-book/SKILL.md` (schema, frontmatter keys, file kinds).\n3. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/method-baseline.md` (Eric's approved baseline; quote lines verbatim with line number AND tag).\n4. `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md` (map; page key PDF idx = printed + 11, 0-based).\n5. Lessons from the Chapter 5 and 6 reviews: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6b_opus.md` and `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6d_opus.md`. Common misses: unlabelled writer judgments, \"contested\" set for things that are not real contradictions (different questions, populations or quantities), misread figure values, baseline quotes without tags, invented examples, process notes in entry bodies (never mention batches or other agents in an entry).\n6. Two existing entries in the output folder as style references.\n\n## Mac paths\n- Vault library (read-only, dedup greps): `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`\n- Staging library: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library`\n- Output folder: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`\n- Source PDF (noisy text layer): `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`\n- Text: `~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"import pymupdf; d=pymupdf.open('<pdf>'); print(d[N].get_text())\"`. Render every page with a figure, table or number to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/7a_pN.png` (`d[N].get_pixmap(dpi=200).save(...)`) and open it with Read. Write \"about\" for values read off a graph. Only claim \"verified against the page image\" if you opened it.\n\n## Hard rules\n- Add NEW entry files only. Do NOT edit any existing entry and do NOT edit `_book.md` (the coordinator owns it). Chapter 6 entries are being fixed right now in the same folder; do not link to any file whose name starts with a Ch6 topic unless it already existed before today's Ch6 work (prefer links to Ch1-5 and vault entries). Dedup against vault and staging: bodybuilding methods, sets/reps schemes, hypertrophy and intensity methods may be covered by Zatsiorsky, Bompa, Supertraining Ch1-5, and research-paper briefs. Add an entry only for a genuinely new angle and link the existing one in Related; where another library source disagrees, record it and say which. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your report.\n- Use the source's own examples, otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\". No mechanism the authors do not give. `contested` only for a real contradiction (same question, same quantity) or a named library source that disagrees; give both pages. Locators \"PDF p. X / printed p. Y\" on every number, definition and quote. \"The authors\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author (a passage naming Verkhoshansky in the third person is Siff writing; bodybuilding material is likely Siff's voice: check).\n- Frontmatter `chapter:` uses the section number (e.g. \"7.1\"). `created: 2026-10-03`. No em dashes in titles or body (section headers keep `**What it is** —`). No review history. Never wrap a [[wikilink]] across lines. Blank line between sections.\n- Quality over coverage; stop at a clean boundary if needed and say where.\n- Write a scope file `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7a.txt` listing your new filenames, one per line.\n- Lint: `cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky`; fix anything flagged in YOUR files and paste the lines for your files.\n- No git. No vault edits.\n\n## Final reply\nEntries by kind and confidence; entry count per section with printed and PDF page ranges (for `_book.md`); where you stopped; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots; anything you could not read; lint output.\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \"tool not found\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\n    ToolSearch(query: \"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\")\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n</context_window_protection>"},"uuid":"a4f1febf-ea4c-4b58-ba29-cd557e2fcfd2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:06.690Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
