s entries are the\nextractor's 0-based page index (printed + 11), matching the offset above. In a normal\n1-based PDF viewer (and the Read tool's `pages` parameter), the same page shows as\nprinted + 12, i.e. one more than the entries' \"PDF p.\" number.\n\n**Confidence rule for this book:** `proven` only for findings that are established,\nwidely-replicated physiology or mechanics (e.g. force-velocity relationship, motor unit\nrecruitment order, connective tissue loading response) independent of this text.\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"cf8d5d09-b504-4a10-b390-2a6d67d45c5b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:12.038Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\ntype: book-index\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\ntitle: \"Supertraining (6th Edition, Expanded)\"\nauthor: Yuri Verkhoshansky and Mel C. Siff\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\ningest-tier: full-text\nsource: \"TB's Training Info/Verkhoshansky/Supertraining (6th Edition - Expanded Version) - Yuri Verkhoshansky & Mel Siff.pdf (Drive); staging copy md5 d3453c5e459eb08ec0a89a4300a66e54\"\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# Supertraining (6th Edition, Expanded) — ingest map\n\nPDF is real text: 591 pages, 576 with extractable text (`extract_pdf.py --probe`:\n`{\"pages\": 591, \"text_pages\": 576, \"scanned\": false}`), not scanned.\n\n**Print-to-PDF page offset = +11** (PDF page = printed page + 11). Verified at six\nindependent chapter/section starts, not just the offset's origin point: Ch1 (printed 1 =\nPDF 12, \"STRENGTH AND THE MUSCULAR SYSTEM\"), Ch2 (printed 95 = PDF 106, \"SPORT SPECIFIC\nSTRENGTH TRAINING\"), Ch3 (printed 125 = PDF 136, \"FACTORS INFLUENCING STRENGTH\nPRODUCTION\"), Ch4 (printed 201 = PDF 212, \"THE MEANS OF SPECIAL STRENGTH TRAINING\"), Ch7\n(printed 393 = PDF 404, \"STRENGTH TRAINING METHODS\"), and the Addendum's second section\n(printed 563 = PDF 574, \"9.2 The Shock Method for Developing Power\"). The offset holds\nunchanged from the front matter through page 574 of 591; no evidence of a mid-book\nrepagination (no unnumbered plate inserts were found at the checked points).\n\n**PDF numbers are 0-based:** the \"PDF p. N\" locators in this book's entries are the\nextractor's 0-based page index (printed + 11), matching the offset above. In a normal\n1-based PDF viewer (and the Read tool's `pages` parameter), the same page shows as\nprinted + 12, i.e. one more than the entries' \"PDF p.\" number.\n\n**Confidence rule for this book:** `proven` only for findings that are established,\nwidely-replicated physiology or mechanics (e.g. force-velocity relationship, motor unit\nrecruitment order, connective tissue loading response) independent of this text.\nSoviet-era empirical findings and training prescriptions original to Verkhoshansky, Siff,\nor the Soviet sport-science tradition they cite are capped at `supported`, even when\nstated with confidence in the text, because they are largely single-lab or\npractice-derived rather than independently replicated in the modern literature available\nto this ingest. `contested` is set where modern evidence, or another library book, takes\na different position (e.g. the two-factor fitness-fatigue model vs. classical\nsupercompensation, already flagged as a live tension in `periodization--bompa`).\n\n| # | Chapter | Printed pp | PDF idx | Status | Entries |\n|---|---------|-----------|---------|--------|---------|\n| 1 | Strength and the Muscular System | 1-94 | 12-105 | done | 41 (see section rows below) |\n| 1.intro | Objectives … Strength Deficit (unnumbered lead-in) | 1-9 | 12-20 | done | 6 |\n| 1.1 | Preliminary Issues | 10-16 | 21-27 | done | 6 |\n| 1.2 | Fundamental Biomechanics of Strength | 17-23 | 28-34 | done | 5 |\n| 1.3 | A Philosophy of Physical Training | 24-26 | 35-37 | done | 1 |\n| 1.4 | Specificity in Training | 27-31 | 38-42 | done | 3 |\n| 1.5 | Strength and Fitness | 32-32 | 43-43 | done | 1 |\n| 1.6 | The Nature of Strength | 33-35 | 44-46 | done | 3 |\n| 1.7 | The Muscle Complex | 35-48 | 46-59 | done | 4 |\n| 1.8-1.11 | Classification, Cocontraction, Types, Triphasic Nature of Muscle Action | 49-54 | 60-65 | done | 2 |\n| 1.12 | Types of Muscle Fibre | 55-64 | 66-75 | done | 2 |\n| 1.13 | The Mechanism of Muscle Growth | 65-68 | 76-79 | done | 1 |\n| 1.14 | Neurophysiological Aspects of Exercise | 69-72 | 80-83 | done | 1 |\n| 1.15 | Bioenergetics and the Energy Systems | 73-81 | 84-92 | done | 2 |\n| 1.16 | Adaptation and the Training Effect | 82-94 | 93-105 | done | 4 |\n| 2 | Sport Specific Strength Training | 95-124 | 106-135 | done | 12 (see section rows below) |\n| 2.1.1 | Increasing the Working Effect of Movements | 97-99 | 108-110 | done | 1 |\n| 2.1.2 | Perfecting the Motor Pattern of Sports Movements | 100-104 | 111-115 | done | 3 (one entry also spans 2.2.2) |\n| 2.1.3 | Perfecting the Kinesiological Pattern of Movements | 105-106 | 116-117 | done | 1 |\n| 2.2.2 | Sports Implications of Strength Indices | 109 | 120 | done | 2 (includes the entry that also spans 2.1.2) |\n| 2.2.3 | The Functional Topography of the Muscular System | 110-111 | 121-122 | done | 1 |\n| 2.2.4 | Motor Specialisation in Developing Sports Mastery | 112-115 | 123-126 | done | 1 |\n| 2.3.1 | The Structure of Physical Fitness | 116 | 127 | done | 1 |\n| 2.3.2 | The Interrelation between Motor Abilities | 117-119 | 128-130 | done | 2 |\n| 2.3.4 | General Concepts of the Structure of Physical Fitness | 120-124 | 131-135 | done | 1 |\n| 3 | Factors Influencing Strength Production | 125-200 | 136-211 | done | 60 (see section rows below) |\n| 3.1 | Regimes of Muscular Work | 125-128 | 136-139 | done | 2 |\n| 3.2.1 | Explosive Strength | 129-141 | 140-152 | done | 5 |\n| 3.2.2 | Strength-Endurance | 142-143 | 153-154 | done | 1 |\n| 3.3.1-3.3.9 | Influence of External Conditions on Strength (pre-working state through the secular increase in strength) | 144-161 | 155-172 | done | 13 |\n| 3.4.1 | The Warm-up and Pre-Activity Preparation | 162-163 | 173-174 | done | 1 