the 3.5.6 restatement (printed 181) of \"bony\ninsertion weakest at slow loading rate, soft tissue weakest at fast loading rate\" was NOT\nre-atomized, since it duplicates [[claim--collagen-loading-curve-and-injury-risk]] (Ch1,\nKennedy 1976/Frankel & Nordin 1980); only the genuinely new adjacent detail (the graded\nbone-tendon transitional-stiffness system and its disruption by anabolic steroids) got its\nown entry, [[claim--tendon-insertion-transitional-stiffness-gradient-disrupted-by-steroids]].\nChapters 4-9 remain pending for later sessions; resume at Chapter 4, The Means of Special\nStrength Training (printed 201, PDF idx 212)._\n\n_Batch boundary, this session: Chapter 4 section 4.1 \"The Problem of Training Means\" (4.1.1\nCharacteristics of Strength Increase, 4.1.2 The Effect of the Strength Training Means;\nprinted 201-206, PDF idx 212-217) is done, 6 entries. The short unnumbered lead-in before\n4.1 (page 201/PDF 212, history-of-the-field narrative) produced no entry of its own; it is\nscene-setting with no discrete claim. One dedup note: the 4.1.1 passage at printed 202\n(PDF 213) repeats, via the same Verkhoshansky 1977 citation chain, the\nKorobkov/Gerasimov/Vasiliev \"strength gains are roughly uniform at 20/40/60/80% of 1RM in\nearly training\" finding and the Hettinger & Muller (1953) one-third-max threshold finding\nalready captured in [[claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]] (Ch1, 1.2); that\nidentical material was NOT re-atomized. The genuinely new adjacent material in 4.1.1\n(Zimkin 1961 and Muller & Hettinger 1957 training-age-dependent load percentages;\nChernyavsky 1966 light/heavy load-proportion result) became\n[[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]]. Two further new\nclaim entries cover delayed onset of strength gains\n([[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]]) and detraining/\nretention timelines, including an unreconciled internal contradiction between the\nChernyavsky 20-session decay curve and the Vasiliev/McMorris & Eklins 40-session retention\ndata ([[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]]). Section 4.1.2's \"Algebraic\nRelations\" passage (Commutative/Associative Laws applied to training order) and its\nAcute/Delayed Training Effect and Particular/Cumulative Effect taxonomy became two framework\nentries ([[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]],\n[[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]]); the remaining\n\"Steps Towards Structuring the Training Process\" material (training load's five\ncharacteristics; volume's two functions; structured system beats random combination even at\nlower volume, citing Verkhoshansky 1966/1970, Tatyan 1974, Khodykin 1975; Figure 4.1,\nverified against the page image) became\n[[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]]. Chapter\n4 sections 4.2 (Different Means of Strength Development: electrostimulation, resistance and\nkinetic-energy training, isometric, eccentric, isokinetic, machines, symmetric training,\nmuscle isolation), 4.3 (Dynamic Correspondence) and 4.4 (Strength Training and General\nEndurance) remain pending; resume at 4.2, printed 207 / PDF idx 218, \"4.2 Different Means of\nStrength Development\" / \"4.2.1 Electrostimulation.\"_\n\n_Batch boundary, this session: Chapter 4 section 4.2 \"Different Means of Strength\nDevelopment\" is now fully done, all nine subsections (4.2.1 Electrostimulation through 4.2.9\nMuscle Isolation; printed 207-241, PDF idx 218-252), 35 entries total (8+4+3+7+2+6+3+1+1 per\nthe section rows above). Every table and figure encountered was opened as a page image\n(Figs 4.2, 4.3, 4.6, 4.7, 4.8, 4.11, 4.12, 4.13, 4.14, 4.16) before being cited; Figs 4.4,\n4.5, 4.9 and 4.10 are also cited and were checked against the page images (PDF idx 230 and\n234). Fig 4.15 (PDF idx 239, isometric endurance against forearm-immersion temperature,\nClarke et al 1958; its caption is image-only) is cited in\n[[claim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases]] and was checked against the page\nimage. Voice: 4.2.1\nElectrostimulation has first-person Siff material only on printed p.215: \"the ES training\napplied to the author by Serge Reding\" (Siff as the subject) and \"Work that I witnessed\" in\nRatov's Moscow laboratory. The printed p.209 Reding passage names Siff in the third person\n(\"one of the authors (Siff)\"), and the printed p.208 Ratov passage is the authors'.\n[[case-study--reding-electrostimulation-protocol-siff-account]] and\n[[claim--ratov-functional-electrostimulation-emg-triggered-sport-specific]] attribute each\npassage accordingly. The MACS/MICS \"integrated theory\" of\nelectrostimulation (4.2.1.4-4.2.1.5, citing Becker, Nordenstrom and Pilla on bioelectric\ncell-signalling) is rated `contested`, with its mainstream-status judgment labelled\nInference, rather than presented as settled physiology;\nsee [[claim--macs-mics-integrated-theory-of-electrostimulation]]. Two forward/backward\ncross-references the book itself makes were completed as reciprocal links: the 4.2.2 passage\non load-dependent proximal/distal muscle recruitment explicitly forward-references 4.2.9\n(now [[claim--muscle-recruitment-consistency-and-proximal-distal-shift-with-load]] linked to\n[[claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load]]), and the\nCh3.3.4.4 entry [[claim--natural-asymmetry-caution-against-isokinetic-symmetry-correction]]\nexplicitly deferred \"symmetric training\" to 4.2.8, now fulfilled by\n[[claim--pursuing-perfect-bilateral-symmetry-can-be-counterproductive]] (reciprocal link\nrecommended on the Ch3 entry, not made since it is outside this session's assigned\nwrite-scope). [[heuristic--isokinetic-testing-limitations-for-functional-sport-specificity]]\n(4.2.6.2) substantially extends the existing Ch2 entry\n[[heuristic--isometric-dynamometry-misrepresents-dynamic-functional-strength]] with a\ndetailed, itemised mechanism list; a reciprocal link on that Ch2 entry is recommended but not\nmade (outside this session's write-scope). No entries were needed for chapter 4's own\nelectrostimulation/isometric/eccentric/isokinetic/symmetric-training/muscle-isolation\nconcepts already covered elsewhere in this folder or in the vault; dedup was checked against\nMoyer's `framework--isometric-training-variables-and-adaptations` (vault, a distinct\ndose-variable framework, not duplicated), Handford 2024's eccentric-training-methods paper\n(vault, not duplicated, no direct overlap found), and Bishop 2016's asymmetry paper (vault,\nalready linked from the Ch3 entry this session's 4.2.8 entry completes). Chapter 4 sections\n4.3 (Dynamic Correspondence as a Means of Strength) and 4.4 (Strength Training and General\nEndurance) remain pending; resume at 4.3, printed 241 / PDF idx 252, \"4.3 Dynamic\nCorrespondence as a Means of Strength\" / \"4.3.1 The Amplitude and Direction of Movement.\"_\n\n_Batch boundary, this session: Chapter 4 sections 4.3 \"Dynamic Correspondence as a Means of\nStrength Training\" (4.3.1-4.3.6, including the \"Parachute Resistance Training\" subsection;\nprinted 241-248, PDF idx 252-259) and 4.4 \"Strength Training and General Endurance\" (with its\nunnumbered subsections Oxidative Capacity and Muscular Endurance, Strength Training and General\nEndurance, The Process of Functional Specialisation, Factor Analysis; printed 248-253, PDF idx\n259-264) are done: 13 and 10 entries. Two page notes for the rows above: 4.3.6 runs onto\nprinted 248 (PDF 259), where 4.4 starts mid-page; and printed 254 (PDF 265) is blank. Figs\n4.17-4.24 and Tables 4.1-4.2 were checked against page images (PDF 253-257, 260-264).\nSupertraining had no entry for the five dynamic-correspondence criteria themselves (only the\nCh1.4 ten-dimension specificity entry and Evans's applied case study);\n[[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]] is now the anchor. Contested in this\nbatch: [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]]\n(raise VO2 max p.249 vs not p.250; less glycolysis p.249 vs high glycolytic productivity\np.253) and [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]]\n(Pattengale & Holloszy 1967 is a treadmill-running study by its title). Chapter 4 is now\ncomplete; resume at Chapter 5, The Methods of Special Strength Training, printed 255 / PDF idx\n266._\n\n_Batch boundary, 2026-10-03 (Mac): Chapter 5 complete in three parallel batches (5a: 5.1-5.2.3, 19 entries; 5b: 5.2.4-5.2.5, 30; 5c: 5.3-5.6, 26), each through an independent round-1 review, fixes, and a round-2 review (reviews and fix logs in `staging/review_supertraining_ch5{a,b,c}_opus*.md` and `staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5{a,b,c}_applied.md`). Section boundaries fall mid-page at PDF 266, 278, 296 and 298. Resume at Chapter 6, Organisation of Training, printed 313 / PDF idx 324._\n     344\n_book.md\ncase-study--alternate-leg-bounding-with-thigh-cuffs-for-sprinters.md\ncase-study--antonova-and-levchenko-high-strength-volume-lowers-explosive-qualities.md\ncase-study--arm-throw-height-after-barbell-press-tonic-work.md\ncase-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers.md\ncase-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means.md\ncase-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification.md\ncase-study--junior-sprinters-short-and-long-jump-training-effects.md\ncase-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers.md\ncase-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments.md\ncase-study--reding-electrostimulation-protocol-siff-account.md\ncase-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners.md\ncase-study--skater-factor-structure-by-qualification.md\ncase-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions.md\ncase-study--tabachnik-100m-sprinter-model-characteristics.md\ncase-study--triple-jumper-plantarflex