{"parentUuid":"2b425cfb-1422-4101-9692-c0c8c3cdc691","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["<context_guidance>\n  <tip>\n    Reading to Edit the file? Read is correct — Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against.\n    Reading to analyze, summarize, or extract from the file? Use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code) — the bytes stay in the sandbox and only what your code prints enters your conversation.\n  </tip>\n</context_guidance>"],"hookName":"PreToolUse:Read","toolUseID":"toolu_01Rz3kVTE1JNNtfCjVVXwAbK","hookEvent":"PreToolUse"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"e098d9dc-4a11-4a5c-aa44-bcf3f643dfd8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:13.638Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nPreToolUse:Read hook additional context: <context_guidance>\n  <tip>\n    Reading to Edit the file? Read is correct — Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against.\n    Reading to analyze, summarize, or extract from the file? Use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code) — the bytes stay in the sandbox and only what your code prints enters your conversation.\n  </tip>\n</context_guidance>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c1bd450a-f4ec-4e69-990d-cdcc6deec826","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rz3kVTE1JNNtfCjVVXwAbK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Eric's method baseline (draft for Eric's review)\n2\t\n3\tPurpose: this is the step-1 baseline for Flint. It pulls every position, practice, and decision rule out of Eric's own 23-document corpus (Baylor Applied Performance playbook, protocols, assessment manual, professional writing, and HHPR 5355 graduate-course material) so that every future synthesis note comparing an outside source (Landon Evans, TB's Training Info, Old Bull, Harries RTP, SCN/Moyer, etc.) has something of Eric's own to compare against. Date: 2026-09-26. Revised 2026-09-28 with Eric's method interview (`docs/interview/2026-09-28-method-interview.md`, 30 of 30 answered).\n4\t\n5\t## Provenance note (read this before the bullets)\n6\t\n7\tNot all 23 documents are Eric's own words, and the two richest sources (the class transcripts) have a diarization problem. Read this once, then use these tags:\n8\t\n9\t- **Eric-authored, high confidence**: the two SportSmith articles, the Performance Playbook Development Plan, the Force Plate Testing V3 and Iso Seated Calf protocols, the Applied Performance Playbook (departmental document under his unit), and the HD ODSF metrics note (adopted practice, though the framework itself is Hawkin Dynamics' own).\n10\t- **Course slide decks (Week 1, Week 3, Class 4)**: co-branded to Ruf/Rash/Wenger on every title slide. Per `spring-2026-teaching.md`, Eric was the assigned instructor for the Feb. 5 and Feb. 12 sessions (Week 3 Lecture and Class 4 2026 respectively), so bullets from those two decks are marked **Explicit**. Week 1 (Jan. 22, \"All\") and Class 8/Lesson 8 (Mar. 19, Wenger's session) are marked **Inferred** — shared team material, not confirmed as Eric's individual position.\n11\t- **Both class transcripts (Class 1, Class 3) are diarized entirely under one Fireflies speaker label, \"Chris Ruf,\" for every line, across 100+ minutes combined.** This is true even for Class 3 (Feb. 5), which the syllabus assigns solo to Rash. Fireflies likely defaulted to the meeting organizer's name rather than doing real per-speaker diarization. That means specific sentences in these transcripts cannot be confidently attributed to Rash vs. Ruf vs. Wenger. Bullets drawn from the transcripts are marked **Inferred**, with a citation note, even where the statement itself is a direct, confident, first-person claim in the text. Flint should treat these as \"the HHPR 5355 teaching team's shared position, individual speaker unconfirmed\" until Eric ratifies them.\n12\t  - **Confirmed by Eric, 2026-09-26:** HHPR 5355 is taught each spring by Chris Ruf, Eric and Mike Wenger. The lecture material is split among the three of them. This confirms the course is partly Eric's, but it cannot say who spoke a given transcript line. So transcript bullets stay tagged Inferred and read as **course team positions**. Each one becomes Eric's own only when he claims it, in review or in the interview.\n13\t- **Reference material, not Eric's position**: `intencurriculumn.md` is Jason Avedesian's (Clemson) authored curriculum end to end, used by the Development Plan as a benchmark, not as something Eric wrote or necessarily agrees with. The two Unit Goal Programming Standards documents are explicitly staff templates filled in (thinly) by Geyer and Skodinski, not Eric. The AP Assessment Manual V12 and the TechnologyTemplateSOP (TMG) have uncertain/likely-legacy authorship (see Tensions below) and are marked accordingly.\n14\t\n15\tSections below use **Explicit** (the source states Eric holds this position, with confidence in authorship per the tiers above) and **Inferred** (implied by protocol design, shared course material, or transcript content with unconfirmed speaker attribution).\n16\t\n17\tTags added 2026-09-28 from the method interview:\n18\t\n19\t- **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Qn)**: Eric stated or claimed the position in the interview. His words are quoted as \"Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Qn):\". Q1 claimed exactly four course positions (Vermeil hierarchy, undulating periodization, Dynamic Correspondence, the football energy-system priority stack); other course material stays Inferred.\n20\t- **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Qn)**: Eric said the source or practice is not current. The old content is kept and marked, not deleted, so a synthesis note can see what changed.\n21\t- **Superseded by interview (Qn)**: a documented position, sometimes from Eric's own slides, that his interview answer replaces.\n22\t\n23\t---\n24\t\n25\t## Philosophy and decision principles\n26\t\n27\t- Applied Performance's stated mission is to \"drive elite performance ... by leveraging science and technology through education and relationships,\" enabling the high-performance team to answer questions, ask better questions, and discover new ones — not to hand down answers unilaterally. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]\n28\t- The department's four operating principles are Athlete Monitoring and Analysis, Athlete Evaluation and Return-to-Performance, Performance Team Collaboration, and Research and Development. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]\n29\t- Institutional framing: \"Prepare Champions for Life\" across four pillars (academic, athletic, social, spiritual), operationalized through six \"One Standard, One Accord\" principles. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md pp.3-4]\n30\t- Data collection is judged on cost versus insight: \"Data collection has a cost in time, athlete compliance, and staff attention. When the insight stops justifying that cost, it's time to stop collecting.\" **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n31\t- A test can be scientifically valid and still be the wrong choice operationally: \"The lesson wasn't about the test. It was about the infrastructure required to make a test useful ... even a valid test stops being valuable\" without staff ownership and consistent collection. **Explicit** [2026-02-20-sportsmith-6otb-nordbord-er.md]\n32\t- General rule for any test or technology, stated as a standing rule rather than a fresh call each time. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q3): \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)**\n33\t- The coach's eye and objective data work in sequence: the eye flags, data investigates. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q6): \"There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to look and gather data to investigate further.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q6)**\n34\t- \"Inconsistent data from a good test is still bad data\" — a test's validity doesn't rescue it if it can't be collected consistently at scale. **Explicit** [2026-02-20-sportsmith-6otb-nordbord-er.md]\n35\t- Reject ownership language (\"my team,\" \"my athletes\"): the athlete's development is shared across every department that touches them, not owned by any one coach. **Inferred (transcript, speaker unconfirmed)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.5, \"we got to get away from like the me and my\"]\n36\t- Attribution in practice: outside frameworks are absorbed into the team's own way of working rather than credited to one person. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q27): \"We fold it into the mantra of 'this is how we do our business.'\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q27)** Inference: this is how Eric applies the \"we/our\" bullet above, so that bullet reads as practice, though the transcript line itself is still unclaimed.\n37\t- The \"five expectations\" culture rules (Golden Rule, Be Disciplined, Be Relentless, Communicate, No Excuses) were presented in the course as absolute [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.1-2]. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q26): \"These are more a relic of another coaching era that has held over. I would say none [no deliberate exception], but they are rarely mentioned around here any more.\" **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q26)**\n38\t- Injury risk can be reduced, not eliminated, through sound programming, progression, and collaboration with Athletic Medicine — an explicit refusal to promise injury prevention. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.4]\n39\t- Be skeptical of social-media-driven training trends: a viral clip is \"just a brief snapshot,\" stripped of the context that would tell you whether it's sound. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.3]\n40\t- Recovery has to be earned by training hard first; a culture of fear grew out of early GPS/load-monitoring work that pushed teams toward under-training in the name of \"recovery.\" **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.9-10]\n41\t- \"Knowledge from experience\" as a stated operating value: safety first, keep things simple, teach basic movement skills and basic lifts before anything advanced, treat strength as the base, and require bodyweight competency before loaded work. **Inferred (transcript + Week 1 slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.4-5; week1-lecture.md p.12]\n42\t\n43\t## Department model and roles\n44\t\n45\t- Applied Performance operates inside the Baylor Athletics Health & Wellness department, alongside (not above or below) Athletics Performance, Athletic Medicine, Performance Nutrition, and Mental Health Services. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md pp.5-6]\n46\t- Asked whether Applied Performance is a peer discipline that enables Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine and Nutrition, or in-house programming support for Athletic Performance, Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q5): \"Both.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q5)** Resolves Tension 2.\n47\t- Coach-facing reports are built by whoever supports the team, which for most teams is strength staff. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q28): \"Strength staff will build it most likely; we don't have sport science support for most teams. When applicable, sport science staff would provide it.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q28)**\n48\t- The athlete is described as the hub of a wheel, with every support function (academics, Athletic Medicine, nutrition, mental health, Applied Performance) as a spoke — no single discipline owns the athlete's development. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.5]\n49\t- An Athletic Performance coach is framed in explicit contrast to a narrower \"Strength Coach\" identity: \"we/our\" and holistic mind-body-spirit development versus \"me/my\" and just lifting/running. **Inferred (transcript + Week 1 slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.5; week1-lecture.md p.13]\n50\t- The current Baylor Applied Performance Operations Guide is judged, by Eric's own gap analysis, as missing: an intern/GA curriculum, sport-specific data dictionaries, daily operational checklists, Baylor-specific (not generic-template) technology workflows, and a guiding-principles/policy framework for staff and interns. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md Section 1, 3.2]\n51\t- A three-phase roadmap (Foundation, weeks 1-4; Operational Depth, weeks 5-10; Education and Culture, weeks 11-16+) is the stated plan to close those gaps, ending with an annual Playbook review cycle each May. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md Section 4]\n52\t- Some assessments coaches already run inside practice should not be duplicated; use that existing data to triangulate rather than re-testing, and set objective criteria for coach-mandated tests when none exist. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8)**; was Inferred (author uncertain) [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md, \"Team Cultures and Assessments\"]\n53\t\n54\t## Testing and assessment\n55\t\n56\t- Assessment is continuous, not a single test day: start with movement quality, injury history, and training background, then observe daily via warm-ups and practice. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.4-5]\n57\t- Testing protocol has to be exactly repeatable over time (\"how you do it in January needs to be the same as how you do it in April\") or the resulting data cannot be trusted for comparison. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.6]\n58\t- Favor a small number of valid, reliable tests mapped to clear KPI categories over an exhaustive battery — illustrated with a simple baseball profile (bodyweight, 60-yard dash, vertical jump, broad jump, back squat, push-up max). **Inferred (transcript + Week 1 slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.6; week1-lecture.md p.16]\n59\t- Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.7] (See the cadence bullet below: there is no department-wide cadence.)\n60\t- There is no common testing cadence across sports; each sport keeps its own schedule. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q9): \"We currently don't have a consistent cadence across all sports. Individual sports have their own unique schedules.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q9)**\n61\t- No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q10): \"things are ballparked based on other information we're grabbing on the athlete: watching them move in real time, then using historical context to make future programming decisions around projected 1RMs.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**\n62\t- Change of direction is not tested in most sports. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q13): \"If the data is captured, it's on an individual sport basis and that information lives with that coach.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q13)** The RTP templates still gate on COD and FMS, run by Athletic Medicine or the sport coach (Tension 18, resolved 2026-10-02).\n63\t- The Assessment Manual V12 is not current. A new, more robust manual is in development and will incorporate the NCAA technology and sport-science recommendations. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q8)** Every bullet below sourced to `ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md` is therefore Legacy.\n64\t- Preparedness (the athlete's underlying physical capability, built over a long period) is explicitly distinguished from readiness (day-to-day fluctuation) — both are tracked, but they answer different questions. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.7]\n65\t- Reassessment timing is governed by three factors: proximity to competition, the expected magnitude of change for that training phase and athlete level, and current recovery status: \"do not reassess an athlete if there is known fatigue.\" **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8)**; was Inferred (author uncertain) [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md, \"Reassessment\"]\n66\t- 1RM testing is reserved for intermediate/advanced athletes with real exposure to near-maximal loads; novices and most women's-sport testing default to repetition-max protocols, which are described as better tolerated neurologically, psychologically, and physically for smaller-framed lifters. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**: no sport tests true 1RM now; was Inferred (transcript + Week 1 slides) [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.18; week1-lecture.md p.39]\n67\t- Velocity-based 1RM estimation (linear-displacement transducer, ~10% load increments to 85-90% or a target mean velocity) is preferred over live 1RM attempts to avoid the fatigue and injury risk of true maxing, with published target mean velocities at 1RM (squat 0.3 m/s, bench 0.2 m/s, power hang snatch 1.0 m/s, power hang clean 0.8 m/s). **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8, Q10)**; was Inferred (author uncertain) [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md] Inference: Q10 describes projection from observation and history and does not mention a velocity protocol.\n68\t- The Baylor Sport Assessment Table (V4) assigns a specific battery per sport (movement screen, anthropometrics, CMJ, broad jump, weighted throw, upper/lower strength estimation, strength endurance, aerobic fitness, repeat sprint ability, linear sprint, change of direction) rather than testing every sport the same way. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q9)**; was Inferred. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q9): \"That assessment schedule was never used.\" Inference: the table was never practice, not just outdated. [update-baylor-sport-assessment-table-v4.md]\n69\t- The current data dictionary is organized by vendor (Catapult, Perch, ForceDecks, Polar), not by sport, which Eric's own gap analysis flags as a real usability problem for staff and a priority to fix with sport-specific, threshold-contextualized dictionaries. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md 3.2.3, Phase 2]\n70\t\n71\t## Force plate and neuromuscular monitoring\n72\t\n73\t- Force plates (CMJ testing) were already the department's longitudinal performance-tracking tool well before the 2022-23 wellness-survey experiment, and remained in place after that experiment ended. **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n74\t- Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective wellness questionnaires, because it is \"objective, consistent, and actionable in a way the wellness surveys never quite were.\" **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n75\t- The mRSI decision read happens on Wednesdays, against individualized thresholds, not a single cut-off or position-group thresholds. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q2): \"Wednesdays, the thresholds should be individualized.\" Individualized against: Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q2a): \"Percent change from baseline or a rolling average. We've used standard deviations and SWC to diagnose signal versus noise.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q2, Q2a)** Inference: \"should be\" may describe the intended rule rather than a fully running one; no numeric threshold was given. See Tension 12 (daily testing versus a Wednesday read), resolved 2026-10-02: daily jumps build the baseline and SWC, Wednesday is the decision read.\n76\t- The dynamic strength index (DSI: CMJ against isometric mid-thigh pull peak force) chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q21): \"we incorporate the countermovement jump and the isometric mid-thigh pull to get a dynamic strength index, to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)** This is not the SJ-CMJ gap the question asked about. Evidence check done 2026-09-29 (`docs/synthesis/_work/dsi-evidence-check.md`): cite it as Eric's practice, tagged [contested; the ratio tracks IMTP strength; one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]. See Tension 17.\n77\t- The standard CMJ family run on ForceDecks: hands-free squat, squat jump (quarter-squat start, 3s hold), countermovement jump (x3), hop test / repeated CMJ (10 reps total, n=5 pairs), and single-leg CMJ (x3 per leg) — each with a scripted weigh-in, stabilization, and rep protocol. **Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)** [revised-force-plate-testing-v3.md]\n78\t- The isometric seated calf raise is run on the ForceDecks \"Mid-Thigh Pull\" setting: 90-degree ankle/knee position, belt pre-tensioned to a fingertip's gap, 3 maximal 6-second contractions with 15 seconds' rest between reps. **Inferred (protocol)** [protocol-iso-seated-calf-raise.md; revised-force-plate-testing-v3.md]\n79\t- Metric selection for force-plate reporting follows a disciplined framework: pick 1-2 output metrics (the obvious performance result, e.g. jump height), 1-3 driver metrics (what produces the output, e.g. braking/propulsive force), 1-3 strategy metrics (how the athlete achieved it, e.g. phase durations), optional symmetry metrics (L/R differences), and add \"fluffy\" metrics (peak power, average power, RFD) only with a specific, defensible rationale. **Adopted practice, framework not Eric's own** [hd-odsf-metrics.md — origin: Hawkin Dynamics]\n80\t- NordBord hamstring testing was retired for roster-wide football monitoring (after starting January 2023) because a 25-30 minute per-group session could not be sustained across 120+ athletes without large, uncontrolled gaps in the data, and staff turnover ended the coordination needed to run it at scale. The decision rule stated: keep a demanding test only where you have \"smaller numbers, clear clinical purpose, dedicated oversight\" (now used for targeted return-to-play groups paired with joint-specific isometric testing); drop it where you can't guarantee consistent, comparable collection. **Explicit** [2026-02-20-sportsmith-6otb-nordbord-er.md]\n81\t\n82\t## Load monitoring and GPS\n83\t\n84\t- The core GPS technology stack is Catapult (device/OpenField software) with Kitman Labs as the central athlete-management system for cross-technology storage, scaling, and visualization. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.7]\n85\t- Core GPS metrics tracked: maximum velocity, total distance, player load, field time, heart-rate bands, velocity/acceleration/deceleration effort bands, and Inertial Movement Analysis (IMA) for indoor sessions without GPS coverage. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md pp.11-13, Data Dictionary]\n86\t- A documented step-by-step SOP exists for the full GPS session lifecycle: charging, alarm-setting, activity creation, live period management, post-session sync, data cleaning (spike detection), tagging, and PDF export/reporting. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md pp.9-11]\n87\t- No sport-specific or position-specific GPS/load thresholds currently exist in the documented data dictionary; it is generic and vendor-organized, and building contextualized per-sport thresholds is named as unfinished, prioritized work. **Explicit (gap, stated as a gap)** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md 3.2.3, Phase 2] The formal gap is confirmed. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q7): \"They do not exist formally.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q7)** The question also asked about informal thresholds; that half was not answered.\n88\t- Heavy GPS/load-monitoring data was described as having bred a \"culture of fear\" in the field broadly (do less, to protect recovery), which the course material argues actually leaves athletes under-prepared for competition. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.9-10]\n89\t- Daily subjective wellness surveys, rolled out across multiple sports via the AMS app starting summer 2022, were abandoned after roughly one academic year because athletes learned to game the consistent, low-effort answers the survey rewarded; the data looked clean but carried no real signal. Wellness surveys are still used, deliberately, in return-to-play scenarios and with athletes managing chronic health conditions, where subjective context still adds value objective data can't supply. **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n90\t\n91\t## Speed and sprint\n92\t\n93\t- Linear sprint testing uses a 2-point start for team-sport athletes (for reliability), athlete self-selected start timing, at least two maximal-effort reps over 30-40 meters. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8)**; was Inferred (author uncertain) [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md]\n94\t- Split times are interpreted by physical quality, not just total time: 0-10m is read as an acceleration score (reflecting maximal strength/power/slow stretch-shortening-cycle development), 30-40m as a speed score (reflecting sprint-specific/fast stretch-shortening-cycle development), and 40m peak velocity is derived from the 30-40m split divided by 10. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8)**; was Inferred (author uncertain) [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md]\n95\t- SmartSpeed timing gates are the standard tool for sprint, agility, and reaction-time testing across the department. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.7]\n96\t- Speed is taught as the quality with the shortest ground-contact-time demand (~0.08s male, ~0.10s female at max velocity) and is described as harder to train and more genetically constrained than strength or power, but as the biggest separator between good and great athletes at the elite level. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.11]\n97\t- Issurin's residual training effects (speed fades fastest, 5 ± 3 days; Block Periodization PDF p. 35, as cited in `docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md`) drive speed programming. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q16): \"I believe Issurin. It drives our speed programming in the off-season and what drives sprinting recommendations in season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q16)** See the residual-effects bullets under Conditioning and Periodization, and Tension 11.\n98\t\n99\t## Strength and power\n100\t\n101\t- The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \"compass.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + shared course material) [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.9-10; week1-lecture.md pp.23,26]\n102\t- Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk), replacing the loose and often-misapplied idea of \"sport specific.\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.13-16; week1-lecture.md pp.32-36]\n103\t- The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + slides) [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.14; week1-lecture.md p.34]\n104\t- Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints); single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.13; week1-lecture.md p.31]\n105\t- Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work. In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + shared slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.20-21; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.21] Not claimed in the interview. The in-season dose itself is unknown: Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q18): \"I don't have the specific sets and reps.\"\n106\t- Undulating/non-linear periodization (Parker/Miller/Panariello), waving volume week to week (e.g., medium-high, medium, high, low over four weeks) is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression, specifically to avoid staleness while still building tolerance for higher loads. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript, speaker unconfirmed but first-person: \"For me personally, this is a more preferential model to use\") [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.24-26]\n107\t- Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block, used consistently across strength and power teaching. **Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.19; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.15] Inference: with no true 1RM testing (Q10), any %1RM prescription rests on projected maxes. Kept on that explicit basis (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02).\n108\t- Strength's residual training effect is treated as roughly 30 days; a minimal retention dose is still trained within that window, even during lower-strength-emphasis blocks. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.24-25] Inference: consistent with Eric siding with Issurin (Q16), whose strength residual is 30 ± 5 days, though Q16 was about speed and he did not claim this bullet.\n109\t- Tracy Fober's maxim (\"use a barbell like a chisel, not a sledgehammer\") is adopted as a guiding standard: prioritize movement quality and confidence-through-competence over chasing hypertrophy or 1RM numbers, especially early in a training cycle. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.21; week1-lecture.md p.46]\n110\t\n111\t## Conditioning and energy systems\n112\t\n113\t- Conditioning is defined two ways: (1) how well an athlete can meet the energy requirements of their specific sport/activity, and (2) how well they sustain strength, speed, and power output across a full competition. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md p.22]\n114\t- All three energy systems (alactic ~10-12s highest power, glycolytic/anaerobic-lactic ~60-90s medium capacity, aerobic hours-long lowest power/highest capacity) are always active simultaneously; programming targets the adaptation, not the drill label. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md p.19; class-4-2026.md p.15]\n115\t- A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once — priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands (e.g., for football: prepared for fall camp first, then the first game). **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.31]\n116\t- For football specifically, the stated priority stack is: Goal 1, alactic and aerobic capacity/power for fall-camp demands; Goal 2, the same two systems for game demands; glycolytic conditioning is treated as a \"worst case scenario\" to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md pp.34, 39]; claimed again in the interview, **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**\n117\t- Glycolytic conditioning is still not a primary target in season, but staff do program it in the off-season. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q19): \"It does hold up, but our staff does program it in the off-season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)**\n118\t- Anaerobic (lactate) threshold heart-rate zones are assigned by athlete body size rather than a single number: roughly 165 bpm for big/tall athletes, 170 for medium, 175 for small, with Zone 1-4 bands set as percentages of that individual threshold. **Superseded by interview (Q20)**; was Explicit [class-4-2026.md pp.28-29]\n119\t- The anaerobic-threshold heart rate in use is the age-based method (the slide deck's max HR = 220-age for men, 226-age for women, threshold at about 85% of that [class-4-2026.md p.27]). Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q20): \"Age-based.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q20)** Resolves Tension 6.\n120\t- The traits easiest to build (aerobic) are lost fastest under detraining; the traits hardest to build (alactic/speed) are lost slowest; this ordering is used to sequence what needs the most frequent re-exposure. **Superseded by interview (Q16)**; was Explicit [class-4-2026.md p.47] Eric now sides with Issurin (speed fades fastest). See Tension 11.\n121\t- Training intensities between roughly 75-90% of maximal effort are labeled (via Francis) \"no man's land\" — too fast to build alactic power cleanly, too slow to build real aerobic capacity — and are treated as a zone to generally avoid as a primary training target. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.48]\n122\t- \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\": lifting, agility work, and skill drills each load a specific energy system (power vs. capacity, alactic/glycolytic/aerobic) and should be classified and counted as conditioning, not treated as separate from it. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.31, tables pp.35-42]\n123\t- For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts), not distance capacity — a better-developed aerobic system means more available energy on play 10, 11, or 12 of a drive, not longer continuous running ability. **Inferred (transcript)** [2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md p.9]\n124\t- Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md p.51; 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.11-12]\n125\t- Joel Jamieson's *Ultimate MMA Conditioning* is recommended as the single best practical resource for energy-system development, on the basis that its methods transfer cleanly to non-combat sports despite the MMA framing. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md pp.49-50; 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.11-12]\n126\t\n127\t## Rehab and return to play\n128\t\n129\t- Applied Performance's stated role in return-to-performance is to supply information that supports Athletics Performance and Athletic Medicine's decisions on training and RTP periodization — a support/advisory role, not a stated final-decision role. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]\n130\t- What Applied Performance actually contributes to RTP is testing and progression, with shared loading decisions, and the involvement has no fixed end point. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q4): \"Testing and progression. I collaborate on loading and progression decisions when needed. It doesn't really end.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q4)** See Tension 14 on \"support\" versus \"doesn't really end\".\n131\t- RTP does not include a readiness assessment by Applied Performance; the written templates carry that decision. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q22): \"We don't assess readiness. The template answers that.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q22)** See Tension 13.\n132\t- KPI-threshold identification for diagnostic screening and performance testing is named as a departmental operating principle, but no specific RTP criteria, battery, or phased-return protocol appears anywhere in this corpus. **Explicit (principle) / gap (no criteria stated)** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6] Update: a written protocol exists outside the corpus. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q4, Q4a): \"There is a written protocol\", namely the Baylor UE Rehab Program Template and the Baylor LE Rehab Program Template (both .docx, currently in Eric's Downloads folder). **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q4a)** Not yet staged, so Flint does not know their content; staging a text copy is the next step.\n133\t- Daily wellness-style subjective monitoring, dropped for general roster use, is explicitly kept in return-to-play scenarios specifically because subjective context still fills a gap objective testing can't. **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n134\t- NordBord hamstring testing, dropped for roster-wide use, is explicitly retained for smaller, targeted return-to-play groups paired with joint-specific isometric testing (ForceFrame-style). **Explicit** [2026-02-20-sportsmith-6otb-nordbord-er.md]\n135\t\n136\t*This was the thinnest discipline in the corpus. After the interview (2026-09-28), the role is clear (testing and progression, collaborative loading, Q4) and a written protocol exists (the UE and LE templates, Q4a), but its criteria are still unread by Flint until the templates are staged.*\n137\t\n138\t## Injury risk and prevention\n139\t\n140\t- Injury risk is reduced, not eliminated, through sound progression of load, training means, and density, in collaboration with Athletic Medicine — an explicit refusal to claim injury prevention as achievable. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.4; week1-lecture.md p.9]\n141\t- Bodyweight-control competency is treated as a marker of injury risk in its own right: an athlete who can bench 400 lb but can't do 10 clean push-ups has \"something out of whack\" that needs fixing regardless of their strength numbers. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.5]\n142\t- Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design; abrupt, large changes are treated as a direct injury-risk driver, not just a training-quality issue. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.27]\n143\t- No formal injury-risk model (ACWR, tissue-capacity tracking, a biomechanical screening protocol with defined red flags) is attributed to Eric anywhere in this corpus; the detailed treatment of ACWR and injury-risk modeling that does exist (Clemson curriculum) is reference material only, not confirmed Baylor practice. Confirmed by the interview: there is no formal risk model.\n144\t- ACWR is looked at but not trusted for any decision. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q12): \"We dabble with ACWR but I don't personally trust it for anything. I think it's raw, has error, is misleading, and lacks context generally. We don't have any formal risk model that we follow. We've avoided them up to this point, but there's not a really good reason why.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q12)**\n145\t- Movement screens are collected and filed, not used to change programs. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q12): \"there isn't anything that changes decisions made to a program; it's more or less just collected and referred to for future needs.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q12)** Inference: this sits uneasily with his own Q3 rule; see Tension 15.\n146\t\n147\t*Thin discipline, now answered: the finding is that nothing formal exists (no risk model, ACWR distrusted, screens not acted on).*\n148\t\n149\t## Recovery and sleep\n150\t\n151\t- Recovery capacity must be \"earned\" by training hard enough first; recovery technology (Theragun, Normatec, cryo) is treated as useful but not a substitute for managing training stress and dose correctly. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.9]\n152\t- Recovery appears in the department's own subcategory list of Athletic Performance (alongside strength, conditioning, speed, agility, power, nutrition, applied performance, sports psychology, RTP), but no specific recovery protocol, sleep target, or monitoring tool is described anywhere in this corpus. **Explicit (named as a subcategory) / gap (no protocol stated)** [week1-lecture.md p.11]\n153\t- Recovery is managed through training-load design alone; nothing objective is tracked, sleep included. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q23): \"It's handled through training-load design alone. We're not tracking anything objective.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q23)**\n154\t\n155\t*Thin discipline, now answered: the finding is that nothing formal exists beyond load design.*\n156\t\n157\t## Nutrition and body composition\n158\t\n159\t- Performance Nutrition is explicitly a separate Health & Wellness unit from Applied Performance, owning individual nutrition consultations, supplement education, body composition and bone density assessment, and the training table/fueling stations. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.5]\n160\t- Body composition testing is capped at no more than 4 times per year. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8)**; was Inferred (author uncertain) [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md, \"Anthropometrics\"]\n161\t- Anthropometrics (height, weight, muscle-to-fat ratio) are described as strongly associated with success in most sports and worth tracking even though they are owned by Performance Nutrition rather than Applied Performance. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8)**; was Inferred (author uncertain) [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md, \"Anthropometrics\"]\n162\t- No nutrition decision rules, body-composition targets, or thresholds are attributed to Eric anywhere in this corpus; this appears to be a deliberate department-design choice (Performance Nutrition owns it) rather than an oversight.\n163\t- No force-plate or GPS data flows to Performance Nutrition; bodyweight is consulted only when there is a problem. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q24): \"It does not. Only if there is an issue will bodyweight be consulted. Nothing proactive.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q24)**\n164\t\n165\t*Thin discipline, now answered: the finding is that there is no proactive data link between Applied Performance and Performance Nutrition.*\n166\t\n167\t## Coaching, skill acquisition and constraints-led approach\n168\t\n169\t- A trained \"coach's eye,\" built through repetition and attention, is treated as at least as important as any technology for daily assessment: \"you don't need a fancy test or anything to do that. But you do have to have a good coach's eye.\" **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.8] Eric's own position (interview 2026-09-28, Q6) is narrower: the eye tells you where to look, and data then investigates. See the Philosophy bullet and Tension 3.\n170\t- Constraints-led and external-cue coaching are not part of Eric's practice. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q25): \"I don't apply it, as I don't fully understand it. I want to know more.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q25)** A learning request, a candidate for a primer.\n171\t- New movement patterns are expected to take roughly 3-4 weeks of practice to become efficient and low-energy-cost, which informs how much proficiency to expect when a new exercise or drill is introduced. **Inferred (transcript)** [2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.6-7]\n172\t- Movement efficiency (skill) is treated as directly reducing energy cost — elite athletes (cited examples: LeBron James, Deion Sanders) are described as outlasting and outperforming peers partly through tactical efficiency that lowers the physical output required to achieve the same result. **Inferred (transcript)** [2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.8-9]\n173\t- Basic movement skills and basic lifts are taught before anything advanced, on the observed premise that many incoming athletes have high sport-specific skill from early specialization but poor general movement competency. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.4-5]\n174\t\n175\t*Still thin. The interview added the coach's-eye rule (Q6) and confirmed that constraints-led coaching is not in use (Q25). The rest of this section is shared course material, unclaimed.*\n176\t\n177\t## Periodization and programming\n178\t\n179\t- Macro/meso/microcycle structure is used as the standard planning language, with an explicit reminder that a \"training week\" does not have to equal 7 calendar days — it can be extended to fit training themes around a real competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.17-18; week1-lecture.md p.39]\n180\t- The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power, each with defined intensity/volume bands (e.g., hypertrophy phase 50-75% intensity, 3-6 sets of 5-20 reps; strength phase 80-90% intensity, moderate volume), before a transition into competition. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.19-21; week1-lecture.md pp.43-48]\n181\t- In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.21]\n182\t- Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block — \"you can't ride two horses with one saddle.\" Compatible qualities are trained together; the rest are trained at a retention dose. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.28-29; week1-lecture.md p.70]\n183\t- An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract: lay out the competition calendar, key non-training dates (holidays, exam weeks), and phase-by-phase volume/intensity waves, then adapt the route as circumstances change while still reaching the same destination. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.22-23; 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.13-15]\n184\t- Training residual effects set the retraining cadence: aerobic capacity and max strength both need re-exposure roughly every 30 days; speed needs re-exposure roughly every 5 days or tissue readiness for max-velocity work degrades. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.27] Inference: these are Issurin's figures, and Eric sides with Issurin (interview 2026-09-28, Q16), so the speed half reads as his; the aerobic and strength halves were not asked directly.\n185\t- In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q14): \"in football you want to peak every Saturday, but definitely throughout the month of November as you get closer to the postseason ... That's when players need to be at or near their peak as they head into the postseason and playoffs.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)** He did not say whether the postseason gets a distinct taper.\n186\t- The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**\n187\t- Bye weeks: three practice days, extra rest and recovery, slightly higher practice volume than a game week, a speed and strength top-up, and harder work for players with few game snaps. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q17): \"guys that aren't getting a ton of play will probably practice harder that week than guys that have gotten a ton of snaps.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)**\n188\t- The head coach's system of play and roster identity (e.g., a \"home run hitters\" lineup vs. a \"steal bases\" lineup) is treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.16]\n189\t\n190\t## Data, technology and reporting\n191\t\n192\t- The full technology stack: Catapult (GPS), Kitman Labs (central AMS/data store), Perch (camera-based velocity-based training), Polar (heart rate), and the VALD suite — DynaMo, ForceDecks, ForceFrame, Hub, NordBord, SmartSpeed. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.7]\n193\t- Kitman Labs is positioned specifically as the cross-technology data store and analysis/visualization layer, distinct from each vendor's own reporting tool (e.g., VALD Hub, OpenField Cloud). **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.7]\n194\t- System of record: each source system holds its own data (force data in VALD, Catapult data in OpenField), Kitman AMS aggregates, and the source system wins when Kitman disagrees. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q11): \"They're separate entities; one doesn't overrule the other. If Kitman had wrong numbers from VALD or OpenField, we would go to those sources to correct it: the source systems overrule any errors Kitman made bringing data into the platform.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q11)**\n195\t- Documented GPS/VALD workflows exist for the full device lifecycle, but Eric's own gap analysis flags them as generic, template-driven, and lacking Baylor-specific detail (serial numbers, storage locations, troubleshooting) except for the Catapult workflow, which has genuine operational depth. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md 3.2.2]\n196\t- Credential/login management is currently placeholder-only in the Playbook; a secure credential-vault protocol is named as needed but not yet implemented. **Explicit (gap, stated as a to-do)** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.8; performance-playbook-development-plan.md Section 4]\n197\t- Standardized weekly/monthly coach-facing reporting templates do not yet exist; building them is named as unfinished Phase 2 work. **Explicit (gap, stated as a to-do)** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md Phase 2] Who builds reports today: strength staff for most teams (interview 2026-09-28, Q28; see Department model).\n198\t- Tensiomyography (TMG) is part of the toolkit, with a documented setup/operations SOP, but that SOP is headed with the names of a prior Director and Associate Director of Applied Performance (Althoff, Nelson), not Eric, and current active use is unclear from this corpus. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q29)**; was Inferred (legacy document, status unclear) [technologytemplatesop.md] Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q29): \"Only Chris Ruf with Track still uses it.\"\n199\t\n200\t## Education and staff development\n201\t\n202\t- HHPR 5355 (\"Power, Speed, Agility, & Periodization in Training\") is Eric's own graduate-level course, co-taught with Chris Ruf and Michael Wenger; grading is split across two exams (10% and 20%), a group training-plan project (20%), and lecture/lab participation (40%), with a practical (teach-back) component on exams. **Inferred (transcript, syllabus content)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.1]\n203\t- A standalone, 16-week Intern Sport Science Curriculum is proposed, deliberately separate from the Playbook so it can be distributed and revised on its own cycle, aligned to the NSCA Essentials of Sport Science (CPSS) textbook, and adapted from a Clemson model to Baylor's own technology stack (ForceDecks, Catapult, ForceFrame, NordBord, DynaMo, Perch, SmartSpeed, Polar) and to include football alongside Olympic-sport examples. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md Section 5] Status: a proposal only. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q30): \"It has not been built yet.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q30)**\n204\t- Curriculum design principle: interns need both the \"why\" (conceptual education) and the \"how\" (hands-on technical training) each week, and should leave the program with a professional portfolio of real work products (dashboards, reports, presentations, infographics), not just device-operation checklists. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md 5.1, 5.4]\n205\t- The proposed curriculum deliberately adds sessions on interdisciplinary collaboration with Athletic Medicine, Performance Nutrition, and Mental Health Services, reflecting Baylor's specific department structure (Applied Performance inside Health & Wellness) rather than copying Clemson's Olympic-sports-only framing. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md 5.3]\n206\t\n207\t---\n208\t\n209\t## No material found\n210\t\n211\tNone. Every one of the sixteen required sections had at least some material. Several sections are thin, though, and should be read as gaps rather than settled positions: **Rehab and return to play**, **Injury risk and prevention**, **Recovery and sleep**, **Nutrition and body composition**, and **Coaching, skill acquisition and constraints-led approach**. See `interview-prep.md`.\n212\t\n213\tUpdate after the interview (2026-09-28): four of the five thin sections now have an answer, and for three of them the answer is that nothing formal exists, which is itself the finding. Injury risk: no formal model, ACWR distrusted, screens not acted on (Q12). Recovery and sleep: load design only, nothing objective tracked (Q23). Nutrition: no proactive data flow (Q24). Rehab and RTP: role stated (Q4, Q22) and a written protocol exists in the UE and LE templates (Q4a), not yet staged. Coaching stays thin: constraints-led coaching is not used (Q25), and the rest is unclaimed course material.\n214\t\n215\t---\n216\t\n217\t## Tensions and contradictions\n218\t\n219\tStatus after the interview (2026-09-28): resolved 1 (partly), 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9; still open 7 (partly), 8, 10. New tensions from the interview are 11 to 16. Resolved by Eric 2026-10-02: 12, 16 and 18 (COD/FMS gates). Each entry below carries its status on its last line.\n220\t\n221\t1. **The task's own premise doesn't fully hold.** Both class transcripts (Class 1 and Class 3) are diarized entirely under a single Fireflies speaker label, \"Chris Ruf,\" for every line — including Class 3, which the Spring 2026 teaching schedule assigns solo to Rash (`spring-2026-teaching.md`: \"Feb. 5 | Conditioning - Alactic-Aerobic | Rash\"). Fireflies likely defaulted to the meeting organizer's name instead of doing real per-speaker diarization. This means roughly 30,000 words of the richest, most first-person material in this corpus cannot be confidently attributed to Rash specifically versus Ruf or Wenger. Every transcript-sourced bullet above is marked Inferred for this reason, even where the underlying statement is a confident, first-person claim.\n222\t   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): partly resolved.** Q1 claimed four positions (Vermeil hierarchy, undulating periodization, Dynamic Correspondence, the football energy-system stack), now Explicit. Every other transcript bullet stays Inferred.\n223\t\n224\t2. **Department reporting model is not internally consistent.** The Playbook (p.6) frames Applied Performance as a peer discipline inside Health & Wellness that \"enables\" Athletics Performance, Athletic Medicine, Nutrition, and Mental Health to answer their own questions. But Week 1 course material lists \"Applied Performance\" as one of many training sub-categories nested under Athletic Performance itself (p.11), and the same transcript narration describes Rash's role as \"helping program and plan the training\" — a support-staff framing, not a peer-department framing. Which model actually governs day-to-day authority is not reconciled anywhere in these documents.\n225\t   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q5): \"Both.\" Both framings are true at once.\n226\t\n227\t3. **Coach's-eye-first versus objective-first is argued both ways.** The course material repeatedly argues that \"you don't need a fancy test... you have to have a good coach's eye\" and criticizes the GPS/load-monitoring era for breeding a fear-driven under-training culture. Eric's own SportSmith writing moves the opposite direction on daily monitoring specifically: he replaced subjective wellness surveys with objective force-plate mRSI because subjective self-report couldn't be trusted. Both share a skepticism of trendy or convenient data, but they land on opposite defaults (trust the trained eye vs. require an objective number) for the daily-monitoring question specifically.\n228\t   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q6): the coach's eye flags where to look; data then investigates. The two are sequential, not rival defaults.\n229\t\n230\t4. **The Assessment Manual (V12) reads as an older document than the current technology stack.** It builds its 1RM-estimation protocol around GymAware, and its CMJ description references a \"contact mat\" for jump height. The current Playbook technology stack and the Development Plan both center velocity-based training on Perch, not GymAware, and the current Force Plate Testing V3 protocol runs all CMJ variants exclusively on ForceDecks force plates, with no contact mat mentioned. This suggests the Assessment Manual predates the current VALD/Perch-centered stack and may be a legacy document rather than the currently operative protocol — worth confirming directly with Eric before treating any of its specifics as current practice.\n231\t   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q8): V12 \"is not current\"; a new manual built on the NCAA technology and sport-science recommendations is in development. All V12 bullets are marked Legacy.\n232\t\n233\t5. **Testing cueing instructions conflict across two protocol documents for the same test.** The Assessment Manual's CMJ instructions say explicitly: \"Do not coach the strategy of jumping.\" The current Force Plate Testing V3 protocol gives testers specific verbal cues to give athletes during CMJ, squat-jump, and hop-test administration (\"keep chest up,\" \"land softly,\" \"land stiff-legged on only the toes\"). These may be reconcilable (safety/technique cues vs. jump-strategy cues are different things), but the two documents read differently on how much a tester should say out loud.\n234\t   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved by Q8.** Inference: with V12 not current, the Force Plate Testing V3 cues govern. Eric did not address the cueing question itself.\n235\t\n236\t6. **Two different anaerobic-threshold heart-rate methods appear back to back in the same slide deck without being reconciled.** One slide gives an age-based formula (max HR = 220-age for men, 226-age for women, threshold at ~85% of that), and the very next slides give a body-size-based table (big/medium/small athlete, ~165/170/175 bpm threshold) as the operational standard used elsewhere throughout the conditioning material. Which one is actually used day to day is not stated. [class-4-2026.md pp.27-29]\n237\t   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q20): \"Age-based.\" The body-size table is marked Superseded. Note that the body-size table was from Eric's own assigned session, so his slides and his practice differ.\n238\t\n239\t7. **The Development Plan's own critique cuts against the corpus it's part of.** It states the current Baylor Playbook \"has no educational component whatsoever\" and reads \"like a vendor product reference manual,\" even though Eric personally co-teaches a full graduate-level course covering much of the same material the Playbook is missing. The department's teaching capacity clearly exists; it just hasn't been captured back into the Playbook or a formal intern curriculum yet.\n240\t   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): still open, confirmed.** Eric (Q30): the intern curriculum \"has not been built yet.\"\n241\t\n242\t8. **The Playbook's NordBord documentation has not caught up with practice.** The Playbook's Technology Overview and Workflows still document a full, unqualified, roster-style NordBord data-collection SOP. The SportSmith piece describes explicitly retiring roster-wide NordBord testing for football starting after January 2023, keeping it only for small, targeted return-to-play groups. The written SOP has not been updated to reflect that decision.\n243\t   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): open.** Not asked. Inference: the Q3 rule (\"if it doesn't answer a question anyone has...\") would retire the roster-wide SOP.\n244\t\n245\t9. **A personally-stated periodization preference is stated inside an unreliable-speaker transcript.** \"For me personally, this is a more preferential model to use\" (about undulating over linear periodization) is a strong, direct, first-person preference claim — but it comes from the same unreliably-diarized Class 1 transcript described in Tension 1, so it cannot be confirmed as Eric's own stated preference without his direct confirmation.\n246\t   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric claimed undulating periodization (Q1).\n247\t\n248\t10. **Older vs. current unit-goal templates show almost no adopted content.** The Geyer and Skodinski Unit Goal Programming Standards documents are staff-authored templates for individual sport programming philosophy; Geyer's is mostly filled in (periodization model, weekly split, KPI tests for softball), Skodinski's is nearly blank (just the section headers). Neither is attributed to Eric, and there's no equivalent document in this corpus showing Eric's own answers to the same template questions — a real gap if Flint wants Eric's own unit-goal-style programming philosophy in his own words.\n249\t   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): open.** Not asked.\n250\t\n251\tNew tensions raised by the interview (2026-09-28):\n252\t\n253\t11. **Eric's own slide against his interview on residual effects.** The class-4 slide (p. 47), from a session Eric taught, says aerobic fitness is lost fastest and speed slowest. In the interview he sides with Issurin, speed fades fastest (Q16), and says Issurin drives speed programming. The slide bullet is marked Superseded. Inference: the slide still circulates in HHPR 5355 and should be reconciled before next spring. The peaking synthesis keeps the counter-evidence both ways.\n254\t12. **Daily mRSI testing against a Wednesday read.** The SportSmith piece describes mRSI from daily CMJ testing as the daily readiness marker [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]. Q2 names Wednesday as the day. Inference: daily collection with one weekly decision day would reconcile them, but Eric did not say which; worth one follow-up.\n255\t    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** Daily jumps are collected to build the rolling baseline and SWC; Wednesday is the single decision read that changes the week.\n256\t13. **\"We don't assess readiness\" against mRSI as the readiness marker.** Q22 says Applied Performance does not assess readiness in RTP; the SportSmith piece and Q2 describe mRSI readiness reads. Inference: Q22 is scoped to RTP, so these probably do not conflict, but the RTP templates should be checked.\n257\t14. **\"Support\" role against \"it doesn't really end\".** The Playbook frames RTP input as support to Athletic Medicine and Athletic Performance decisions [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]. Q4 describes ongoing testing, progression and shared loading decisions with no end point. Inference: compatible (support can be continuous), but the Playbook wording understates the role.\n258\t15. **The Q3 rule against current practice.** Eric's rule is that a test with no question or need has no place (Q3). Yet ACWR is still looked at (\"We dabble with ACWR\") though he does not \"trust it for anything\", and movement screens are \"just collected\" without changing decisions (Q12). Inference: by his own rule both are candidates to retire or to be given a question.\n259\t16. **No true 1RM against %1RM prescription.** No sport tests true 1RM (Q10), while Prilepin's chart and the phase bands are written in %1RM [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.19; week1-lecture.md pp.43-48]. Inference: loads are percentages of projected maxes, so their error carries into every prescription. Not a contradiction in practice, but worth stating.\n260\t    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** Keep %1RM and Prilepin prescription, stated explicitly as built on projected maxes (observation plus history, Q10), not tested 1RMs.\n261\t17. **DSI as a plyo-vs-strength switch against the evidence.** Eric uses DSI to choose plyometric versus max-strength emphasis (Q21). The evidence check (`docs/synthesis/_work/dsi-evidence-check.md`) finds that IMTP PF explains most of DSI's variance, that the 0.60/0.80 cutoffs were never validated, and that the one controlled trial found a benefit only in its strength arm. Inference: keep the test, but decide on CMJ PF and IMTP PF alongside the ratio, and set cutoffs from the roster.\n262\t18. **COD and FMS gates in the RTP templates against Q12/Q13.** The LE template gates Evaluation on the 10-5 COD test and on FMS > 14 (`docs/interview/2026-09-28-method-interview.md`), while Q13 says COD is not tested in most sports and Q12 says screens do not change decisions.\n263\t    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** The gates stay in the RTP templates. The baseline names who runs them (Athletic Medicine or the sport coach), not Applied Performance, consistent with \"the template answers readiness\" (Q22).\n264\t\n265\t---\n266\t\n267\t## Named influences\n268\t\n269\t| Influence | What's credited to them | Where | Provenance |\n270\t|---|---|---|---|\n271\t| Al Vermeil (transcribed once as \"Albert Meal\") | Hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity → strength → power → speed → sport skill); described as the department's programming \"compass\" | class1-transcript.md pp.9-10, 18-19 | Claimed by Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q1) |\n272\t| Dan Pfaff (transcribed as \"Coach Path/Paf/Pabst\") | The \"coach's eye\" ideal — biomechanical assessment purely by trained observation, illustrated with an anecdote about diagnosing a runner's spinal alignment from 50 meters away | class1-transcript.md pp.6-7 | Course material, speaker unconfirmed |\n273\t| Yuri Verkhoshansky | Principle of Dynamic Correspondence, for judging real exercise transfer | class1-transcript.md p.14; week1-lecture.md p.34 | Claimed by Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q1) |\n274\t| Anatoly Bondarchuk (transcribed as \"Bondurciu\") | General prep / specific prep / specific development / competitive exercise taxonomy | class1-transcript.md p.16; week1-lecture.md p.36 | Course material |\n275\t| Johnny Parker, Al Miller, Rob Panariello (transcribed as \"Panarillo\") | Undulating/non-linear periodization model, described as personally preferred | class1-transcript.md pp.25-26; week1-lecture.md pp.59-63; lesson8summary.md #19 | Claimed by Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q1) |\n276\t| Vladimir Issurin (*Block Periodization*) | Residual training effects (speed fades fastest); drives off-season speed programming and in-season sprint recommendations | interview 2026-09-28, Q16; class1-transcript.md p.27 and week1-lecture.md p.66 reproduce his table | Claimed by Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q16) |\n277\t| Louis Simmons / Westside Barbell (via Zatsiorsky and Siff) | Conjugate/concurrent method: max effort, dynamic effort, repetitive effort | class1-transcript.md pp.26-27; week1-lecture.md pp.63-66 | Course material |\n278\t| A.S. Prilepin (transcribed once as \"the Philippines chart\") | Rep/set/intensity guide, used as the default across strength and power teaching | class1-transcript.md p.19; week1-lecture.md p.47; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.15 | Shared team material |\n279\t| Tracy Fober | \"Use a barbell like a chisel, not a sledgehammer\" — quality-first coaching maxim, cited from a 2019 Baylor AP clinic and her Instagram | class1-transcript.md p.21; week1-lecture.md p.46 | Course material |\n280\t| Stu McMillan | Four goals of strength training (produce force, absorb force, decrease injury chance, teach positional context) | lesson8summary.md #3; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.5 | Wenger's session (Mar. 19) |\n281\t| Fred Hatfield; JL Holdsworth | Strength/alignment aphorisms (\"can't shoot a cannon out of a canoe\"; \"monster tires on a Prius with bad alignment\") | hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.4; lesson8summary.md #2 | Wenger's session |\n282\t| Joel Jamieson (*Ultimate MMA Conditioning*) | Recommended as the best practical resource on energy-system development methods | 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.11-12; week3-lecture.md pp.49-50 | Eric's assigned session (transcript speaker unconfirmed; slides confirmed) |\n283\t| Jason Avedesian / Clemson Olympic Sports Science | Entire intern curriculum (force plates, GPS, force-velocity profiling, data hygiene, visualization, soft skills, research methods, injury risk, S&C) | intencurriculumn.md, all pages | Reference source, not Eric's voice — used by the Development Plan as a benchmark |\n284\t| Hawkin Dynamics | Output/Driver/Strategy/Fluffy (ODSF) metric-selection framework for force-plate reporting | hd-odsf-metrics.md | Vendor framework, adopted as practice |\n285\t| NSCA (*Essentials of Sport Science* / CPSS) | Textbook basis for the Clemson curriculum and for Baylor's own proposed intern curriculum | performance-playbook-development-plan.md Section 5 | Cited by Eric's own Development Plan |\n286\t| Zatsiorsky; Haff & Triplett; Parker/Miller/Panariello (*The System*) | Recommended HHPR 5355 course textbooks | week1-lecture.md p.4 | Course syllabus |\n287\t"}]},"uuid":"892ed5b7-dfb4-4b86-ba9d-b7bc669e5855","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:13.868Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/method-baseline.md","content":"# Eric's method baseline (draft for Eric's review)\n\nPurpose: this is the step-1 baseline for Flint. It pulls every position, practice, and decision rule out of Eric's own 23-document corpus (Baylor Applied Performance playbook, protocols, assessment manual, professional writing, and HHPR 5355 graduate-course material) so that every future synthesis note comparing an outside source (Landon Evans, TB's Training Info, Old Bull, Harries RTP, SCN/Moyer, etc.) has something of Eric's own to compare against. Date: 2026-09-26. Revised 2026-09-28 with Eric's method interview (`docs/interview/2026-09-28-method-interview.md`, 30 of 30 answered).\n\n## Provenance note (read this before the bullets)\n\nNot all 23 documents are Eric's own words, and the two richest sources (the class transcripts) have a diarization problem. Read this once, then use these tags:\n\n- **Eric-authored, high confidence**: the two SportSmith articles, the Performance Playbook Development Plan, the Force Plate Testing V3 and Iso Seated Calf protocols, the Applied Performance Playbook (departmental document under his unit), and the HD ODSF metrics note (adopted practice, though the framework itself is Hawkin Dynamics' own).\n- **Course slide decks (Week 1, Week 3, Class 4)**: co-branded to Ruf/Rash/Wenger on every title slide. Per `spring-2026-teaching.md`, Eric was the assigned instructor for the Feb. 5 and Feb. 12 sessions (Week 3 Lecture and Class 4 2026 respectively), so bullets from those two decks are marked **Explicit**. Week 1 (Jan. 22, \"All\") and Class 8/Lesson 8 (Mar. 19, Wenger's session) are marked **Inferred** — shared team material, not confirmed as Eric's individual position.\n- **Both class transcripts (Class 1, Class 3) are diarized entirely under one Fireflies speaker label, \"Chris Ruf,\" for every line, across 100+ minutes combined.** This is true even for Class 3 (Feb. 5), which the syllabus assigns solo to Rash. Fireflies likely defaulted to the meeting organizer's name rather than doing real per-speaker diarization. That means specific sentences in these transcripts cannot be confidently attributed to Rash vs. Ruf vs. Wenger. Bullets drawn from the transcripts are marked **Inferred**, with a citation note, even where the statement itself is a direct, confident, first-person claim in the text. Flint should treat these as \"the HHPR 5355 teaching team's shared position, individual speaker unconfirmed\" until Eric ratifies them.\n  - **Confirmed by Eric, 2026-09-26:** HHPR 5355 is taught each spring by Chris Ruf, Eric and Mike Wenger. The lecture material is split among the three of them. This confirms the course is partly Eric's, but it cannot say who spoke a given transcript line. So transcript bullets stay tagged Inferred and read as **course team positions**. Each one becomes Eric's own only when he claims it, in review or in the interview.\n- **Reference material, not Eric's position**: `intencurriculumn.md` is Jason Avedesian's (Clemson) authored curriculum end to end, used by the Development Plan as a benchmark, not as something Eric wrote or necessarily agrees with. The two Unit Goal Programming Standards documents are explicitly staff templates filled in (thinly) by Geyer and Skodinski, not Eric. The AP Assessment Manual V12 and the TechnologyTemplateSOP (TMG) have uncertain/likely-legacy authorship (see Tensions below) and are marked accordingly.\n\nSections below use **Explicit** (the source states Eric holds this position, with confidence in authorship per the tiers above) and **Inferred** (implied by protocol design, shared course material, or transcript content with unconfirmed speaker attribution).\n\nTags added 2026-09-28 from the method interview:\n\n- **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Qn)**: Eric stated or claimed the position in the interview. His words are quoted as \"Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Qn):\". Q1 claimed exactly four course positions (Vermeil hierarchy, undulating periodization, Dynamic Correspondence, the football energy-system priority stack); other course material stays Inferred.\n- **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Qn)**: Eric said the source or practice is not current. The old content is kept and marked, not deleted, so a synthesis note can see what changed.\n- **Superseded by interview (Qn)**: a documented position, sometimes from Eric's own slides, that his interview answer replaces.\n\n---\n\n## Philosophy and decision principles\n\n- Applied Performance's stated mission is to \"drive elite performance ... by leveraging science and technology through education and relationships,\" enabling the high-performance team to answer questions, ask better questions, and discover new ones — not to hand down answers unilaterally. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]\n- The department's four operating principles are Athlete Monitoring and Analysis, Athlete Evaluation and Return-to-Performance, Performance Team Collaboration, and Research and Development. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]\n- Institutional framing: \"Prepare Champions for Life\" across four pillars (academic, athletic, social, spiritual), operationalized through six \"One Standard, One Accord\" principles. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md pp.3-4]\n- Data collection is judged on cost versus insight: \"Data collection has a cost in time, athlete compliance, and staff attention. When the insight stops justifying that cost, it's time to stop collecting.\" **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n- A test can be scientifically valid and still be the wrong choice operationally: \"The lesson wasn't about the test. It was about the infrastructure required to make a test useful ... even a valid test stops being valuable\" without staff ownership and consistent collection. **Explicit** [2026-02-20-sportsmith-6otb-nordbord-er.md]\n- General rule for any test or technology, stated as a standing rule rather than a fresh call each time. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q3): \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)**\n- The coach's eye and objective data work in sequence: the eye flags, data investigates. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q6): \"There is a place for the coach's eye: when something looks off, it can direct you where to look and gather data to investigate further.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q6)**\n- \"Inconsistent data from a good test is still bad data\" — a test's validity doesn't rescue it if it can't be collected consistently at scale. **Explicit** [2026-02-20-sportsmith-6otb-nordbord-er.md]\n- Reject ownership language (\"my team,\" \"my athletes\"): the athlete's development is shared across every department that touches them, not owned by any one coach. **Inferred (transcript, speaker unconfirmed)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.5, \"we got to get away from like the me and my\"]\n- Attribution in practice: outside frameworks are absorbed into the team's own way of working rather than credited to one person. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q27): \"We fold it into the mantra of 'this is how we do our business.'\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q27)** Inference: this is how Eric applies the \"we/our\" bullet above, so that bullet reads as practice, though the transcript line itself is still unclaimed.\n- The \"five expectations\" culture rules (Golden Rule, Be Disciplined, Be Relentless, Communicate, No Excuses) were presented in the course as absolute [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.1-2]. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q26): \"These are more a relic of another coaching era that has held over. I would say none [no deliberate exception], but they are rarely mentioned around here any more.\" **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q26)**\n- Injury risk can be reduced, not eliminated, through sound programming, progression, and collaboration with Athletic Medicine — an explicit refusal to promise injury prevention. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.4]\n- Be skeptical of social-media-driven training trends: a viral clip is \"just a brief snapshot,\" stripped of the context that would tell you whether it's sound. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.3]\n- Recovery has to be earned by training hard first; a culture of fear grew out of early GPS/load-monitoring work that pushed teams toward under-training in the name of \"recovery.\" **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.9-10]\n- \"Knowledge from experience\" as a stated operating value: safety first, keep things simple, teach basic movement skills and basic lifts before anything advanced, treat strength as the base, and require bodyweight competency before loaded work. **Inferred (transcript + Week 1 slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.4-5; week1-lecture.md p.12]\n\n## Department model and roles\n\n- Applied Performance operates inside the Baylor Athletics Health & Wellness department, alongside (not above or below) Athletics Performance, Athletic Medicine, Performance Nutrition, and Mental Health Services. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md pp.5-6]\n- Asked whether Applied Performance is a peer discipline that enables Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine and Nutrition, or in-house programming support for Athletic Performance, Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q5): \"Both.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q5)** Resolves Tension 2.\n- Coach-facing reports are built by whoever supports the team, which for most teams is strength staff. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q28): \"Strength staff will build it most likely; we don't have sport science support for most teams. When applicable, sport science staff would provide it.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q28)**\n- The athlete is described as the hub of a wheel, with every support function (academics, Athletic Medicine, nutrition, mental health, Applied Performance) as a spoke — no single discipline owns the athlete's development. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.5]\n- An Athletic Performance coach is framed in explicit contrast to a narrower \"Strength Coach\" identity: \"we/our\" and holistic mind-body-spirit development versus \"me/my\" and just lifting/running. **Inferred (transcript + Week 1 slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.5; week1-lecture.md p.13]\n- The current Baylor Applied Performance Operations Guide is judged, by Eric's own gap analysis, as missing: an intern/GA curriculum, sport-specific data dictionaries, daily operational checklists, Baylor-specific (not generic-template) technology workflows, and a guiding-principles/policy framework for staff and interns. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md Section 1, 3.2]\n- A three-phase roadmap (Foundation, weeks 1-4; Operational Depth, weeks 5-10; Education and Culture, weeks 11-16+) is the stated plan to close those gaps, ending with an annual Playbook review cycle each May. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md Section 4]\n- Some assessments coaches already run inside practice should not be duplicated; use that existing data to triangulate rather than re-testing, and set objective criteria for coach-mandated tests when none exist. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8)**; was Inferred (author uncertain) [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md, \"Team Cultures and Assessments\"]\n\n## Testing and assessment\n\n- Assessment is continuous, not a single test day: start with movement quality, injury history, and training background, then observe daily via warm-ups and practice. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.4-5]\n- Testing protocol has to be exactly repeatable over time (\"how you do it in January needs to be the same as how you do it in April\") or the resulting data cannot be trusted for comparison. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.6]\n- Favor a small number of valid, reliable tests mapped to clear KPI categories over an exhaustive battery — illustrated with a simple baseball profile (bodyweight, 60-yard dash, vertical jump, broad jump, back squat, push-up max). **Inferred (transcript + Week 1 slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.6; week1-lecture.md p.16]\n- Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.7] (See the cadence bullet below: there is no department-wide cadence.)\n- There is no common testing cadence across sports; each sport keeps its own schedule. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q9): \"We currently don't have a consistent cadence across all sports. Individual sports have their own unique schedules.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q9)**\n- No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q10): \"things are ballparked based on other information we're grabbing on the athlete: watching them move in real time, then using historical context to make future programming decisions around projected 1RMs.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**\n- Change of direction is not tested in most sports. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q13): \"If the data is captured, it's on an individual sport basis and that information lives with that coach.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q13)** The RTP templates still gate on COD and FMS, run by Athletic Medicine or the sport coach (Tension 18, resolved 2026-10-02).\n- The Assessment Manual V12 is not current. A new, more robust manual is in development and will incorporate the NCAA technology and sport-science recommendations. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q8)** Every bullet below sourced to `ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md` is therefore Legacy.\n- Preparedness (the athlete's underlying physical capability, built over a long period) is explicitly distinguished from readiness (day-to-day fluctuation) — both are tracked, but they answer different questions. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.7]\n- Reassessment timing is governed by three factors: proximity to competition, the expected magnitude of change for that training phase and athlete level, and current recovery status: \"do not reassess an athlete if there is known fatigue.\" **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8)**; was Inferred (author uncertain) [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md, \"Reassessment\"]\n- 1RM testing is reserved for intermediate/advanced athletes with real exposure to near-maximal loads; novices and most women's-sport testing default to repetition-max protocols, which are described as better tolerated neurologically, psychologically, and physically for smaller-framed lifters. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**: no sport tests true 1RM now; was Inferred (transcript + Week 1 slides) [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.18; week1-lecture.md p.39]\n- Velocity-based 1RM estimation (linear-displacement transducer, ~10% load increments to 85-90% or a target mean velocity) is preferred over live 1RM attempts to avoid the fatigue and injury risk of true maxing, with published target mean velocities at 1RM (squat 0.3 m/s, bench 0.2 m/s, power hang snatch 1.0 m/s, power hang clean 0.8 m/s). **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8, Q10)**; was Inferred (author uncertain) [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md] Inference: Q10 describes projection from observation and history and does not mention a velocity protocol.\n- The Baylor Sport Assessment Table (V4) assigns a specific battery per sport (movement screen, anthropometrics, CMJ, broad jump, weighted throw, upper/lower strength estimation, strength endurance, aerobic fitness, repeat sprint ability, linear sprint, change of direction) rather than testing every sport the same way. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q9)**; was Inferred. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q9): \"That assessment schedule was never used.\" Inference: the table was never practice, not just outdated. [update-baylor-sport-assessment-table-v4.md]\n- The current data dictionary is organized by vendor (Catapult, Perch, ForceDecks, Polar), not by sport, which Eric's own gap analysis flags as a real usability problem for staff and a priority to fix with sport-specific, threshold-contextualized dictionaries. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md 3.2.3, Phase 2]\n\n## Force plate and neuromuscular monitoring\n\n- Force plates (CMJ testing) were already the department's longitudinal performance-tracking tool well before the 2022-23 wellness-survey experiment, and remained in place after that experiment ended. **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n- Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective wellness questionnaires, because it is \"objective, consistent, and actionable in a way the wellness surveys never quite were.\" **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n- The mRSI decision read happens on Wednesdays, against individualized thresholds, not a single cut-off or position-group thresholds. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q2): \"Wednesdays, the thresholds should be individualized.\" Individualized against: Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q2a): \"Percent change from baseline or a rolling average. We've used standard deviations and SWC to diagnose signal versus noise.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q2, Q2a)** Inference: \"should be\" may describe the intended rule rather than a fully running one; no numeric threshold was given. See Tension 12 (daily testing versus a Wednesday read), resolved 2026-10-02: daily jumps build the baseline and SWC, Wednesday is the decision read.\n- The dynamic strength index (DSI: CMJ against isometric mid-thigh pull peak force) chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q21): \"we incorporate the countermovement jump and the isometric mid-thigh pull to get a dynamic strength index, to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)** This is not the SJ-CMJ gap the question asked about. Evidence check done 2026-09-29 (`docs/synthesis/_work/dsi-evidence-check.md`): cite it as Eric's practice, tagged [contested; the ratio tracks IMTP strength; one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]. See Tension 17.\n- The standard CMJ family run on ForceDecks: hands-free squat, squat jump (quarter-squat start, 3s hold), countermovement jump (x3), hop test / repeated CMJ (10 reps total, n=5 pairs), and single-leg CMJ (x3 per leg) — each with a scripted weigh-in, stabilization, and rep protocol. **Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)** [revised-force-plate-testing-v3.md]\n- The isometric seated calf raise is run on the ForceDecks \"Mid-Thigh Pull\" setting: 90-degree ankle/knee position, belt pre-tensioned to a fingertip's gap, 3 maximal 6-second contractions with 15 seconds' rest between reps. **Inferred (protocol)** [protocol-iso-seated-calf-raise.md; revised-force-plate-testing-v3.md]\n- Metric selection for force-plate reporting follows a disciplined framework: pick 1-2 output metrics (the obvious performance result, e.g. jump height), 1-3 driver metrics (what produces the output, e.g. braking/propulsive force), 1-3 strategy metrics (how the athlete achieved it, e.g. phase durations), optional symmetry metrics (L/R differences), and add \"fluffy\" metrics (peak power, average power, RFD) only with a specific, defensible rationale. **Adopted practice, framework not Eric's own** [hd-odsf-metrics.md — origin: Hawkin Dynamics]\n- NordBord hamstring testing was retired for roster-wide football monitoring (after starting January 2023) because a 25-30 minute per-group session could not be sustained across 120+ athletes without large, uncontrolled gaps in the data, and staff turnover ended the coordination needed to run it at scale. The decision rule stated: keep a demanding test only where you have \"smaller numbers, clear clinical purpose, dedicated oversight\" (now used for targeted return-to-play groups paired with joint-specific isometric testing); drop it where you can't guarantee consistent, comparable collection. **Explicit** [2026-02-20-sportsmith-6otb-nordbord-er.md]\n\n## Load monitoring and GPS\n\n- The core GPS technology stack is Catapult (device/OpenField software) with Kitman Labs as the central athlete-management system for cross-technology storage, scaling, and visualization. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.7]\n- Core GPS metrics tracked: maximum velocity, total distance, player load, field time, heart-rate bands, velocity/acceleration/deceleration effort bands, and Inertial Movement Analysis (IMA) for indoor sessions without GPS coverage. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md pp.11-13, Data Dictionary]\n- A documented step-by-step SOP exists for the full GPS session lifecycle: charging, alarm-setting, activity creation, live period management, post-session sync, data cleaning (spike detection), tagging, and PDF export/reporting. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md pp.9-11]\n- No sport-specific or position-specific GPS/load thresholds currently exist in the documented data dictionary; it is generic and vendor-organized, and building contextualized per-sport thresholds is named as unfinished, prioritized work. **Explicit (gap, stated as a gap)** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md 3.2.3, Phase 2] The formal gap is confirmed. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q7): \"They do not exist formally.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q7)** The question also asked about informal thresholds; that half was not answered.\n- Heavy GPS/load-monitoring data was described as having bred a \"culture of fear\" in the field broadly (do less, to protect recovery), which the course material argues actually leaves athletes under-prepared for competition. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.9-10]\n- Daily subjective wellness surveys, rolled out across multiple sports via the AMS app starting summer 2022, were abandoned after roughly one academic year because athletes learned to game the consistent, low-effort answers the survey rewarded; the data looked clean but carried no real signal. Wellness surveys are still used, deliberately, in return-to-play scenarios and with athletes managing chronic health conditions, where subjective context still adds value objective data can't supply. **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n\n## Speed and sprint\n\n- Linear sprint testing uses a 2-point start for team-sport athletes (for reliability), athlete self-selected start timing, at least two maximal-effort reps over 30-40 meters. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8)**; was Inferred (author uncertain) [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md]\n- Split times are interpreted by physical quality, not just total time: 0-10m is read as an acceleration score (reflecting maximal strength/power/slow stretch-shortening-cycle development), 30-40m as a speed score (reflecting sprint-specific/fast stretch-shortening-cycle development), and 40m peak velocity is derived from the 30-40m split divided by 10. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8)**; was Inferred (author uncertain) [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md]\n- SmartSpeed timing gates are the standard tool for sprint, agility, and reaction-time testing across the department. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.7]\n- Speed is taught as the quality with the shortest ground-contact-time demand (~0.08s male, ~0.10s female at max velocity) and is described as harder to train and more genetically constrained than strength or power, but as the biggest separator between good and great athletes at the elite level. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.11]\n- Issurin's residual training effects (speed fades fastest, 5 ± 3 days; Block Periodization PDF p. 35, as cited in `docs/synthesis/peaking-and-taper-in-season-football.md`) drive speed programming. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q16): \"I believe Issurin. It drives our speed programming in the off-season and what drives sprinting recommendations in season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q16)** See the residual-effects bullets under Conditioning and Periodization, and Tension 11.\n\n## Strength and power\n\n- The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \"compass.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + shared course material) [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.9-10; week1-lecture.md pp.23,26]\n- Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk), replacing the loose and often-misapplied idea of \"sport specific.\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.13-16; week1-lecture.md pp.32-36]\n- The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + slides) [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.14; week1-lecture.md p.34]\n- Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints); single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.13; week1-lecture.md p.31]\n- Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work. In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + shared slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.20-21; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.21] Not claimed in the interview. The in-season dose itself is unknown: Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q18): \"I don't have the specific sets and reps.\"\n- Undulating/non-linear periodization (Parker/Miller/Panariello), waving volume week to week (e.g., medium-high, medium, high, low over four weeks) is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression, specifically to avoid staleness while still building tolerance for higher loads. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript, speaker unconfirmed but first-person: \"For me personally, this is a more preferential model to use\") [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.24-26]\n- Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block, used consistently across strength and power teaching. **Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.19; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.15] Inference: with no true 1RM testing (Q10), any %1RM prescription rests on projected maxes. Kept on that explicit basis (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02).\n- Strength's residual training effect is treated as roughly 30 days; a minimal retention dose is still trained within that window, even during lower-strength-emphasis blocks. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.24-25] Inference: consistent with Eric siding with Issurin (Q16), whose strength residual is 30 ± 5 days, though Q16 was about speed and he did not claim this bullet.\n- Tracy Fober's maxim (\"use a barbell like a chisel, not a sledgehammer\") is adopted as a guiding standard: prioritize movement quality and confidence-through-competence over chasing hypertrophy or 1RM numbers, especially early in a training cycle. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.21; week1-lecture.md p.46]\n\n## Conditioning and energy systems\n\n- Conditioning is defined two ways: (1) how well an athlete can meet the energy requirements of their specific sport/activity, and (2) how well they sustain strength, speed, and power output across a full competition. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md p.22]\n- All three energy systems (alactic ~10-12s highest power, glycolytic/anaerobic-lactic ~60-90s medium capacity, aerobic hours-long lowest power/highest capacity) are always active simultaneously; programming targets the adaptation, not the drill label. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md p.19; class-4-2026.md p.15]\n- A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once — priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands (e.g., for football: prepared for fall camp first, then the first game). **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.31]\n- For football specifically, the stated priority stack is: Goal 1, alactic and aerobic capacity/power for fall-camp demands; Goal 2, the same two systems for game demands; glycolytic conditioning is treated as a \"worst case scenario\" to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md pp.34, 39]; claimed again in the interview, **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**\n- Glycolytic conditioning is still not a primary target in season, but staff do program it in the off-season. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q19): \"It does hold up, but our staff does program it in the off-season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)**\n- Anaerobic (lactate) threshold heart-rate zones are assigned by athlete body size rather than a single number: roughly 165 bpm for big/tall athletes, 170 for medium, 175 for small, with Zone 1-4 bands set as percentages of that individual threshold. **Superseded by interview (Q20)**; was Explicit [class-4-2026.md pp.28-29]\n- The anaerobic-threshold heart rate in use is the age-based method (the slide deck's max HR = 220-age for men, 226-age for women, threshold at about 85% of that [class-4-2026.md p.27]). Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q20): \"Age-based.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q20)** Resolves Tension 6.\n- The traits easiest to build (aerobic) are lost fastest under detraining; the traits hardest to build (alactic/speed) are lost slowest; this ordering is used to sequence what needs the most frequent re-exposure. **Superseded by interview (Q16)**; was Explicit [class-4-2026.md p.47] Eric now sides with Issurin (speed fades fastest). See Tension 11.\n- Training intensities between roughly 75-90% of maximal effort are labeled (via Francis) \"no man's land\" — too fast to build alactic power cleanly, too slow to build real aerobic capacity — and are treated as a zone to generally avoid as a primary training target. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.48]\n- \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\": lifting, agility work, and skill drills each load a specific energy system (power vs. capacity, alactic/glycolytic/aerobic) and should be classified and counted as conditioning, not treated as separate from it. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.31, tables pp.35-42]\n- For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts), not distance capacity — a better-developed aerobic system means more available energy on play 10, 11, or 12 of a drive, not longer continuous running ability. **Inferred (transcript)** [2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md p.9]\n- Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md p.51; 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.11-12]\n- Joel Jamieson's *Ultimate MMA Conditioning* is recommended as the single best practical resource for energy-system development, on the basis that its methods transfer cleanly to non-combat sports despite the MMA framing. **Explicit** [week3-lecture.md pp.49-50; 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.11-12]\n\n## Rehab and return to play\n\n- Applied Performance's stated role in return-to-performance is to supply information that supports Athletics Performance and Athletic Medicine's decisions on training and RTP periodization — a support/advisory role, not a stated final-decision role. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]\n- What Applied Performance actually contributes to RTP is testing and progression, with shared loading decisions, and the involvement has no fixed end point. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q4): \"Testing and progression. I collaborate on loading and progression decisions when needed. It doesn't really end.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q4)** See Tension 14 on \"support\" versus \"doesn't really end\".\n- RTP does not include a readiness assessment by Applied Performance; the written templates carry that decision. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q22): \"We don't assess readiness. The template answers that.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q22)** See Tension 13.\n- KPI-threshold identification for diagnostic screening and performance testing is named as a departmental operating principle, but no specific RTP criteria, battery, or phased-return protocol appears anywhere in this corpus. **Explicit (principle) / gap (no criteria stated)** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6] Update: a written protocol exists outside the corpus. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q4, Q4a): \"There is a written protocol\", namely the Baylor UE Rehab Program Template and the Baylor LE Rehab Program Template (both .docx, currently in Eric's Downloads folder). **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q4a)** Not yet staged, so Flint does not know their content; staging a text copy is the next step.\n- Daily wellness-style subjective monitoring, dropped for general roster use, is explicitly kept in return-to-play scenarios specifically because subjective context still fills a gap objective testing can't. **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n- NordBord hamstring testing, dropped for roster-wide use, is explicitly retained for smaller, targeted return-to-play groups paired with joint-specific isometric testing (ForceFrame-style). **Explicit** [2026-02-20-sportsmith-6otb-nordbord-er.md]\n\n*This was the thinnest discipline in the corpus. After the interview (2026-09-28), the role is clear (testing and progression, collaborative loading, Q4) and a written protocol exists (the UE and LE templates, Q4a), but its criteria are still unread by Flint until the templates are staged.*\n\n## Injury risk and prevention\n\n- Injury risk is reduced, not eliminated, through sound progression of load, training means, and density, in collaboration with Athletic Medicine — an explicit refusal to claim injury prevention as achievable. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.4; week1-lecture.md p.9]\n- Bodyweight-control competency is treated as a marker of injury risk in its own right: an athlete who can bench 400 lb but can't do 10 clean push-ups has \"something out of whack\" that needs fixing regardless of their strength numbers. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.5]\n- Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design; abrupt, large changes are treated as a direct injury-risk driver, not just a training-quality issue. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.27]\n- No formal injury-risk model (ACWR, tissue-capacity tracking, a biomechanical screening protocol with defined red flags) is attributed to Eric anywhere in this corpus; the detailed treatment of ACWR and injury-risk modeling that does exist (Clemson curriculum) is reference material only, not confirmed Baylor practice. Confirmed by the interview: there is no formal risk model.\n- ACWR is looked at but not trusted for any decision. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q12): \"We dabble with ACWR but I don't personally trust it for anything. I think it's raw, has error, is misleading, and lacks context generally. We don't have any formal risk model that we follow. We've avoided them up to this point, but there's not a really good reason why.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q12)**\n- Movement screens are collected and filed, not used to change programs. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q12): \"there isn't anything that changes decisions made to a program; it's more or less just collected and referred to for future needs.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q12)** Inference: this sits uneasily with his own Q3 rule; see Tension 15.\n\n*Thin discipline, now answered: the finding is that nothing formal exists (no risk model, ACWR distrusted, screens not acted on).*\n\n## Recovery and sleep\n\n- Recovery capacity must be \"earned\" by training hard enough first; recovery technology (Theragun, Normatec, cryo) is treated as useful but not a substitute for managing training stress and dose correctly. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.9]\n- Recovery appears in the department's own subcategory list of Athletic Performance (alongside strength, conditioning, speed, agility, power, nutrition, applied performance, sports psychology, RTP), but no specific recovery protocol, sleep target, or monitoring tool is described anywhere in this corpus. **Explicit (named as a subcategory) / gap (no protocol stated)** [week1-lecture.md p.11]\n- Recovery is managed through training-load design alone; nothing objective is tracked, sleep included. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q23): \"It's handled through training-load design alone. We're not tracking anything objective.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q23)**\n\n*Thin discipline, now answered: the finding is that nothing formal exists beyond load design.*\n\n## Nutrition and body composition\n\n- Performance Nutrition is explicitly a separate Health & Wellness unit from Applied Performance, owning individual nutrition consultations, supplement education, body composition and bone density assessment, and the training table/fueling stations. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.5]\n- Body composition testing is capped at no more than 4 times per year. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8)**; was Inferred (author uncertain) [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md, \"Anthropometrics\"]\n- Anthropometrics (height, weight, muscle-to-fat ratio) are described as strongly associated with success in most sports and worth tracking even though they are owned by Performance Nutrition rather than Applied Performance. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q8)**; was Inferred (author uncertain) [ap-assessment-manual-draft-v12.md, \"Anthropometrics\"]\n- No nutrition decision rules, body-composition targets, or thresholds are attributed to Eric anywhere in this corpus; this appears to be a deliberate department-design choice (Performance Nutrition owns it) rather than an oversight.\n- No force-plate or GPS data flows to Performance Nutrition; bodyweight is consulted only when there is a problem. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q24): \"It does not. Only if there is an issue will bodyweight be consulted. Nothing proactive.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q24)**\n\n*Thin discipline, now answered: the finding is that there is no proactive data link between Applied Performance and Performance Nutrition.*\n\n## Coaching, skill acquisition and constraints-led approach\n\n- A trained \"coach's eye,\" built through repetition and attention, is treated as at least as important as any technology for daily assessment: \"you don't need a fancy test or anything to do that. But you do have to have a good coach's eye.\" **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.8] Eric's own position (interview 2026-09-28, Q6) is narrower: the eye tells you where to look, and data then investigates. See the Philosophy bullet and Tension 3.\n- Constraints-led and external-cue coaching are not part of Eric's practice. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q25): \"I don't apply it, as I don't fully understand it. I want to know more.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q25)** A learning request, a candidate for a primer.\n- New movement patterns are expected to take roughly 3-4 weeks of practice to become efficient and low-energy-cost, which informs how much proficiency to expect when a new exercise or drill is introduced. **Inferred (transcript)** [2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.6-7]\n- Movement efficiency (skill) is treated as directly reducing energy cost — elite athletes (cited examples: LeBron James, Deion Sanders) are described as outlasting and outperforming peers partly through tactical efficiency that lowers the physical output required to achieve the same result. **Inferred (transcript)** [2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.8-9]\n- Basic movement skills and basic lifts are taught before anything advanced, on the observed premise that many incoming athletes have high sport-specific skill from early specialization but poor general movement competency. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.4-5]\n\n*Still thin. The interview added the coach's-eye rule (Q6) and confirmed that constraints-led coaching is not in use (Q25). The rest of this section is shared course material, unclaimed.*\n\n## Periodization and programming\n\n- Macro/meso/microcycle structure is used as the standard planning language, with an explicit reminder that a \"training week\" does not have to equal 7 calendar days — it can be extended to fit training themes around a real competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.17-18; week1-lecture.md p.39]\n- The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power, each with defined intensity/volume bands (e.g., hypertrophy phase 50-75% intensity, 3-6 sets of 5-20 reps; strength phase 80-90% intensity, moderate volume), before a transition into competition. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.19-21; week1-lecture.md pp.43-48]\n- In-season programming deliberately waves volume and intensity even when the practice schedule looks repetitive week to week, specifically to avoid training monotony for the athlete. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.21]\n- Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block — \"you can't ride two horses with one saddle.\" Compatible qualities are trained together; the rest are trained at a retention dose. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.28-29; week1-lecture.md p.70]\n- An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract: lay out the competition calendar, key non-training dates (holidays, exam weeks), and phase-by-phase volume/intensity waves, then adapt the route as circumstances change while still reaching the same destination. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.22-23; 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.13-15]\n- Training residual effects set the retraining cadence: aerobic capacity and max strength both need re-exposure roughly every 30 days; speed needs re-exposure roughly every 5 days or tissue readiness for max-velocity work degrades. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.27] Inference: these are Issurin's figures, and Eric sides with Issurin (interview 2026-09-28, Q16), so the speed half reads as his; the aerobic and strength halves were not asked directly.\n- In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q14): \"in football you want to peak every Saturday, but definitely throughout the month of November as you get closer to the postseason ... That's when players need to be at or near their peak as they head into the postseason and playoffs.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)** He did not say whether the postseason gets a distinct taper.\n- The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**\n- Bye weeks: three practice days, extra rest and recovery, slightly higher practice volume than a game week, a speed and strength top-up, and harder work for players with few game snaps. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q17): \"guys that aren't getting a ton of play will probably practice harder that week than guys that have gotten a ton of snaps.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q17)**\n- The head coach's system of play and roster identity (e.g., a \"home run hitters\" lineup vs. a \"steal bases\" lineup) is treated as a direct input into whether a team's programming leans toward maximal strength or toward speed/power emphasis. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.16]\n\n## Data, technology and reporting\n\n- The full technology stack: Catapult (GPS), Kitman Labs (central AMS/data store), Perch (camera-based velocity-based training), Polar (heart rate), and the VALD suite — DynaMo, ForceDecks, ForceFrame, Hub, NordBord, SmartSpeed. **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.7]\n- Kitman Labs is positioned specifically as the cross-technology data store and analysis/visualization layer, distinct from each vendor's own reporting tool (e.g., VALD Hub, OpenField Cloud). **Explicit** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.7]\n- System of record: each source system holds its own data (force data in VALD, Catapult data in OpenField), Kitman AMS aggregates, and the source system wins when Kitman disagrees. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q11): \"They're separate entities; one doesn't overrule the other. If Kitman had wrong numbers from VALD or OpenField, we would go to those sources to correct it: the source systems overrule any errors Kitman made bringing data into the platform.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q11)**\n- Documented GPS/VALD workflows exist for the full device lifecycle, but Eric's own gap analysis flags them as generic, template-driven, and lacking Baylor-specific detail (serial numbers, storage locations, troubleshooting) except for the Catapult workflow, which has genuine operational depth. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md 3.2.2]\n- Credential/login management is currently placeholder-only in the Playbook; a secure credential-vault protocol is named as needed but not yet implemented. **Explicit (gap, stated as a to-do)** [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.8; performance-playbook-development-plan.md Section 4]\n- Standardized weekly/monthly coach-facing reporting templates do not yet exist; building them is named as unfinished Phase 2 work. **Explicit (gap, stated as a to-do)** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md Phase 2] Who builds reports today: strength staff for most teams (interview 2026-09-28, Q28; see Department model).\n- Tensiomyography (TMG) is part of the toolkit, with a documented setup/operations SOP, but that SOP is headed with the names of a prior Director and Associate Director of Applied Performance (Althoff, Nelson), not Eric, and current active use is unclear from this corpus. **Legacy (interview 2026-09-28, Q29)**; was Inferred (legacy document, status unclear) [technologytemplatesop.md] Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q29): \"Only Chris Ruf with Track still uses it.\"\n\n## Education and staff development\n\n- HHPR 5355 (\"Power, Speed, Agility, & Periodization in Training\") is Eric's own graduate-level course, co-taught with Chris Ruf and Michael Wenger; grading is split across two exams (10% and 20%), a group training-plan project (20%), and lecture/lab participation (40%), with a practical (teach-back) component on exams. **Inferred (transcript, syllabus content)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.1]\n- A standalone, 16-week Intern Sport Science Curriculum is proposed, deliberately separate from the Playbook so it can be distributed and revised on its own cycle, aligned to the NSCA Essentials of Sport Science (CPSS) textbook, and adapted from a Clemson model to Baylor's own technology stack (ForceDecks, Catapult, ForceFrame, NordBord, DynaMo, Perch, SmartSpeed, Polar) and to include football alongside Olympic-sport examples. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md Section 5] Status: a proposal only. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q30): \"It has not been built yet.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q30)**\n- Curriculum design principle: interns need both the \"why\" (conceptual education) and the \"how\" (hands-on technical training) each week, and should leave the program with a professional portfolio of real work products (dashboards, reports, presentations, infographics), not just device-operation checklists. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md 5.1, 5.4]\n- The proposed curriculum deliberately adds sessions on interdisciplinary collaboration with Athletic Medicine, Performance Nutrition, and Mental Health Services, reflecting Baylor's specific department structure (Applied Performance inside Health & Wellness) rather than copying Clemson's Olympic-sports-only framing. **Explicit** [performance-playbook-development-plan.md 5.3]\n\n---\n\n## No material found\n\nNone. Every one of the sixteen required sections had at least some material. Several sections are thin, though, and should be read as gaps rather than settled positions: **Rehab and return to play**, **Injury risk and prevention**, **Recovery and sleep**, **Nutrition and body composition**, and **Coaching, skill acquisition and constraints-led approach**. See `interview-prep.md`.\n\nUpdate after the interview (2026-09-28): four of the five thin sections now have an answer, and for three of them the answer is that nothing formal exists, which is itself the finding. Injury risk: no formal model, ACWR distrusted, screens not acted on (Q12). Recovery and sleep: load design only, nothing objective tracked (Q23). Nutrition: no proactive data flow (Q24). Rehab and RTP: role stated (Q4, Q22) and a written protocol exists in the UE and LE templates (Q4a), not yet staged. Coaching stays thin: constraints-led coaching is not used (Q25), and the rest is unclaimed course material.\n\n---\n\n## Tensions and contradictions\n\nStatus after the interview (2026-09-28): resolved 1 (partly), 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9; still open 7 (partly), 8, 10. New tensions from the interview are 11 to 16. Resolved by Eric 2026-10-02: 12, 16 and 18 (COD/FMS gates). Each entry below carries its status on its last line.\n\n1. **The task's own premise doesn't fully hold.** Both class transcripts (Class 1 and Class 3) are diarized entirely under a single Fireflies speaker label, \"Chris Ruf,\" for every line — including Class 3, which the Spring 2026 teaching schedule assigns solo to Rash (`spring-2026-teaching.md`: \"Feb. 5 | Conditioning - Alactic-Aerobic | Rash\"). Fireflies likely defaulted to the meeting organizer's name instead of doing real per-speaker diarization. This means roughly 30,000 words of the richest, most first-person material in this corpus cannot be confidently attributed to Rash specifically versus Ruf or Wenger. Every transcript-sourced bullet above is marked Inferred for this reason, even where the underlying statement is a confident, first-person claim.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): partly resolved.** Q1 claimed four positions (Vermeil hierarchy, undulating periodization, Dynamic Correspondence, the football energy-system stack), now Explicit. Every other transcript bullet stays Inferred.\n\n2. **Department reporting model is not internally consistent.** The Playbook (p.6) frames Applied Performance as a peer discipline inside Health & Wellness that \"enables\" Athletics Performance, Athletic Medicine, Nutrition, and Mental Health to answer their own questions. But Week 1 course material lists \"Applied Performance\" as one of many training sub-categories nested under Athletic Performance itself (p.11), and the same transcript narration describes Rash's role as \"helping program and plan the training\" — a support-staff framing, not a peer-department framing. Which model actually governs day-to-day authority is not reconciled anywhere in these documents.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q5): \"Both.\" Both framings are true at once.\n\n3. **Coach's-eye-first versus objective-first is argued both ways.** The course material repeatedly argues that \"you don't need a fancy test... you have to have a good coach's eye\" and criticizes the GPS/load-monitoring era for breeding a fear-driven under-training culture. Eric's own SportSmith writing moves the opposite direction on daily monitoring specifically: he replaced subjective wellness surveys with objective force-plate mRSI because subjective self-report couldn't be trusted. Both share a skepticism of trendy or convenient data, but they land on opposite defaults (trust the trained eye vs. require an objective number) for the daily-monitoring question specifically.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q6): the coach's eye flags where to look; data then investigates. The two are sequential, not rival defaults.\n\n4. **The Assessment Manual (V12) reads as an older document than the current technology stack.** It builds its 1RM-estimation protocol around GymAware, and its CMJ description references a \"contact mat\" for jump height. The current Playbook technology stack and the Development Plan both center velocity-based training on Perch, not GymAware, and the current Force Plate Testing V3 protocol runs all CMJ variants exclusively on ForceDecks force plates, with no contact mat mentioned. This suggests the Assessment Manual predates the current VALD/Perch-centered stack and may be a legacy document rather than the currently operative protocol — worth confirming directly with Eric before treating any of its specifics as current practice.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q8): V12 \"is not current\"; a new manual built on the NCAA technology and sport-science recommendations is in development. All V12 bullets are marked Legacy.\n\n5. **Testing cueing instructions conflict across two protocol documents for the same test.** The Assessment Manual's CMJ instructions say explicitly: \"Do not coach the strategy of jumping.\" The current Force Plate Testing V3 protocol gives testers specific verbal cues to give athletes during CMJ, squat-jump, and hop-test administration (\"keep chest up,\" \"land softly,\" \"land stiff-legged on only the toes\"). These may be reconcilable (safety/technique cues vs. jump-strategy cues are different things), but the two documents read differently on how much a tester should say out loud.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved by Q8.** Inference: with V12 not current, the Force Plate Testing V3 cues govern. Eric did not address the cueing question itself.\n\n6. **Two different anaerobic-threshold heart-rate methods appear back to back in the same slide deck without being reconciled.** One slide gives an age-based formula (max HR = 220-age for men, 226-age for women, threshold at ~85% of that), and the very next slides give a body-size-based table (big/medium/small athlete, ~165/170/175 bpm threshold) as the operational standard used elsewhere throughout the conditioning material. Which one is actually used day to day is not stated. [class-4-2026.md pp.27-29]\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric (Q20): \"Age-based.\" The body-size table is marked Superseded. Note that the body-size table was from Eric's own assigned session, so his slides and his practice differ.\n\n7. **The Development Plan's own critique cuts against the corpus it's part of.** It states the current Baylor Playbook \"has no educational component whatsoever\" and reads \"like a vendor product reference manual,\" even though Eric personally co-teaches a full graduate-level course covering much of the same material the Playbook is missing. The department's teaching capacity clearly exists; it just hasn't been captured back into the Playbook or a formal intern curriculum yet.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): still open, confirmed.** Eric (Q30): the intern curriculum \"has not been built yet.\"\n\n8. **The Playbook's NordBord documentation has not caught up with practice.** The Playbook's Technology Overview and Workflows still document a full, unqualified, roster-style NordBord data-collection SOP. The SportSmith piece describes explicitly retiring roster-wide NordBord testing for football starting after January 2023, keeping it only for small, targeted return-to-play groups. The written SOP has not been updated to reflect that decision.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): open.** Not asked. Inference: the Q3 rule (\"if it doesn't answer a question anyone has...\") would retire the roster-wide SOP.\n\n9. **A personally-stated periodization preference is stated inside an unreliable-speaker transcript.** \"For me personally, this is a more preferential model to use\" (about undulating over linear periodization) is a strong, direct, first-person preference claim — but it comes from the same unreliably-diarized Class 1 transcript described in Tension 1, so it cannot be confirmed as Eric's own stated preference without his direct confirmation.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): resolved.** Eric claimed undulating periodization (Q1).\n\n10. **Older vs. current unit-goal templates show almost no adopted content.** The Geyer and Skodinski Unit Goal Programming Standards documents are staff-authored templates for individual sport programming philosophy; Geyer's is mostly filled in (periodization model, weekly split, KPI tests for softball), Skodinski's is nearly blank (just the section headers). Neither is attributed to Eric, and there's no equivalent document in this corpus showing Eric's own answers to the same template questions — a real gap if Flint wants Eric's own unit-goal-style programming philosophy in his own words.\n   **Status (interview 2026-09-28): open.** Not asked.\n\nNew tensions raised by the interview (2026-09-28):\n\n11. **Eric's own slide against his interview on residual effects.** The class-4 slide (p. 47), from a session Eric taught, says aerobic fitness is lost fastest and speed slowest. In the interview he sides with Issurin, speed fades fastest (Q16), and says Issurin drives speed programming. The slide bullet is marked Superseded. Inference: the slide still circulates in HHPR 5355 and should be reconciled before next spring. The peaking synthesis keeps the counter-evidence both ways.\n12. **Daily mRSI testing against a Wednesday read.** The SportSmith piece describes mRSI from daily CMJ testing as the daily readiness marker [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]. Q2 names Wednesday as the day. Inference: daily collection with one weekly decision day would reconcile them, but Eric did not say which; worth one follow-up.\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** Daily jumps are collected to build the rolling baseline and SWC; Wednesday is the single decision read that changes the week.\n13. **\"We don't assess readiness\" against mRSI as the readiness marker.** Q22 says Applied Performance does not assess readiness in RTP; the SportSmith piece and Q2 describe mRSI readiness reads. Inference: Q22 is scoped to RTP, so these probably do not conflict, but the RTP templates should be checked.\n14. **\"Support\" role against \"it doesn't really end\".** The Playbook frames RTP input as support to Athletic Medicine and Athletic Performance decisions [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]. Q4 describes ongoing testing, progression and shared loading decisions with no end point. Inference: compatible (support can be continuous), but the Playbook wording understates the role.\n15. **The Q3 rule against current practice.** Eric's rule is that a test with no question or need has no place (Q3). Yet ACWR is still looked at (\"We dabble with ACWR\") though he does not \"trust it for anything\", and movement screens are \"just collected\" without changing decisions (Q12). Inference: by his own rule both are candidates to retire or to be given a question.\n16. **No true 1RM against %1RM prescription.** No sport tests true 1RM (Q10), while Prilepin's chart and the phase bands are written in %1RM [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.19; week1-lecture.md pp.43-48]. Inference: loads are percentages of projected maxes, so their error carries into every prescription. Not a contradiction in practice, but worth stating.\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** Keep %1RM and Prilepin prescription, stated explicitly as built on projected maxes (observation plus history, Q10), not tested 1RMs.\n17. **DSI as a plyo-vs-strength switch against the evidence.** Eric uses DSI to choose plyometric versus max-strength emphasis (Q21). The evidence check (`docs/synthesis/_work/dsi-evidence-check.md`) finds that IMTP PF explains most of DSI's variance, that the 0.60/0.80 cutoffs were never validated, and that the one controlled trial found a benefit only in its strength arm. Inference: keep the test, but decide on CMJ PF and IMTP PF alongside the ratio, and set cutoffs from the roster.\n18. **COD and FMS gates in the RTP templates against Q12/Q13.** The LE template gates Evaluation on the 10-5 COD test and on FMS > 14 (`docs/interview/2026-09-28-method-interview.md`), while Q13 says COD is not tested in most sports and Q12 says screens do not change decisions.\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** The gates stay in the RTP templates. The baseline names who runs them (Athletic Medicine or the sport coach), not Applied Performance, consistent with \"the template answers readiness\" (Q22).\n\n---\n\n## Named influences\n\n| Influence | What's credited to them | Where | Provenance |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Al Vermeil (transcribed once as \"Albert Meal\") | Hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity → strength → power → speed → sport skill); described as the department's programming \"compass\" | class1-transcript.md pp.9-10, 18-19 | Claimed by Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q1) |\n| Dan Pfaff (transcribed as \"Coach Path/Paf/Pabst\") | The \"coach's eye\" ideal — biomechanical assessment purely by trained observation, illustrated with an anecdote about diagnosing a runner's spinal alignment from 50 meters away | class1-transcript.md pp.6-7 | Course material, speaker unconfirmed |\n| Yuri Verkhoshansky | Principle of Dynamic Correspondence, for judging real exercise transfer | class1-transcript.md p.14; week1-lecture.md p.34 | Claimed by Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q1) |\n| Anatoly Bondarchuk (transcribed as \"Bondurciu\") | General prep / specific prep / specific development / competitive exercise taxonomy | class1-transcript.md p.16; week1-lecture.md p.36 | Course material |\n| Johnny Parker, Al Miller, Rob Panariello (transcribed as \"Panarillo\") | Undulating/non-linear periodization model, described as personally preferred | class1-transcript.md pp.25-26; week1-lecture.md pp.59-63; lesson8summary.md #19 | Claimed by Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q1) |\n| Vladimir Issurin (*Block Periodization*) | Residual training effects (speed fades fastest); drives off-season speed programming and in-season sprint recommendations | interview 2026-09-28, Q16; class1-transcript.md p.27 and week1-lecture.md p.66 reproduce his table | Claimed by Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q16) |\n| Louis Simmons / Westside Barbell (via Zatsiorsky and Siff) | Conjugate/concurrent method: max effort, dynamic effort, repetitive effort | class1-transcript.md pp.26-27; week1-lecture.md pp.63-66 | Course material |\n| A.S. Prilepin (transcribed once as \"the Philippines chart\") | Rep/set/intensity guide, used as the default across strength and power teaching | class1-transcript.md p.19; week1-lecture.md p.47; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.15 | Shared team material |\n| Tracy Fober | \"Use a barbell like a chisel, not a sledgehammer\" — quality-first coaching maxim, cited from a 2019 Baylor AP clinic and her Instagram | class1-transcript.md p.21; week1-lecture.md p.46 | Course material |\n| Stu McMillan | Four goals of strength training (produce force, absorb force, decrease injury chance, teach positional context) | lesson8summary.md #3; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.5 | Wenger's session (Mar. 19) |\n| Fred Hatfield; JL Holdsworth | Strength/alignment aphorisms (\"can't shoot a cannon out of a canoe\"; \"monster tires on a Prius with bad alignment\") | hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.4; lesson8summary.md #2 | Wenger's session |\n| Joel Jamieson (*Ultimate MMA Conditioning*) | Recommended as the best practical resource on energy-system development methods | 2026-02-05-hp5355-class3-transcript.md pp.11-12; week3-lecture.md pp.49-50 | Eric's assigned session (transcript speaker unconfirmed; slides confirmed) |\n| Jason Avedesian / Clemson Olympic Sports Science | Entire intern curriculum (force plates, GPS, force-velocity profiling, data hygiene, visualization, soft skills, research methods, injury risk, S&C) | intencurriculumn.md, all pages | Reference source, not Eric's voice — used by the Development Plan as a benchmark |\n| Hawkin Dynamics | Output/Driver/Strategy/Fluffy (ODSF) metric-selection framework for force-plate reporting | hd-odsf-metrics.md | Vendor framework, adopted as practice |\n| NSCA (*Essentials of Sport Science* / CPSS) | Textbook basis for the Clemson curriculum and for Baylor's own proposed intern curriculum | performance-playbook-development-plan.md Section 5 | Cited by Eric's own Development Plan |\n| Zatsiorsky; Haff & Triplett; Parker/Miller/Panariello (*The System*) | Recommended HHPR 5355 course textbooks | week1-lecture.md p.4 | Course syllabus |\n","numLines":287,"startLine":1,"totalLines":287}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c1bd450a-f4ec-4e69-990d-cdcc6deec826","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
