{"parentUuid":"3a0587d1-949e-4b40-adaa-d5988a1c40e8","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpKPMui3i8kSrx3qbN2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WUjXJyk9zzCUz7nxT89UKt","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/ && cat heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice.md; echo ===========; cat framework--compensatory-acceleration-training.md","description":"Read two likely-overlap entries as style refs"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10219,"cache_read_input_tokens":131251,"output_tokens":763,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":581},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10219},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":763,"cache_read_input_tokens":131251,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10219,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10219,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01WUjXJyk9zzCUz7nxT89UKt":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/ && cat heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice.md; echo ===========; cat framework--compensatory-acceleration-training.md","description":"Read two likely-overlap entries as style refs"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffpKP5Y1PnaxqNvFrT2G","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"59033b05-2773-4ba1-9a43-bba1e6f4f5b6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:36.193Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"59033b05-2773-4ba1-9a43-bba1e6f4f5b6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Exit code 1\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"1.1\"\ntopic: strength deficit and method selection\ncategory: [strength, programming, explosive-strength]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# Strength Deficit Size Points to a Different Training Method\n\n**What it is** — If an athlete's strength deficit (see\n[[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]) is large for a given muscle\ngroup, the \"In general\" rule on printed 10 recommends near-maximal neuromuscular\nstimulation (weightlifting or plyometric methods). If the deficit is small, it recommends\nsubmaximal hypertrophy work (bodybuilding-style loading) followed by maximal efforts\nagainst heavy loads. Note: the jump paragraph on the same page gives the opposite pairing\nfor the jump-test version of the deficit (large jump-test difference means 5-8RM\nstrength/hypertrophy work with CAT; small difference means shock/ballistic methods); the\nsource is internally inconsistent on this point (see\n[[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]] for both statements with pages).\nThe book expands CAT two ways: \"Compensatory Activation Training\" with 5-8RM loads\n(printed 10) and, in the separate CAT technique discussion, \"Compensatory Acceleration\nTraining\" for voluntarily managing bar speed, where \"Compensatory Action Training\" is also\nproposed as a broader name (printed 21). The book does not say whether these are the same\nmethod. Verkhoshansky's research, as reported by the authors, found the strength deficit\nincreases as external resistance and movement time decrease, meaning it matters most for fast,\nlow-resistance actions such as running, jumping, and throwing, and matters less for slow,\nhigh-resistance actions. (PDF p. 21, printed p. 10.)\n\n**Why it works** — Inference: a large deficit means there is untapped voluntary/neural\ncapacity still available, so training that improves recruitment and rate of activation (max\neffort or reactive/plyometric methods) has the most room to produce gains. A small deficit\nmeans the athlete is already recruiting close to their ceiling voluntarily, so further\ngains require growing the tissue itself (hypertrophy) before neural methods can express\nmore force. This reasoning applies only to the \"In general\" rule; the source's own\njump-paragraph rule reverses it (see above).\n\n**When to apply** — **Inference:** as a screening question for method selection, applied\nonly to a directly-measured strength deficit (not a jump-test proxy, see below): a large\ndeficit points toward near-maximal/reactive methods (weightlifting, plyometrics) over added\nhypertrophy volume, and a small deficit toward a hypertrophy or general-strength emphasis\nfirst, per the \"In general\" rule (printed 10). This specific application is not stated in\nthe text.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not treat \"CAT\" as a single validated modern method name; the\nbook itself uses it for two different things (5-8RM strength/hypertrophy loading on printed\n10, bar-acceleration cueing on printed 21). **Inference:** do not use this rule, in either\ndirection, to set training emphasis from a jump-test deficit; because the book gives\nopposite pairings for the jump-test case specifically (see\n[[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]), and the modern evidence on that\ntest (Kozinc et al. 2022) does not support a diagnostic cutoff or training algorithm, no\nsafe programming rule can be derived from the jump-test version of this heuristic. The\n\"In general\" rule quoted above is the more textually supported reading for a directly\nmeasured (non-jump-test) deficit, but even that rests on practitioner experience rather\nthan controlled data.\n\n**Example** — Inference: no example is given; the jump-test direction is contested (see\n[[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]] and Kozinc et al. 2022).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]],\n[[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]],\n[[research-papers/kozinc-2022-the-difference-between-squat-jump-and-countermovement-jump-in-770-male-and-female-particip]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--raise-fmm-or-cut-the-explosive-strength-deficit]]: Zatsiorsky's training-stage alternative to this contested deficit-to-method rule; [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]] (5.2.4, brief maximal tension for large resistance and plyometrics for small; it cites this entry for choosing between them by the strength deficit; rated contested)\n(eval):1: ========== not found","is_error":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WUjXJyk9zzCUz7nxT89UKt"}]},"uuid":"87de5129-f11f-4afd-a0e0-a019ea5cdc69","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:36.318Z","toolUseResult":"Error: Exit code 1\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"1.1\"\ntopic: strength deficit and method selection\ncategory: [strength, programming, explosive-strength]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# Strength Deficit Size Points to a Different Training Method\n\n**What it is** — If an athlete's strength deficit (see\n[[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]) is large for a given muscle\ngroup, the \"In general\" rule on printed 10 recommends near-maximal neuromuscular\nstimulation (weightlifting or plyometric methods). If the deficit is small, it recommends\nsubmaximal hypertrophy work (bodybuilding-style loading) followed by maximal efforts\nagainst heavy loads. Note: the jump paragraph on the same page gives the opposite pairing\nfor the jump-test version of the deficit (large jump-test difference means 5-8RM\nstrength/hypertrophy work with CAT; small difference means shock/ballistic methods); the\nsource is internally inconsistent on this point (see\n[[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]] for both statements with pages).\nThe book expands CAT two ways: \"Compensatory Activation Training\" with 5-8RM loads\n(printed 10) and, in the separate CAT technique discussion, \"Compensatory Acceleration\nTraining\" for voluntarily managing bar speed, where \"Compensatory Action Training\" is also\nproposed as a broader name (printed 21). The book does not say whether these are the same\nmethod. Verkhoshansky's research, as reported by the authors, found the strength deficit\nincreases as external resistance and movement time decrease, meaning it matters most for fast,\nlow-resistance actions such as running, jumping, and throwing, and matters less for slow,\nhigh-resistance actions. (PDF p. 21, printed p. 10.)\n\n**Why it works** — Inference: a large deficit means there is untapped voluntary/neural\ncapacity still available, so training that improves recruitment and rate of activation (max\neffort or reactive/plyometric methods) has the most room to produce gains. A small deficit\nmeans the athlete is already recruiting close to their ceiling voluntarily, so further\ngains require growing the tissue itself (hypertrophy) before neural methods can express\nmore force. This reasoning applies only to the \"In general\" rule; the source's own\njump-paragraph rule reverses it (see above).\n\n**When to apply** — **Inference:** as a screening question for method selection, applied\nonly to a directly-measured strength deficit (not a jump-test proxy, see below): a large\ndeficit points toward near-maximal/reactive methods (weightlifting, plyometrics) over added\nhypertrophy volume, and a small deficit toward a hypertrophy or general-strength emphasis\nfirst, per the \"In general\" rule (printed 10). This specific application is not stated in\nthe text.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not treat \"CAT\" as a single validated modern method name; the\nbook itself uses it for two different things (5-8RM strength/hypertrophy loading on printed\n10, bar-acceleration cueing on printed 21). **Inference:** do not use this rule, in either\ndirection, to set training emphasis from a jump-test deficit; because the book gives\nopposite pairings for the jump-test case specifically (see\n[[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]), and the modern evidence on that\ntest (Kozinc et al. 2022) does not support a diagnostic cutoff or training algorithm, no\nsafe programming rule can be derived from the jump-test version of this heuristic. The\n\"In general\" rule quoted above is the more textually supported reading for a directly\nmeasured (non-jump-test) deficit, but even that rests on practitioner experience rather\nthan controlled data.\n\n**Example** — Inference: no example is given; the jump-test direction is contested (see\n[[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]] and Kozinc et al. 2022).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]],\n[[principle--rate-of-force-development-as-independent-limiter]],\n[[research-papers/kozinc-2022-the-difference-between-squat-jump-and-countermovement-jump-in-770-male-and-female-particip]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--raise-fmm-or-cut-the-explosive-strength-deficit]]: Zatsiorsky's training-stage alternative to this contested deficit-to-method rule; [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]] (5.2.4, brief maximal tension for large resistance and plyometrics for small; it cites this entry for choosing between them by the strength deficit; rated contested)\n(eval):1: ========== not found","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"59033b05-2773-4ba1-9a43-bba1e6f4f5b6","sessionKind":"bg","userTyp