{"parentUuid":"585b0f10-39cf-4a26-81e5-f52d832877a1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011qM4XMUHjdh5UrM39vD9eR","type":"tool_result","content":"#### framework--compensatory-acceleration-training\nchapter: \"1.2\"\nconfidence: supported\n# Compensatory (Acceleration/Action) Training: Using Proprioceptive Feedback to Actively Manage Bar Speed\n**What it is** — Free weights (unlike some variable-resistance machines) let the lifter\nfeel changing leverage through the range of motion via proprioceptive feedback and respond\nby voluntarily accelerating or decelerating the bar to raise or lower the force produced.\nThis is termed Compensatory Acceleration Training (CAT), most obviously used\nconcentrically, but the text argues it applies just as much eccentrically (muscles\nactively resist gravity's acceleration to control the descent) and even isometrically\n(the lifter must continuously compensate for fatigue-driven declines in neural drive to\nhold a constant position), leading the author to prefer \"Compensatory Action Training\" as\nthe fuller name. (PDF p. 32, printed p. 21; Ch 7.1 develops it further.)\n\n**Why it works** — Because force is not fixed by the weight alone (F = ma; a lighter bar\n#### framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training\nchapter: \"1\"\nconfidence: supported\n# Functional vs. Structural Resistance Training\n**What it is** — Two basic categories of resistance training effect: structural training\ntargets muscle hypertrophy (and some tissue flexibility); functional training targets\nperformance qualities (static strength, speed-strength, muscular endurance, reactive\nability) through four neural mechanisms: (1) intermuscular coordination, sequencing and\nsynchronizing action between different muscle groups; (2) intramuscular coordination,\ncontrol within one muscle via number encoding (how many fibres are recruited), rate\nencoding (firing frequency), and pattern encoding (sequencing of fibre types); (3)\nfacilitatory/inhibitory reflex processes; (4) motor learning, programming the CNS to\nexecute a specific task. No training is purely structural; all training is functional to\nsome degree and may also elicit structural change. (PDF p. 18, printed p. 7.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors state the sequence (\"in the early stages of training or\n#### framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal\nchapter: \"1.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n# Table 1.1: Resistance Training Prescription by Goal (Strength, Power, Hypertrophy, Endurance)\n**What it is** — A dynamic (non-isometric) resistance-training prescription chart by\ngoal (PDF p. 22, printed p. 11):\n\n| Variable | Strength | Power | Hypertrophy | Endurance |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Load (% 1RM) | 80-100 | 70-100 | 60-80 | 40-60 |\n| Reps/set | 1-5 | 1-5 | 8-15 | 25-60 |\n| Sets/exercise | 4-7 | 3-5 | 4-8 | 2-4 |\n| Rest between sets | 2-6 min | 2-6 min | 2-5 min | 1-2 min |\n| Duration/set | 5-10 s | 4-8 s | 20-60 s | 80-150 s |\n| Speed/rep (% max) | 60-100 | 90-100 | 60-90 | 6-80 (as printed; probably a misprint for 60-80) |\n| Sessions/week | 3-6 | 3-6 | 5-7 | 8-14 |\n#### claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method\nchapter: \"4.2.6.3\"\nconfidence: supported\n# The Static-Dynamic Method: Pairing a Brief Isometric Hold With Explosive Concentric Work in One Repetition\n**What it is** — The static-dynamic method sequentially combines isometric and dynamic\n(auxotonic) muscular activity within one exercise. Two variants are given as experimentally\neffective: (1) a 2-3 second isometric contraction at 80% of maximum, immediately followed by\nexplosive concentric dynamic work with a 30%-of-maximum load; (2) a constant load of 75-80%\nof maximum used for both the isometric and dynamic components, illustrated with a half-squat\nexample: the athlete sinks into a half-squat with a barbell on the shoulders, holds that\nposition for 2 seconds, then jumps upward with maximal acceleration before landing carefully\nand repeating. Experimentally, variant (1) proved better than dynamic exercise alone for\ndeveloping speed-strength. For variant (2) the source reads: \"The second variant has an\nequivalent affect on the improvement of speed-strength and absolute strength (Dobrovolsky,\n1972, 1973).\" The comparator is unclear: this may mean an equal effect on both qualities,\nor an effect equivalent to dynamic exercise; the text does not say. (PDF p. 247, printed p.\n#### framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation\nchapter: \"5.2.4\"\nconfidence: supported\n# Eight \"Shock Methods\" for Breaking Long-Term Stagnation in Experienced Athletes, Used Only After Ruling Out Overtraining\n**What it is** — Under \"Various Shock Methods\" (PDF pp. 295-296 / printed pp. 284-285; p. 296 verified against the page image) the text widens \"shock\" beyond plyometrics: \"Training plyometrics may be regarded as one of several shock methods that may be used in special strength training to provide intense stimulation to compel the body to attain a higher level of functional adaptation.\" Because \"The principle of gradual progressive overloading may, after a prolonged period of unvaried use, sometimes fail to improve strength or performance, as is commonly the case among elite athletes\", it \"may be necessary to disrupt the persistent state of stagnation\" with one of:\n- plyometrics\n- forced repetitions\n- supramaximal methods\n- electrical stimulation (macrostimulation)\n- repeated single 1RM training\n- restricted range maxima\n- contrast methods\n- maximal eccentrics\n\nCautions: \"The appropriate method, its timing and the duration of its use must be chosen with great care, since lack of progress may be due to overtraining rather than long-term stagnation. The use of any shock method under such circumstances is potentially harmful and definitely detrimental to performance. These methods are generally unsuitable for novices and should only be employed by experienced athletes with a solid background of special strength training.\" The text notes \"shock method\" was Verkhoshansky's name for the plyometric method when he first formalised it.\n\n#### heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing\nchapter: \"5.6\"\nconfidence: supported\n# Order Circuit Stations by the Setchinov Phenomenon: Alternate Upper and Lower, Front and Back, Agonist and Antagonist\n**What it is** — \"The effectiveness of any circuit may be enhanced by taking cognizance of the Setchinov phenomenon, which encourages restoration by means of diverting activity performed between exercises. For example, this may entail exercise of upper body muscles alternating with exercise of unfatigued muscles of the legs. In applying this principle to circuit training, upper body stations should then alternately exercise upper and lower body muscles, back and front muscles, or agonistic and antagonistic muscles\" (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308, checked against the page image). The authors' layouts (Figs 5.25-5.28, verified against the page image, PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308; described in the text on PDF pp. 319-320): aerobic stations (AS) alternating one-for-one with resistance stations (RS) that alternate upper and lower body (Fig 5.25); one aerobic station followed by two resistance stations, \"again alternately exercising the upper and lower (or front/back, left/right, etc.) parts of the body\" (Fig 5.27); and one aerobic station followed by three resistance stations (Fig 5.28). Circuits aimed \"more on muscle endurance or strength, can begin with an aerobics station, then progress through a succession of resistance stations, also preferably sequenced in accordance with the Setchinov principle.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the one in the name: diverting activity between exercises \"encourages restoration\" of the muscles just worked (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308). They do not explain how. Inference: the Setchinov (Sechenov) active-rest effect is a classical observation that working other muscles during a rest period speeds recovery of the fatigued ones; the book cites no study for it here, and its size in modern studies is not checked in this entry.\n#### framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour\nchapter: \"5.6\"\nconfidence: untested\n# Circuit Layout Variants: A Two-Station Circuit Is a Superset, and Circuits Can Backtrack, Stagger, Detour, Pyramid or Strip\n**What it is** — The authors propose \"a systems approach adapted from applied mathematics\" to circuits. \"The traditional approach requires one to proceed from starting station to terminal station for a certain number of sets, without backtracking, omitting stations or diverting to extra equipment\"; \"The more generalised approach includes the possibility of backtracking, omitting stations, altering the sequence of stations, diverting to other equipment, using different speeds of movement or time periods on each station, changing the exercise pattern at each station, and changing the types of muscle contraction at each station\" (PDF p. 320 / printed p. 309, checked against the page image). The variants, each shown in a figure checked against the page images (PDF pp. 320-322 / printed pp. 309-311):\n- **Superset = two-station circuit.** \"a circuit with two stations is exactly the same as a superset with two exercises (Fig 5.29).\"\n- **Retrophase circuit.** \"backtrack by one station for every two stations completed\" (Fig 5.30); backtracking more \"may cause confusion if there is a large group.\"\n- **Dual-set retrophase and sequenced supersets.** \"complete two sets at each station before moving to the next station, then backtrack to complete the remaining two sets\" (Fig 5.31b); or superset A and B \"before going on to superset exercises C and D\" (Fig 5.31a).\n- **Staggered or zig-zag circuit.** Lets clients \"progress through portions of a circuit easily without obstructing others\"; some do the inner circuit, some the outer, advanced clients \"zigzag through the entire circuit,\" with aerobic stations in the middle of the floor (Fig 5.32).\n- **Detour circuit.** Clients \"exit at a particular point to perform a sub-circuit\" of free weights, aerobic, skill, flexibility, running, plyometric or water exercises elsewhere (Fig 5.33).\n- **Other variants** (PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311): repetitions to failure at each station; \"pyramid circuits\" in which load rises \"from 40-80% of 1RM\" and reps fall \"e.g. from 10 to 4\" with each lap; \"stripping circuits\" that pyramid up to \"a 6RM or 10RM on each station, then progressively strip the load down,\" generally \"about 10kg each time\"; and variable-repetition or variable-interval sets. Aqualand circuits mix land and water stations (Figs 5.21-5.23, PDF pp. 317-318).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated purposes: more variety \"thereby diminishing the likelihood of stagnation and boredom\" (PDF p. 320 / printed p. 309), less crowding at stations (staggered circuits), and access to equipment outside the circuit (detours). For water stations they give a physical reason: \"the resistance offered by water increases with the velocity of movement (actually, the square of the velocity)\" and \"is also directly proportional to the area of the limb pushing against the water\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image).\n#### framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric\nchapter: \"5.2.1\"\nconfidence: contested\n# Three Methods for Maximum Strength (Repetitive Effort, Brief Maximal Tension, Isometric) and When Each Is Used\n**What it is** — Maximum strength in this chapter is \"the maximal force that a person can develop voluntarily,\" shown \"under isometric conditions or in a very slow movement against a maximal load.\" There are \"two basic methods\": \"the method of repetitive effort and the method of brief maximal tension\" (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258), with isometrics discussed as a third option (PDF p. 271-272 / printed p. 260-261). The authors' summary (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261, verified against the page image):\n- **Repetitive effort**: lifting a weight that is raised as strength grows; \"appropriate in the initial stages of strength development and when strength and its speed of production are not important.\" Moderate weights \"(50-60% of 1RM)\" for \"8-12\" reps increase muscle mass; heavy weights \"(90-95% of 1RM)\" for \"3-6\" reps raise strength faster with a smaller mass gain. The effect grows with \"the magnitude of the load and the volume of work.\" The authors attribute its effect to proprioceptive feedback from slow lifting, strong cortex-to-muscle excitation and more motor units recruited (Kabat 1947; Hellebrant & Houtz 1956; PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258).\n- **Brief maximal tension**: \"heavy weights (85-95% of 1RM, i.e. 3-5RM)\" combined with lighter weights in the session and maximal singles \"once or twice a week\"; weightlifters \"execute 5-6 exercises for 6-10 sets of 1-3 repetitions in one training session (Medvedev, 1967; Vorobyev, 1971)\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). It raises maximum strength \"without appreciably increasing muscle mass,\" improves \"the ability to display strength quickly,\" suits athletes needing relative strength, and is used \"when the repetitive effort method has become ineffective,\" when strength must rise \"quickly in a relatively short period with a small volume of work,\" and to maintain strength and \"sporting form\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n- **Isometric tension (with gradual increase in effort)**: maximum strength without mass gain; to maintain strength \"where speed of movement is not important,\" in preparatory training, and \"during the early stages of musculoskeletal rehabilitation, when movement might be restricted.\" Isometric tension \"may be applied slowly or explosively (see 4.2.4)\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is neuromuscular specificity: \"Similar significant increases in maximum strength can be obtained\" by all three, \"However, the strength acquired from each of these methods has its own neuromuscular specificity\" (PDF p. 272 / printed p. 261). For brief maximal tension they say it \"develops the ability to concentrate neuromuscular effort\" and that lifting near-maximal weights \"enhances the ability to accelerate heavy loads\" (PDF p. 271 / printed p. 260). They also note that strength \"is retained for longer if it is founded first upon a base of increased muscle hypertrophy\" and declines faster if built mainly on neuromuscular efficiency (PDF p. 269 / printed p. 258); that retention point is atomized in [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]].\n#### heuristic--plan-variation-to-avoid-restrictive-neural-patterns\nchapter: \"1\"\nconfidence: contested\n# Restrictive, Repetitive Patterns May Overspecialize the Nervous System\n**What it is** — Drawing on neuroplasticity research (Rosenzweig, Diamond, Greenough,\ncortical reorganization in amputees and stroke patients), the text argues the nervous\nsystem rewires quickly (within minutes, not years) around whatever movement pattern is\nrepeated, and lists practical inferences: avoid machines that restrict joint movement to\none plane/pattern (they can reprogram the brain toward that restricted pattern and reduce\nsport-specific capability); avoid over-reliance on ergogenic devices (lifting belts, wrist\nwraps, shoe wedges) that can make efficient unassisted movement harder later; favor\nplanned variation of means, methods, and exercises over fixed repetition of the same\nweights or machines; respect individual movement style rather than forcing one stereotyped\ntechnique on every athlete. (PDF pp. 19-20, printed pp. 8-9.)\n\n**Why it works** — Inference: the underlying neuroscience (adult cortical plasticity, rapid\n#### framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy\nchapter: \"1.13\"\nconfidence: contested\n# Sarcomere Hypertrophy (Functional) vs. Sarcoplasmic Hypertrophy (Non-Functional)\n**What it is** — Two distinct types of muscle hypertrophy are described. Sarcomere\nhypertrophy: an increase in the size and number of sarcomeres within myofibrils; when\nadded in parallel (not just in series) this increases the muscle's cross-sectional density\nof contractile tissue and produces a real increase in strength. Sarcoplasmic hypertrophy:\nan increase in the volume of non-contractile protein and semifluid plasma between fibres;\ncross-sectional area increases, but the density of contractile tissue per unit area\ndecreases, with no corresponding strength increase. Except in sports where added bodymass\nitself matters (bodybuilding, sumo), sarcomere hypertrophy is described as far more\nimportant for athletic performance. Longer, more strenuous submaximal loading (not rapid,\nnear-maximal, low-repetition loading like Olympic weightlifting) is associated with more\nsarcoplasmic and less sarcomere hypertrophy; high-repetition, moderate-resistance training\n(bodybuilding/circuit-style) can also make fast-twitch fibres behave more like slow-twitch","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"bf268875-1779-4e16-ac2b-a9860bb6e456","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:45.854Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"#### framework--compensatory-acceleration-training\nchapter: \"1.2\"\nconfidence: supported\n# Compensatory (Acceleration/Action) Training: Using Proprioceptive Feedback to Actively Manage Bar Speed\n**What it is** — Free weights (unlike some variable-resistance machines) let the lifter\nfeel changing leverage through the range of motion via proprioceptive feedback and respond\nby voluntarily accelerating or decelerating the bar to raise or lower the force produced.\nThis is termed Compensatory Acceleration Training (CAT), most obviously used\nconcentrically, but the text argues it applies just as much eccentrically (muscles\nactively resist gravity's acceleration to control the descent) and even isometrically\n(the lifter must continuously compensate for fatigue-driven declines in neural drive to\nhold a constant position), leading the author to prefer \"Compensatory Action Training\" as\nthe fuller name. (PDF p. 32, printed p. 21; Ch 7.1 develops it further.)\n\n**Why it works** — Because force is not fixed by the weight alone (F = ma; a lighter bar\n#### framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training\nchapter: \"1\"\nconfidence: supported\n# Functional vs. Structural Resistance Training\n**What it is** — Two basic categories of resistance training effect: structural training\ntargets muscle hypertrophy (and some tissue flexibility); functional training targets\nperformance qualities (static strength, speed-strength, muscular endurance, reactive\nability) through four neural mechanisms: (1) intermuscular coordination, sequencing and\nsynchronizing action between different muscle groups; (2) intramuscular coordination,\ncontrol within one muscle via number encoding (how many fibres are recruited), rate\nencoding (firing frequency), and pattern encoding (sequencing of fibre types); (3)\nfacilitatory/inhibitory reflex processes; (4) motor learning, programming the CNS to\nexecute a specific task. No training is purely structural; all training is functional to\nsome degree and may also elicit structural change. (PDF p. 18, printed p. 7.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors state the sequence (\"in the early stages of training or\n#### framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal\nchapter: \"1.1\"\nconfidence: supported\n# Table 1.1: Resistance Training Prescription by Goal (Strength, Power, Hypertrophy, Endurance)\n**What it is** — A dynamic (non-isometric) resistance-training prescription chart by\ngoal (PDF p. 22, printed p. 11):\n\n| Variable | Strength | Power | Hypertrophy | Endurance |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Load (% 1RM) |