:{"toolu_01RVuSxpfiQxVjKDwrqDSLr7":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/ && for f in framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters heuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing; do echo \"#### $f\"; grep -m1 '^chapter' $f.md; grep -m1 '^# ' $f.md; sed -n '/What it is/,/Why it works/p' $f.md | head -8 | cut -c1-600; done; grep -n -i \"pre-exhaust\" framework--methods-for-improving-voluntary-muscle-relaxation.md | cut -c1-400; grep -n -i \"bodybuild\" claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load.md principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods.md | cut -c1-400","description":"Skim more overlap candidates"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffpN4VBKHt91rA9siT8i","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"765a631d-def3-40c3-b2f4-3c3081973d7b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:59:15.309Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"765a631d-def3-40c3-b2f4-3c3081973d7b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RVuSxpfiQxVjKDwrqDSLr7","type":"tool_result","content":"#### framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters\nchapter: \"5.6\"\n# Aerobic vs Anaerobic Circuits: Rough Guidelines for Reps, Load, Rest and Laps\n**What it is** — The authors give \"rough guidelines,\" warning that \"a considerable degree of individuality is involved and no precise figures can be quoted to be universally appropriate for everyone\" (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304, checked against the page image). Cardiovascular circuits \"all fall under the category of CCT,\" and \"A true aerobic circuit would involve the predominant use of very low resistance devices or prolonged rhythmic movements\"; anaerobic circuits \"may be either CCT (continuous) or ICT (interval), with the former resulting largely in muscle endurance, and variations of the l\n\n| | Cardiovascular (aerobic) circuits | Anaerobic circuits |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rest | \"Continuous activity or very brief intervals\" between stations | \"Rest intervals between stations to permit some recovery (1-4 minutes)\" |\n| Station mix | aerobic activity alternating \"between resistance stations\" | \"Fewer aerobic stations and more resistance stations\" |\n| Reps per resistance station | \"20-40\" | \"3-8\" |\n| Load | \"usually not more than 30% of 1RM\" | \"usually more than 70% of 1RM\" |\n#### heuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength\nchapter: \"3.5.8\"\n# Eccentric Quasi-Isometrics: Loading a Joint Slowly Past Its Static Flexibility Limit to Build Active Range\n**What it is** — Quasi-isometric activity (defined generally in\n[[framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology]]) is presented here specifically as a\nmethod for developing active flexibility: exercising a limb through a full ROM against a\nresistance allowed to stretch the joint gently beyond its limit of static flexibility,\nwithout sudden movement that would recruit the myotatic (stretch) reflex. Free weights,\npulleys, and functional training machines are named as versatile tools for this, executing\nnatural resistance patterns. Eccentric quasi-isometrics specifically use the load to force\nthe joint to move slowly and eccentrically beyond its static flexibility limit; this does\n#### claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff\nchapter: \"4.2.5\"\n# Eccentric Training Damages Tissue and Causes Soreness at First, but Adaptation Removes Both Within 2-3 Weeks While Eccentric Work Capacity Rises\n**What it is** — Because the muscle complex lengthens under eccentric contraction, its series\nelastic component (SEC) is exposed to greater strain than under concentric conditions,\nraising injury potential; most muscle strains and ruptures occur during the eccentric phase\nof movement (Garrett, 1986). A single session of intense eccentric exercise commonly causes\npronounced delayed-onset muscle soreness (DOMS), peaking 24-72 hours after exercise and\nresolving within several days (Friden et al, 1983a), accompanied by reduced dynamic strength\nand damage to myofibrils and connective-tissue elements such as the Z bands (a SEC\ncomponent). Friden et al (1983b) also examined longer eccentric-training exposure (3\n#### framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing\nchapter: \"3.4.2\"\n# Post-Tetanic Potentiation (the Book's \"After-Effect\"): Optimal Load, Timing Window, and Sequencing Rules\n**What it is** — When a muscle is stimulated by a series of impulses, its subsequent\nactivity is measurably altered, a phenomenon that, \"in physiology,\" is called post-tetanic\npotentiation, produced by a preceding maximal/near-tetanic stimulus. The Russian-literature\nfindings the text emphasizes: **sequencing matters**: preliminary isometric tension\nfollowed by dynamic work improves the dynamic work (up to 20% effectiveness increase\ndespite fatigue), but with the *reverse* sequence (dynamic then isometric), \"results\ndeteriorate\" (printed 164). The after-effect \"occurs immediately after preliminary\nisometric tension. The first dynamic contraction still retains some trace of inhibitory\n35:phases of movement to enhance mobility, stability, and relaxation; (8) the \"pre-exhaustion\nprinciple--equal-force-different-loading-methods.md:19:(bodybuilding-style hypertrophy training, roughly 8-12 reps), or near-zero acceleration\nprinciple--equal-force-different-loading-methods.md:27:weightlifting, bodybuilding; printed 22), not on a stated mechanism. Inference: force alone\nclaim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load.md:16:**What it is** — Generations of bodybuilding-magazine authors have advocated specific","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"6ccd240c-8bca-4dd5-834c-64143b56b6a8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:59:15.571Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"#### framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters\nchapter: \"5.6\"\n# Aerobic vs Anaerobic Circuits: Rough Guidelines for Reps, Load, Rest and Laps\n**What it is** — The authors give \"rough guidelines,\" warning that \"a considerable degree of individuality is involved and no precise figures can be quoted to be universally appropriate for everyone\" (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304, checked against the page image). Cardiovascular circuits \"all fall under the category of CCT,\" and \"A true aerobic circuit would involve the predominant use of very low resistance devices or prolonged rhythmic movements\"; anaerobic circuits \"may be either CCT (continuous) or ICT (interval), with the former resulting largely in muscle endurance, and variations of the l\n\n| | Cardiovascular (aerobic) circuits | Anaerobic circuits |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rest | \"Continuous activity or very brief intervals\" between stations | \"Rest intervals between stations to permit some recovery (1-4 minutes)\" |\n| Station mix | aerobic activity alternating \"between resistance stations\" | \"Fewer aerobic stations and more resistance stations\" |\n| Reps per resistance station | \"20-40\" | \"3-8\" |\n| Load | \"usually not more than 30% of 1RM\" | \"usually more than 70% of 1RM\" |\n#### heuristic--eccentric-quasi-isometrics-for-active-flexibility-and-starting-strength\nchapter: \"3.5.8\"\n# Eccentric Quasi-Isometrics: Loading a Joint Slowly Past Its Static Flexibility Limit to Build Active Range\n**What it is** — Quasi-isometric activity (defined generally in\n[[framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology]]) is presented here specifically as a\nmethod for developing active flexibility: exercising a limb through a full ROM against a\nresistance allowed to stretch the joint gently beyond its limit of static flexibility,\nwithout sudden movement that would recruit the myotatic (stretch) reflex. Free weights,\npulleys, and functional training machines are named as versatile tools for this, executing\nnatural resistance patterns. Eccentric quasi-isometrics specifically use the load to force\nthe joint to move slowly and eccentrically beyond its static flexibility limit; this does\n#### claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff\nchapter: \"4.2.5\"\n# Eccentric Training Damages Tissue and Causes Soreness at First, but Adaptation Removes Both Within 2-3 Weeks While Eccentric Work Capacity Rises\n**What it is** — Because the muscle complex lengthens under eccentric contraction, its series\nelastic component (SEC) is exposed to greater strain than under concentric conditions,\nraising injury potential; most muscle strains and ruptures occur during the eccentric phase\nof movement (Garrett, 1986). A single session of intense eccentric exercise commonly causes\npronounced delayed-onset muscle soreness (DOMS), peaking 24-72 hours after exercise and\nresolving within several days (Friden et al, 1983a), accompanied by reduced dynamic strength\nand damage to myofibrils and connective-tissue elements such as the Z bands (a SEC\ncomponent). Friden et al (1983b) also examined longer eccentric-training exposure (3\n#### framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing\nchapter: \"3.4.2\"\n# Post-Tetanic Potentiation (the Book's \"After-Effect\"): Optimal Load, Timing Window, and Sequencing Rules\n**What it is** — When a muscle is stimulated by a series of impulses, its subsequent\nactivity is measurably altered, a phenomenon that, \"in physiology,\" is called post-tetanic\npotentiation, produced by a preceding maximal/near-tetanic stimulus. The Russian-literature\nfindings the text emphasizes: **sequencing matters**: preliminary isometric tension\nfollowed by dynamic work improves the dynamic work (up to 20% effectiveness increase\ndespite fatigue), but with the *reverse* sequence (dynamic then isometric), \"results\ndeteriorate\" (printed 164). The after-effect \"occurs immediately after preliminary\nisometric tension. The first dynamic contraction still retains some trace of inhibitory\n35:phases of movement to enhance mobility, stability, and relaxation; (8) the \"pre-exhaustion\nprinciple--equal-force-different-loading-methods.md:19:(bodybuilding-style hypertrophy training, roughly 8-12 reps), or near-zero acceleration\nprinciple--equal-force-different-loading-methods.md:27:weightlifting, bodybuilding; printed 22), not on a stated mechanism. Inference: force alone\nclaim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load.md:16:**What it is** — Generations of bodybuilding-magazine authors have advocated specific","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"765a631d-def3-40c3-b2f4-3c3081973d7b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
