{"parentUuid":"d1d003f5-f483-4bbf-91d9-e9422ba92d62","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpfResjcm3qFN76KRL7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Writing entries now."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":768,"cache_read_input_tokens":220514,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":768,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffpfD36MVfweGdFGVd3B","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"6be8192d-b375-41d9-b6e9-afec11bfa017","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:04:00.590Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"6be8192d-b375-41d9-b6e9-afec11bfa017","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpfResjcm3qFN76KRL7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FruRj2VTrhn4yerfaBvdQJ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7\"\ntopic: classifying strength training methods\ncategory: [strength, programming, methods, classification]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Strength Training Methods Sorted by Structural or Functional Aim (Figs 7.1 and 7.2)\n\n**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 7 by restating that \"all strength training depends on two dominant factors ... namely structural conditioning and functional (central nervous and neuromuscular) conditioning\", so training \"may be categorised according to its major structural and functional aims relative to the importance of nervous system training methods (Fig 7.1)\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). Fig 7.1 (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394, read from the page image) splits Strength Training into Structural (Connective Tissue Hypertrophy and Strength; Muscle Hypertrophy) and Functional (Muscle Endurance; Maximal Strength; Power), with an arrow under the five boxes labelled \"Increasing importance of nervous system training\", running from connective tissue on the left to power on the right. The caption says power \"refers to the qualities of strength-speed and speed-strength\".\n\nFig 7.2 (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395, read from the page image) is a four-column chart: Fitness Quality, Category of Method, Methods, Variations. The authors chose a \"loosely interconnected array of columns and rows, showing only some of the more obvious relationships\" because a single flowchart would be \"logically tedious, unattractive and largely unusable\" given the overlap between methods (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). What the image shows:\n- Fitness qualities: explosive strength, maximal strength, strength-speed, speed-strength, strength-endurance, speed-endurance, flexibility, hypertrophy. Only three carry a drawn connector: explosive strength to Reactive Methods, flexibility to a box of static, passive, active, ballistic and PNF, and hypertrophy to Bodybuilding Methods. The other five quality boxes have no drawn link.\n- Categories: reactive, supramaximal, maximal, circa-maximal, submaximal.\n- Methods with their variations: Plyometrics; Contrast Methods (load release, heavy-light set, stripping, pneumatics, static-dynamic); supramaximal variations (forced reps, restricted range, eccentrics, cheating, ballistics); Weightlifting Method (single rep sets, few rep sets); Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics; an Oxford Method box with no drawn link; Endurance Methods; Pyramiding (load pyramiding, rest pyramiding, stripping, reverse pyramid, APRE, DeLorme method); Bodybuilding Methods (plateau, step, multi-sets, supersets, split routine, continuous sets, progressive segments, pre-exhaustion, reps to failure, alternating sets, miscellaneous); Circuit Training (interval, continuous); Multi-purpose Training (isokinetics, loadless training, zone training, hybrids, interval sets, cluster training).\n\nStripping appears twice (under Contrast Methods and under Pyramiding) and APRE appears twice (under Isometrics and under Pyramiding). The authors give the reason: \"some of the training methods may be used to achieve several different aims by altering variables such as the load, number of repetitions and rest intervals\"; pyramiding \"should not be regarded merely as a bodybuilding method\" because it can serve hypertrophy, strength and endurance \"depending on the load and number of repetitions (which are inversely related)\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394). Likewise cheating and restricted range repetitions, \"although classified as supramaximal methods, may also be used submaximally or circamaximally\", and it is \"the role of the coach to choose a given method according to the intended training goal\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394).\n\n**Choosing between hypertrophy and nervous system work.** On the same page the authors say the choice \"may be based on performance in tests of explosive strength and speed-strength (e.g. vertical jump), as well as on the magnitude of the strength deficit discussed early in Chapter 1. If this deficit at any stage is large, then it would be appropriate to concentrate more on weightlifting and reactive methods, whereas, if the deficit is small, submaximal bodybuilding methods should be relied upon\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394). This is the direction of Chapter 1's \"In general\" rule (\"if the strength deficit is large ... weightlifting or plyometric methods\"; \"If the strength deficit is small, hypertrophy must be induced by submaximal loading methods\", PDF p. 21 / printed p. 10). The jump-test paragraph on that same Chapter 1 page pairs a large deficit with \"strength and hypertrophy training with 5RM to 8RM ... loads\" instead (PDF p. 21 / printed p. 10). Chapter 7 names jump performance as a basis but gives a direction only for the deficit, so it does not settle which reading applies to a jump-test deficit. The rule and its internal conflict are rated `contested` in [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]].\n\nThe authors also point to Table 1.1, \"based largely on the practical experience of generations of bodybuilders and weightlifters\", as \"an initial guide\" read alongside Fig 7.1 (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394; see [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' basis is the two-factor view of strength (structural and functional) stressed \"throughout this text\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393), set out in Chapter 1 ([[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]]). They give no evidence for the ordering of the five aims along the nervous-system arrow. Inference: the arrow is a teaching device that places each aim by how much of its gain the authors attribute to neural change; it is not a measured scale.\n\n**When to apply** — As a menu when building a block: start from the fitness quality the block targets, then pick a category and method, and set load, repetitions and rest to fit the aim, since the same method can serve several aims (PDF pp. 404-405). Eric's baseline (line 103): \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky) ... governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**. Inference: the chart picks a method for a quality; dynamic correspondence still decides which exercise the method is applied to. Eric's baseline (line 76) uses the dynamic strength index \"to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)**, tagged in the baseline as contested on the evidence. Inference: that is the same kind of decision the authors make from a jump test and the strength deficit, made with a different measure.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not read Fig 7.2 as a decision tree. The authors say it shows \"only some of the more obvious relationships\" (PDF p. 404), and five of its eight quality boxes have no drawn link to any method. Do not read the method headings as fixed: a method's aim moves with its load, repetitions and rest (PDF pp. 404-405). The authors' concluding remark for 7.1 also warns that \"no methods operate in isolation of one another; each method has immediate and delayed training effects which have significant effects on other methods that follow hours, days or even weeks later\" (PDF p. 414 / printed p. 403), so a method chosen from the chart still has to be placed in a sequence. Do not use the hypertrophy-or-nervous-system rule on a jump-test deficit without reading the conflict recorded in [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example: pyramiding is placed in its own row because, by changing the load and number of repetitions, it can be used \"in the earlier preparatory stages to meet several general training aims such as increased muscle hypertrophy, strength and endurance\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]] (Chapter 1 source of the structural and functional split); [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]] (the deficit rule; Chapter 7 restates its \"In general\" direction); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Table 1.1); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]; [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]]; [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]]; [[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]] (why APRE sits under Isometrics)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":768,"cache_read_input_tokens":220514,"output_tokens":9636,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":6376},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":768},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":9636,"cache_read_input_tokens":220514,"cache_creation_input_tokens":768,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":768,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FruRj2VTrhn4yerfaBvdQJ":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7\"\ntopic: classifying strength training methods\ncategory: [strength, programming, methods, classification]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Strength Training Methods Sorted by Structural or Functional Aim (Figs 7.1 and 7.2)\n\n**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 7 by restating that \"all strength training depends on two dominant factors ... namely structural conditioning and functional (central nervous and neuromuscular) conditioning\", so training \"may be categorised according to its major structural and functional aims relative to the importance of nervous system training methods (Fig 7.1)\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). Fig 7.1 (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394, read from the page image) splits Strength Training into Structural (Connective Tissue Hypertrophy and Strength; Muscle Hypertrophy) and Functional (Muscle Endurance; Maximal Strength; Power), with an arrow under the five boxes labelled \"Increasing importance of nervous system training\", running from connective tissue on the left to power on the right. The caption says power \"refers to the qualities of strength-speed and speed-strength\".\n\nFig 7.2 (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395, read from the page image) is a four-column chart: Fitness Quality, Category of Method, Methods, Variations. The authors chose a \"loosely interconnected array of columns and rows, showing only some of the more obvious relationships\" because a single flowchart would be \"logically tedious, unattractive and largely unusable\" given the overlap between methods (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). What the image shows:\n- Fitness qualities: explosive strength, maximal strength, strength-speed, speed-strength, strength-endurance, speed-endurance, flexibility, hypertrophy. Only three carry a drawn connector: explosive strength to Reactive Methods, flexibility to a box of static, passive, active, ballistic and PNF, and hypertrophy to Bodybuilding Methods. The other five quality boxes have no drawn link.\n- Categories: reactive, supramaximal, maximal, circa-maximal, submaximal.\n- Methods with their variations: Plyometrics; Contrast Methods (load release, heavy-light set, stripping, pneumatics, static-dynamic); supramaximal variations (forced reps, restricted range, eccentrics, cheating, ballistics); Weightlifting Method (single rep sets, few rep sets); Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics; an Oxford Method box with no drawn link; Endurance Methods; Pyramiding (load pyramiding, rest pyramiding, stripping, reverse pyramid, APRE, DeLorme method); Bodybuilding Methods (plateau, step, multi-sets, supersets, split routine, continuous sets, progressive segments, pre-exhaustion, reps to failure, alternating sets, miscellaneous); Circuit Training (interval, continuous); Multi-purpose Training (isokinetics, loadless training, zone training, hybrids, interval sets, cluster training).\n\nStripping appears twice (under Contrast Methods and under Pyramiding) and APRE appears twice (under Isometrics and under Pyramiding). The authors give the reason: \"some of the training methods may be used to achieve several different aims by altering variables such as the load, number of repetitions and rest intervals\"; pyramiding \"should not be regarded merely as a bodybuilding method\" because it can serve hypertrophy, strength and endurance \"depending on the load and number of repetitions (which are inversely related)\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394). Likewise cheating and restricted range repetitions, \"although classified as supramaximal methods, may also be used submaximally or circamaximally\", and it is \"the role of the coach to choose a given method according to the intended training goal\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394).\n\n**Choosing between hypertrophy and nervous system work.** On the same page the authors say the choice \"may be based on performance in tests of explosive strength and speed-strength (e.g. vertical jump), as well as on the magnitude of the strength deficit discussed early in Chapter 1. If this deficit at any stage is large, then it would be appropriate to concentrate more on weightlifting and reactive methods, whereas, if the deficit is small, submaximal bodybuilding methods should be relied upon\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394). This is the direction of Chapter 1's \"In general\" rule (\"if the strength deficit is large ... weightlifting or plyometric methods\"; \"If the strength deficit is small, hypertrophy must be induced by submaximal loading methods\", PDF p. 21 / printed p. 10). The jump-test paragraph on that same Chapter 1 page pairs a large deficit with \"strength and hypertrophy training with 5RM to 8RM ... loads\" instead (PDF p. 21 / printed p. 10). Chapter 7 names jump performance as a basis but gives a direction only for the deficit, so it does not settle which reading applies to a jump-test deficit. The rule and its internal conflict are rated `contested` in [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]].\n\nThe authors also point to Table 1.1, \"based largely on the practical experience of generations of bodybuilders and weightlifters\", as \"an initial guide\" read alongside Fig 7.1 (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394; see [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' basis is the two-factor view of strength (structural and functional) stressed \"throughout this text\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393), set out in Chapter 1 ([[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]]). They give no evidence for the ordering of the five aims along the nervous-system arrow. Inference: the arrow is a teaching device that places each aim by how much of its gain the authors attribute to neural change; it is not a measured scale.\n\n**When to apply** — As a menu when building a block: start from the fitness quality the block targets, then pick a category and method, and set load, repetitions and rest to fit the aim, since the same method can serve several aims (PDF pp. 404-405). Eric's baseline (line 103): \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky) ... governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**. Inference: the chart picks a method for a quality; dynamic correspondence still decides which exercise the method is applied to. Eric's baseline (line 76) uses the dynamic strength index \"to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)**, tagged in the baseline as contested on the evidence. Inference: that is the same kind of decision the authors make from a jump test and the strength deficit, made with a different measure.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not read Fig 7.2 as a decision tree. The authors say it shows \"only some of the more obvious relationships\" (PDF p. 404), and five of its eight quality boxes have no drawn link to any method. Do not read the method headings as fixed: a method's aim moves with its load, repetitions and rest (PDF pp. 404-405). The authors' concluding remark for 7.1 also warns that \"no methods operate in isolation of one another; each method has immediate and delayed training effects which have significant effects on other methods that follow hours, days or even weeks later\" (PDF p. 414 / printed p. 403), so a method chosen from the chart still has to be placed in a sequence. Do not use the hypertrophy-or-nervous-system rule on a jump-test deficit without reading the conflict recorded in [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example: pyramiding is placed in its own row because, by changing the load and number of repetitions, it can be used \"in the earlier preparatory stages to meet several general training aims such as increased muscle hypertrophy, strength and endurance\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]] (Chapter 1 source of the structural and functional split); [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]] (the deficit rule; Chapter 7 restates its \"In general\" direction); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Table 1.1); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]; [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]]; [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]]; [[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]] (why APRE sits under Isometrics)\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffpfD36MVfweGdFGVd3B","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"bff68801-b16c-44b0-9a37-85ab748b5d6a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:04:26.436Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"bff68801-b16c-44b0-9a37-85ab748b5d6a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a2b72ded-2f77-4788-be97-76a7696d93d6","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FruRj2VTrhn4yerfaBvdQJ","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"51714ca0-69bf-40fa-b33f-90acc99bfe8e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:04:26.549Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7\"\ntopic: classifying strength training methods\ncategory: [strength, programming, methods, classification]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Strength Training Methods Sorted by Structural or Functional Aim (Figs 7.1 and 7.2)\n\n**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 7 by restating that \"all strength training depends on two dominant factors ... namely structural conditioning and functional (central nervous and neuromuscular) conditioning\", so training \"may be categorised according to its major structural and functional aims relative to the importance of nervous system training methods (Fig 7.1)\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). Fig 7.1 (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394, read from the page image) splits Strength Training into Structural (Connective Tissue Hypertrophy and Strength; Muscle Hypertrophy) and Functional (Muscle Endurance; Maximal Strength; Power), with an arrow under the five boxes labelled \"Increasing importance of nervous system training\", running from connective tissue on the left to power on the right. The caption says power \"refers to the qualities of strength-speed and speed-strength\".\n\nFig 7.2 (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395, read from the page image) is a four-column chart: Fitness Quality, Category of Method, Methods, Variations. The authors chose a \"loosely interconnected array of columns and rows, showing only some of the more obvious relationships\" because a single flowchart would be \"logically tedious, unattractive and largely unusable\" given the overlap between methods (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). What the image shows:\n- Fitness qualities: explosive strength, maximal strength, strength-speed, speed-strength, strength-endurance, speed-endurance, flexibility, hypertrophy. Only three carry a drawn connector: explosive strength to Reactive Methods, flexibility to a box of static, passive, active, ballistic and PNF, and hypertrophy to Bodybuilding Methods. The other five quality boxes have no drawn link.\n- Categories: reactive, supramaximal, maximal, circa-maximal, submaximal.\n- Methods with their variations: Plyometrics; Contrast Methods (load release, heavy-light set, stripping, pneumatics, static-dynamic); supramaximal variations (forced reps, restricted range, eccentrics, cheating, ballistics); Weightlifting Method (single rep sets, few rep sets); Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics; an Oxford Method box with no drawn link; Endurance Methods; Pyramiding (load pyramiding, rest pyramiding, stripping, reverse pyramid, APRE, DeLorme method); Bodybuilding Methods (plateau, step, multi-sets, supersets, split routine, continuous sets, progressive segments, pre-exhaustion, reps to failure, alternating sets, miscellaneous); Circuit Training (interval, continuous); Multi-purpose Training (isokinetics, loadless training, zone training, hybrids, interval sets, cluster training).\n\nStripping appears twice (under Contrast Methods and under Pyramiding) and APRE appears twice (under Isometrics and under Pyramiding). The authors give the reason: \"some of the training methods may be used to achieve several different aims by altering variables such as the load, number of repetitions and rest intervals\"; pyramiding \"should not be regarded merely as a bodybuilding method\" because it can serve hypertrophy, strength and endurance \"depending on the load and number of repetitions (which are inversely related)\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394). Likewise cheating and restricted range repetitions, \"although classified as supramaximal methods, may also be used submaximally or circamaximally\", and it is \"the role of the coach to choose a given method according to the intended training goal\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394).\n\n**Choosing between hypertrophy and nervous system work.** On the same page the authors say the choice \"may be based on performance in tests of explosive strength and speed-strength (e.g. vertical jump), as well as on the magnitude of the strength deficit discussed early in Chapter 1. If this deficit at any stage is large, then it would be appropriate to concentrate more on weightlifting and reactive methods, whereas, if the deficit is small, submaximal bodybuilding methods should be relied upon\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394). This is the direction of Chapter 1's \"In general\" rule (\"if the strength deficit is large ... weightlifting or plyometric methods\"; \"If the strength deficit is small, hypertrophy must be induced by submaximal loading methods\", PDF p. 21 / printed p. 10). The jump-test paragraph on that same Chapter 1 page pairs a large deficit with \"strength and hypertrophy training with 5RM to 8RM ... loads\" instead (PDF p. 21 / printed p. 10). Chapter 7 names jump performance as a basis but gives a direction only for the deficit, so it does not settle which reading applies to a jump-test deficit. The rule and its internal conflict are rated `contested` in [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]].\n\nThe authors also point to Table 1.1, \"based largely on the practical experience of generations of bodybuilders and weightlifters\", as \"an initial guide\" read alongside Fig 7.1 (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394; see [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' basis is the two-factor view of strength (structural and functional) stressed \"throughout this text\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393), set out in Chapter 1 ([[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]]). They give no evidence for the ordering of the five aims along the nervous-system arrow. Inference: the arrow is a teaching device that places each aim by how much of its gain the authors attribute to neural change; it is not a measured scale.\n\n**When to apply** — As a menu when building a block: start from the fitness quality the block targets, then pick a category and method, and set load, repetitions and rest to fit the aim, since the same method can serve several aims (PDF pp. 404-405). Eric's baseline (line 103): \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky) ... governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**. Inference: the chart picks a method for a quality; dynamic correspondence still decides which exercise the method is applied to. Eric's baseline (line 76) uses the dynamic strength index \"to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)**, tagged in the baseline as contested on the evidence. Inference: that is the same kind of decision the authors make from a jump test and the strength deficit, made with a different measure.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not read Fig 7.2 as a decision tree. The authors say it shows \"only some of the more obvious relationships\" (PDF p. 404), and five of its eight quality boxes have no drawn link to any method. Do not read the method headings as fixed: a method's aim moves with its load, repetitions and rest (PDF pp. 404-405). The authors' concluding remark for 7.1 also warns that \"no methods operate in isolation of one another; each method has immediate and delayed training effects which have significant effects on other methods that follow hours, days or even weeks later\" (PDF p. 414 / printed p. 403), so a method chosen from the chart still has to be placed in a sequence. Do not use the hypertrophy-or-nervous-system rule on a jump-test deficit without reading the conflict recorded in [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example: pyramiding is placed in its own row because, by changing the load and number of repetitions, it can be used \"in the earlier preparatory stages to meet several general training aims such as increased muscle hypertrophy, strength and endurance\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]] (Chapter 1 source of the structural and functional split); [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]] (the deficit rule; Chapter 7 restates its \"In general\" direction); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Table 1.1); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]; [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]]; [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]]; [[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]] (why APRE sits under Isometrics)\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bff68801-b16c-44b0-9a37-85ab748b5d6a","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"51714ca0-69bf-40fa-b33f-90acc99bfe8e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14769080 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"41e00f83-d276-4b9d-83d0-656896968b19","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:04:26.562Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14769080 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"41e00f83-d276-4b9d-83d0-656896968b19","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffpn3dFqpAnYDFgZYmrh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9767,"cache_read_input_tokens":221282,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9767,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":377,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffpmgm8QsYzCoLBwdAxH","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"48f5189f-a15d-45db-8ec3-f3fa15c2a70b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:04:33.026Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"48f5189f-a15d-45db-8ec3-f3fa15c2a70b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffpn3dFqpAnYDFgZYmrh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FmVPSRvGBA4jn4cLsewmVS","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: methods by load category\ncategory: [strength, methods, maximal-strength, loading]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Maximal, Supramaximal, Circa-maximal and Submaximal Methods: Definitions, Loads and Rests\n\n**What it is** — Section 7.1 defines four load categories of resistance method (PDF pp. 406-407 / printed pp. 395-396, checked against the page images):\n\n**Maximal methods**\n- *Weightlifting method:* \"single repetition explosive sets or low repetition sets (2-3 reps), in which each repetition is separated from the next by a short rest period (1-5 minutes, depending on how close the load is to a 1RM)\", also called \"the *interval set* method\" (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395). Bodybuilders adopted it as the *rest-pause method*, \"sets of 1RM, but with rest periods of 10-20 seconds\"; the authors say the shorter rest \"is possible, since bodybuilding movements do not require the high levels of skill, speed and coordination of the Olympic lifts\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n- *Maximal or circa-maximal concentrics:* the concentric phase of a 1RM or near-1RM lift is completed and the bar is dropped (bumper plates) \"so as to avoid executing the depleting eccentric lowering phase\"; in bodybuilding \"a partner usually assists in the lowering\" (PDF p. 407).\n- *Maximal isometrics:* slow or explosive isometrics \"in single or few repetition sets lasting about 5 seconds with rest intervals of 2-3 minutes between sets\" (PDF p. 407).\n- *Maximal eccentrics:* a load \"about 30-40% greater than one's maximum concentric lift\" taken from racks or raised by a partner and lowered under control (PDF p. 407).\n\n**Supramaximal methods** (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396)\n- *Forced repetitions:* to failure, then \"a few more repetitions with the aid of a training partner, who provides the minimal assistance necessary\" for the concentric phase; the eccentric phase \"is carried out without help\".\n- *Restricted range repetitions:* a load heavier than the full-range maximum, moved through \"a few small range concentric-eccentric repetitions near his strongest region of action\"; done at the end of \"a normal 5-8 repetition\" set to the point of \"burn\", these are \"*burning reps*\". Weightlifters and powerlifters use partial ranges \"to rectify any deficit of strength in that region\", for example pulls \"around knee-to-lower thigh level\" and squats or bench presses at the \"sticking point\", set up in power racks.\n- *Cheating:* momentum, bouncing, swaying or accessory muscles, used above 1RM or at the end of a heavy set to failure; \"There are recommended safe ways of cheating and these must be followed religiously if injury is to be avoided\" (the safe ways are not described).\n- *Ballistics:* \"lowering or dropping a weight rapidly to recruit the myotatic stretch reflex or exploit the stored elastic energy\"; \"the training load is not necessarily supramaximal; rather, it is the resulting muscle tension that reaches this level of intensity.\"\n\n**Circa-maximal methods:** loads \"characteristically about 90-97.5% of 1RM\"; the difference from maximal methods is that circa-maximal work \"may be performed with several successive repetitions without a prolonged rest interval\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n\n**Submaximal methods:** \"loads of about 60-80% of one's 1RM to the point of failure on the last set\", \"mainly for bodybuilding reasons such as hypertrophy or muscle definition\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n\n**Classification inconsistency (not a contradiction).** Maximal eccentrics are described under Maximal Methods in the text, which adds that \"Often the eccentric method is regarded as one of the 'supramaximal' methods\" (PDF p. 407); Fig 7.2 lists eccentrics under supramaximal (PDF p. 406, image). The method is the same; only its heading differs.\n\n**Why `contested`: rest between near-maximal singles.** The weightlifting method gives \"1-5 minutes\" between single repetitions (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395). Two pages later the authors say \"much weightlifting and powerlifting training relies on a specific form of interval resistance training, namely the use of single repetitions of near-maximal weights with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398, image). Both sentences describe the rest between near-maximal singles in weightlifting training, and the book does not reconcile them. Inference: the \"few seconds\" version is closer to the bodybuilders' rest-pause (10-20 s) or to intensive clustering (about 20 s, see [[framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets]]) than to the weightlifting method as first defined.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give mechanisms for only some methods: dropping the bar avoids the \"depleting\" eccentric phase; rest-pause can use shorter rests because bodybuilding movements need less skill, speed and coordination; ballistics recruit \"the myotatic stretch reflex\" or \"stored elastic energy\"; restricted range work targets \"any deficit of strength in that region\" (PDF pp. 406-407). For eccentrics they give risk, not mechanism: the method \"carries an increased risk of injury\", is \"associated with greater muscle soreness than most other methods and should not be used too frequently\", and its dangers \"can be reduced by selecting the load carefully and avoiding sudden changes in movement velocity or trajectory\" (PDF p. 407). The damage and adaptation evidence is atomized in [[claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff]].\n\n**When to apply** — As working definitions when writing or reading a programme: the category names tie a method to a load band (circa-maximal about 90-97.5%, submaximal about 60-80% of 1RM) and a rest pattern. Restricted range work fits a lift that fails at one region of the range (the authors' sticking-point use). Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**. Inference: every %1RM band here then rests on a projected max, and the gap between 90% and 97.5% is smaller than the likely error of a projection, so the circa-maximal band is better set by repetitions or bar speed than by a percentage of a projected max. Eric's baseline (line 107) keeps %1RM prescription \"on that explicit basis\" (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for novices on the supramaximal side: the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods (PDF p. 407), and Chapter 5 says shock methods of this kind \"are generally unsuitable for novices\" ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]). Do not use cheating on the authors' word alone: they say safe ways exist but do not give them. Do not treat the rest figures for singles as settled; the book gives two incompatible values (above). Eccentrics \"should not be used too frequently\" (PDF p. 407), and the authors give no frequency.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples: partial pulls \"around knee-to-lower thigh level\" and squats or bench presses in the range of the \"sticking point\", with power racks securing the start position (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396); and a weightlifter dropping a near-1RM bar on bumper plates to skip the eccentric phase (PDF p. 407).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]; [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]] (Chapter 5's maximum-strength methods and loads); [[claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff]]; [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]] (forced reps, restricted range maxima and maximal eccentrics as shock methods); [[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]] (why partial-range work may transfer narrowly); [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]; [[framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9767,"cache_read_input_tokens":221282,"output_tokens":3168,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":48},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9767},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3168,"cache_read_input_tokens":221282,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9767,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9767,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FmVPSRvGBA4jn4cLsewmVS":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: methods by load category\ncategory: [strength, methods, maximal-strength, loading]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Maximal, Supramaximal, Circa-maximal and Submaximal Methods: Definitions, Loads and Rests\n\n**What it is** — Section 7.1 defines four load categories of resistance method (PDF pp. 406-407 / printed pp. 395-396, checked against the page images):\n\n**Maximal methods**\n- *Weightlifting method:* \"single repetition explosive sets or low repetition sets (2-3 reps), in which each repetition is separated from the next by a short rest period (1-5 minutes, depending on how close the load is to a 1RM)\", also called \"the *interval set* method\" (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395). Bodybuilders adopted it as the *rest-pause method*, \"sets of 1RM, but with rest periods of 10-20 seconds\"; the authors say the shorter rest \"is possible, since bodybuilding movements do not require the high levels of skill, speed and coordination of the Olympic lifts\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n- *Maximal or circa-maximal concentrics:* the concentric phase of a 1RM or near-1RM lift is completed and the bar is dropped (bumper plates) \"so as to avoid executing the depleting eccentric lowering phase\"; in bodybuilding \"a partner usually assists in the lowering\" (PDF p. 407).\n- *Maximal isometrics:* slow or explosive isometrics \"in single or few repetition sets lasting about 5 seconds with rest intervals of 2-3 minutes between sets\" (PDF p. 407).\n- *Maximal eccentrics:* a load \"about 30-40% greater than one's maximum concentric lift\" taken from racks or raised by a partner and lowered under control (PDF p. 407).\n\n**Supramaximal methods** (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396)\n- *Forced repetitions:* to failure, then \"a few more repetitions with the aid of a training partner, who provides the minimal assistance necessary\" for the concentric phase; the eccentric phase \"is carried out without help\".\n- *Restricted range repetitions:* a load heavier than the full-range maximum, moved through \"a few small range concentric-eccentric repetitions near his strongest region of action\"; done at the end of \"a normal 5-8 repetition\" set to the point of \"burn\", these are \"*burning reps*\". Weightlifters and powerlifters use partial ranges \"to rectify any deficit of strength in that region\", for example pulls \"around knee-to-lower thigh level\" and squats or bench presses at the \"sticking point\", set up in power racks.\n- *Cheating:* momentum, bouncing, swaying or accessory muscles, used above 1RM or at the end of a heavy set to failure; \"There are recommended safe ways of cheating and these must be followed religiously if injury is to be avoided\" (the safe ways are not described).\n- *Ballistics:* \"lowering or dropping a weight rapidly to recruit the myotatic stretch reflex or exploit the stored elastic energy\"; \"the training load is not necessarily supramaximal; rather, it is the resulting muscle tension that reaches this level of intensity.\"\n\n**Circa-maximal methods:** loads \"characteristically about 90-97.5% of 1RM\"; the difference from maximal methods is that circa-maximal work \"may be performed with several successive repetitions without a prolonged rest interval\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n\n**Submaximal methods:** \"loads of about 60-80% of one's 1RM to the point of failure on the last set\", \"mainly for bodybuilding reasons such as hypertrophy or muscle definition\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n\n**Classification inconsistency (not a contradiction).** Maximal eccentrics are described under Maximal Methods in the text, which adds that \"Often the eccentric method is regarded as one of the 'supramaximal' methods\" (PDF p. 407); Fig 7.2 lists eccentrics under supramaximal (PDF p. 406, image). The method is the same; only its heading differs.\n\n**Why `contested`: rest between near-maximal singles.** The weightlifting method gives \"1-5 minutes\" between single repetitions (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395). Two pages later the authors say \"much weightlifting and powerlifting training relies on a specific form of interval resistance training, namely the use of single repetitions of near-maximal weights with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398, image). Both sentences describe the rest between near-maximal singles in weightlifting training, and the book does not reconcile them. Inference: the \"few seconds\" version is closer to the bodybuilders' rest-pause (10-20 s) or to intensive clustering (about 20 s, see [[framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets]]) than to the weightlifting method as first defined.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give mechanisms for only some methods: dropping the bar avoids the \"depleting\" eccentric phase; rest-pause can use shorter rests because bodybuilding movements need less skill, speed and coordination; ballistics recruit \"the myotatic stretch reflex\" or \"stored elastic energy\"; restricted range work targets \"any deficit of strength in that region\" (PDF pp. 406-407). For eccentrics they give risk, not mechanism: the method \"carries an increased risk of injury\", is \"associated with greater muscle soreness than most other methods and should not be used too frequently\", and its dangers \"can be reduced by selecting the load carefully and avoiding sudden changes in movement velocity or trajectory\" (PDF p. 407). The damage and adaptation evidence is atomized in [[claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff]].\n\n**When to apply** — As working definitions when writing or reading a programme: the category names tie a method to a load band (circa-maximal about 90-97.5%, submaximal about 60-80% of 1RM) and a rest pattern. Restricted range work fits a lift that fails at one region of the range (the authors' sticking-point use). Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**. Inference: every %1RM band here then rests on a projected max, and the gap between 90% and 97.5% is smaller than the likely error of a projection, so the circa-maximal band is better set by repetitions or bar speed than by a percentage of a projected max. Eric's baseline (line 107) keeps %1RM prescription \"on that explicit basis\" (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for novices on the supramaximal side: the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods (PDF p. 407), and Chapter 5 says shock methods of this kind \"are generally unsuitable for novices\" ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]). Do not use cheating on the authors' word alone: they say safe ways exist but do not give them. Do not treat the rest figures for singles as settled; the book gives two incompatible values (above). Eccentrics \"should not be used too frequently\" (PDF p. 407), and the authors give no frequency.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples: partial pulls \"around knee-to-lower thigh level\" and squats or bench presses in the range of the \"sticking point\", with power racks securing the start position (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396); and a weightlifter dropping a near-1RM bar on bumper plates to skip the eccentric phase (PDF p. 407).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]; [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]] (Chapter 5's maximum-strength methods and loads); [[claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff]]; [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]] (forced reps, restricted range maxima and maximal eccentrics as shock methods); [[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]] (why partial-range work may transfer narrowly); [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]; [[framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffpmgm8QsYzCoLBwdAxH","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"30a828b5-6a7c-4c15-845d-c212528faa04","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:04:57.753Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
