{"parentUuid":"ff115c4d-ea36-4d00-bcc0-5097077de84b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a2b72ded-2f77-4788-be97-76a7696d93d6","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BjRgf5QLRGvqpnAkir5YpN","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"850078f5-e109-4f46-a316-bfa2bf2cecbe","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:05:06.196Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods.md","oldString":"and the gap between 90% and 97.5% is smaller than the likely error of a projection, so the circa-maximal band is better set by repetitions or bar speed than by a percentage of a projected max.","newString":"and the 7.5-point width of the circa-maximal band may be no larger than the error of a projected max, so that band may be easier to hit by repetitions in reserve or bar speed than by a percentage.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: methods by load category\ncategory: [strength, methods, maximal-strength, loading]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Maximal, Supramaximal, Circa-maximal and Submaximal Methods: Definitions, Loads and Rests\n\n**What it is** — Section 7.1 defines four load categories of resistance method (PDF pp. 406-407 / printed pp. 395-396, checked against the page images):\n\n**Maximal methods**\n- *Weightlifting method:* \"single repetition explosive sets or low repetition sets (2-3 reps), in which each repetition is separated from the next by a short rest period (1-5 minutes, depending on how close the load is to a 1RM)\", also called \"the *interval set* method\" (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395). Bodybuilders adopted it as the *rest-pause method*, \"sets of 1RM, but with rest periods of 10-20 seconds\"; the authors say the shorter rest \"is possible, since bodybuilding movements do not require the high levels of skill, speed and coordination of the Olympic lifts\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n- *Maximal or circa-maximal concentrics:* the concentric phase of a 1RM or near-1RM lift is completed and the bar is dropped (bumper plates) \"so as to avoid executing the depleting eccentric lowering phase\"; in bodybuilding \"a partner usually assists in the lowering\" (PDF p. 407).\n- *Maximal isometrics:* slow or explosive isometrics \"in single or few repetition sets lasting about 5 seconds with rest intervals of 2-3 minutes between sets\" (PDF p. 407).\n- *Maximal eccentrics:* a load \"about 30-40% greater than one's maximum concentric lift\" taken from racks or raised by a partner and lowered under control (PDF p. 407).\n\n**Supramaximal methods** (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396)\n- *Forced repetitions:* to failure, then \"a few more repetitions with the aid of a training partner, who provides the minimal assistance necessary\" for the concentric phase; the eccentric phase \"is carried out without help\".\n- *Restricted range repetitions:* a load heavier than the full-range maximum, moved through \"a few small range concentric-eccentric repetitions near his strongest region of action\"; done at the end of \"a normal 5-8 repetition\" set to the point of \"burn\", these are \"*burning reps*\". Weightlifters and powerlifters use partial ranges \"to rectify any deficit of strength in that region\", for example pulls \"around knee-to-lower thigh level\" and squats or bench presses at the \"sticking point\", set up in power racks.\n- *Cheating:* momentum, bouncing, swaying or accessory muscles, used above 1RM or at the end of a heavy set to failure; \"There are recommended safe ways of cheating and these must be followed religiously if injury is to be avoided\" (the safe ways are not described).\n- *Ballistics:* \"lowering or dropping a weight rapidly to recruit the myotatic stretch reflex or exploit the stored elastic energy\"; \"the training load is not necessarily supramaximal; rather, it is the resulting muscle tension that reaches this level of intensity.\"\n\n**Circa-maximal methods:** loads \"characteristically about 90-97.5% of 1RM\"; the difference from maximal methods is that circa-maximal work \"may be performed with several successive repetitions without a prolonged rest interval\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n\n**Submaximal methods:** \"loads of about 60-80% of one's 1RM to the point of failure on the last set\", \"mainly for bodybuilding reasons such as hypertrophy or muscle definition\" (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396).\n\n**Classification inconsistency (not a contradiction).** Maximal eccentrics are described under Maximal Methods in the text, which adds that \"Often the eccentric method is regarded as one of the 'supramaximal' methods\" (PDF p. 407); Fig 7.2 lists eccentrics under supramaximal (PDF p. 406, image). The method is the same; only its heading differs.\n\n**Why `contested`: rest between near-maximal singles.** The weightlifting method gives \"1-5 minutes\" between single repetitions (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395). Two pages later the authors say \"much weightlifting and powerlifting training relies on a specific form of interval resistance training, namely the use of single repetitions of near-maximal weights with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398, image). Both sentences describe the rest between near-maximal singles in weightlifting training, and the book does not reconcile them. Inference: the \"few seconds\" version is closer to the bodybuilders' rest-pause (10-20 s) or to intensive clustering (about 20 s, see [[framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets]]) than to the weightlifting method as first defined.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give mechanisms for only some methods: dropping the bar avoids the \"depleting\" eccentric phase; rest-pause can use shorter rests because bodybuilding movements need less skill, speed and coordination; ballistics recruit \"the myotatic stretch reflex\" or \"stored elastic energy\"; restricted range work targets \"any deficit of strength in that region\" (PDF pp. 406-407). For eccentrics they give risk, not mechanism: the method \"carries an increased risk of injury\", is \"associated with greater muscle soreness than most other methods and should not be used too frequently\", and its dangers \"can be reduced by selecting the load carefully and avoiding sudden changes in movement velocity or trajectory\" (PDF p. 407). The damage and adaptation evidence is atomized in [[claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff]].\n\n**When to apply** — As working definitions when writing or reading a programme: the category names tie a method to a load band (circa-maximal about 90-97.5%, submaximal about 60-80% of 1RM) and a rest pattern. Restricted range work fits a lift that fails at one region of the range (the authors' sticking-point use). Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**. Inference: every %1RM band here then rests on a projected max, and the gap between 90% and 97.5% is smaller than the likely error of a projection, so the circa-maximal band is better set by repetitions or bar speed than by a percentage of a projected max. Eric's baseline (line 107) keeps %1RM prescription \"on that explicit basis\" (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for novices on the supramaximal side: the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods (PDF p. 407), and Chapter 5 says shock methods of this kind \"are generally unsuitable for novices\" ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]). Do not use cheating on the authors' word alone: they say safe ways exist but do not give them. Do not treat the rest figures for singles as settled; the book gives two incompatible values (above). Eccentrics \"should not be used too frequently\" (PDF p. 407), and the authors give no frequency.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples: partial pulls \"around knee-to-lower thigh level\" and squats or bench presses in the range of the \"sticking point\", with power racks securing the start position (PDF p. 407 / printed p. 396); and a weightlifter dropping a near-1RM bar on bumper plates to skip the eccentric phase (PDF p. 407).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]; [[framework--three-methods-for-maximum-strength-repetitive-brief-maximal-isometric]] (Chapter 5's maximum-strength methods and loads); [[claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff]]; [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]] (forced reps, restricted range maxima and maximal eccentrics as shock methods); [[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]] (why partial-range work may transfer narrowly); [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]; [[framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":37,"oldLines":7,"newStart":37,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors give mechanisms for only some methods: dropping the bar avoids the \"depleting\" eccentric phase; rest-pause can use shorter rests because bodybuilding movements need less skill, speed and coordination; ballistics recruit \"the myotatic stretch reflex\" or \"stored elastic energy\"; restricted range work targets \"any deficit of strength in that region\" (PDF pp. 406-407). For eccentrics they give risk, not mechanism: the method \"carries an increased risk of injury\", is \"associated with greater muscle soreness than most other methods and should not be used too frequently\", and its dangers \"can be reduced by selecting the load carefully and avoiding sudden changes in movement velocity or trajectory\" (PDF p. 407). The damage and adaptation evidence is atomized in [[claim--eccentric-training-damage-adaptation-and-injury-risk-tradeoff]]."," ","-**When to apply** — As working definitions when writing or reading a programme: the category names tie a method to a load band (circa-maximal about 90-97.5%, submaximal about 60-80% of 1RM) and a rest pattern. Restricted range work fits a lift that fails at one region of the range (the authors' sticking-point use). Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**. Inference: every %1RM band here then rests on a projected max, and the gap between 90% and 97.5% is smaller than the likely error of a projection, so the circa-maximal band is better set by repetitions or bar speed than by a percentage of a projected max. Eric's baseline (line 107) keeps %1RM prescription \"on that explicit basis\" (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02).","+**When to apply** — As working definitions when writing or reading a programme: the category names tie a method to a load band (circa-maximal about 90-97.5%, submaximal about 60-80% of 1RM) and a rest pattern. Restricted range work fits a lift that fails at one region of the range (the authors' sticking-point use). Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**. Inference: every %1RM band here then rests on a projected max, and the 7.5-point width of the circa-maximal band may be no larger than the error of a projected max, so that band may be easier to hit by repetitions in reserve or bar speed than by a percentage. Eric's baseline (line 107) keeps %1RM prescription \"on that explicit basis\" (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02)."," "," **When NOT to apply** — Not for novices on the supramaximal side: the authors class eccentrics, forced reps and cheating as higher-risk methods (PDF p. 407), and Chapter 5 says shock methods of this kind \"are generally unsuitable for novices\" ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]). Do not use cheating on the authors' word alone: they say safe ways exist but do not give them. Do not treat the rest figures for singles as settled; the book gives two incompatible values (above). Eccentrics \"should not be used too frequently\" (PDF p. 407), and the authors give no frequency."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ff115c4d-ea36-4d00-bcc0-5097077de84b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"850078f5-e109-4f46-a316-bfa2bf2cecbe","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffppJMaiSVE4gDeNErrJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KhNk6J37B2Jmh3GvogYiNF","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: bodybuilding regimes and set methods\ncategory: [hypertrophy, bodybuilding, sets-and-reps, programming]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Three Bodybuilding Regimes (Classical, Extensive, Intensive) and the Add-on Methods They Use\n\n**What it is** — Under Submaximal Methods the authors give three regimes (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397, checked against the page image):\n\n| Regime | Sets x reps | Load | Rest between sets |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A. Classical | 3-5 x 8-10, same load | not stated | 2-5 min |\n| B. Extensive (\"high volume\") | 3-5 x 15-20 | \"about 60-70%\" | 2-3 min, \"increasing with increase in load from set to set\" |\n| C. Intensive (\"high intensity\") | 3-5 x 5-8 | \"80-90%\" | 2-5 min, increasing with load |\n\nThe classical regime \"may also be regarded as a particular case of the *plateau system*\". A second classical form raises the load each set so that repetitions fall \"from 10 in the first set to 4-5 repetitions in the last set\", with rest rising as load rises: \"a typical form of the *pyramid system*\". For the intensive regime the authors point to \"Figs 1.8 and 1.9\" (not checked for this entry).\n\n\"Each of the preceding regimes may use\" forced repetitions, cheating, eccentrics (negatives), burning repetitions and ballistics (defined in [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]), plus these \"popularly used\" methods (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397):\n- **Supersets:** set 1 of exercise 1, then set 1 of exercise 2, and so on. \"Since supersets are not intended to develop strength, the athlete allows little or no rest between sets.\" More than two exercises is *multi-setting*; the *giant set* is \"4-6 different exercises performed in a mini-circuit\".\n- **Pre-exhaustion:** a single-joint exercise first \"preferentially exhausts one of the muscle groups that will be used in the following multi-joint exercise\", which also \"compels other muscles to act strongly as accessory muscles\".\n- **Repetitions to failure:** until no further repetition is possible without help; \"often used for muscle endurance and definition training\".\n- **Split routine:** two components trained on successive workouts (upper then lower body; or flexors and adductors one day, extensors and abductors the next); advanced athletes may split within a day. \"A common weekly split routine prescribes split training on Monday-Tuesday, resting on Wednesday, more split training on Thursday-Friday, completed by a weekend of passive or active rest.\"\n- **Zone training** (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398): one body zone per session, so a whole-body regime \"must be spread over several sessions or days\"; split routines are \"one variation of this method\".\n\n**Set against Table 1.1.** The book's own goal chart gives hypertrophy as 60-80% 1RM, 8-15 repetitions, 4-8 sets, 2-5 min rest (PDF p. 22 / printed p. 11, per [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]). The three regimes overlap it on load and rest; the extensive regime's 15-20 repetitions and the intensive regime's 5-8 repetitions fall outside 8-15, and all three use 3-5 sets against 4-8. Inference: these are named variants inside a broad goal chart, not two answers to one question, so this is recorded as a difference, not a contradiction. The submaximal category itself is \"about 60-80% of one's 1RM\" (PDF p. 407), while the intensive regime reaches 90%.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give a reason only for supersets (little rest because they \"are not intended to develop strength\") and for pre-exhaustion (the pre-exhausted prime mover forces accessory muscles to work harder, PDF p. 408). They give no mechanism for the regimes and no study. Chapter 7 places these methods under the structural aim of hypertrophy ([[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]); Chapter 1 sets out the kinds of hypertrophy they produce ([[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]).\n\n**When to apply** — When a hypertrophy or work-capacity phase is planned and the coach wants named, bounded regimes. Eric's baseline (line 180): \"The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power, each with defined intensity/volume bands (e.g., hypertrophy phase 50-75% intensity, 3-6 sets of 5-20 reps ...)\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: the classical and extensive regimes sit inside those course bands; the intensive regime's 80-90% sits above the course's hypertrophy band, in the range the course gives for the strength phase (\"strength phase 80-90% intensity\", same line). Split routines and zone training suit athletes who lift on more days than a whole-body session allows.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not as sport strength methods. The authors say bodybuilding methods \"are of minimal significance for developing most of the specific functional types of strength fitness required in competitive sport\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401). Supersets are, in the authors' words, \"not intended to develop strength\" (PDF p. 408). Eric's baseline (line 104): \"single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: pre-exhaustion and isolation-led splits build a session around single-joint work, which runs against that rule outside a dedicated hypertrophy block. Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**; the regimes' percentages would rest on projected maxes.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: \"if supine dumbbell flyes are performed before a bench press, the pectoral and anterior deltoid muscles are pre-exhausted and the triceps are forced to become more vigorously involved\" (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Table 1.1); [[framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids]] (plateau and pyramid systems); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]; [[framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations]]; [[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]; [[claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load]] (the book's view on how far a single-joint exercise isolates a muscle); [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]] (a two-station circuit is a superset)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3298,"cache_read_input_tokens":231049,"output_tokens":2965,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":79},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3298},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2965,"cache_read_input_tokens":231049,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3298,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3298,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01KhNk6J37B2Jmh3GvogYiNF":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: bodybuilding regimes and set methods\ncategory: [hypertrophy, bodybuilding, sets-and-reps, programming]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Three Bodybuilding Regimes (Classical, Extensive, Intensive) and the Add-on Methods They Use\n\n**What it is** — Under Submaximal Methods the authors give three regimes (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397, checked against the page image):\n\n| Regime | Sets x reps | Load | Rest between sets |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A. Classical | 3-5 x 8-10, same load | not stated | 2-5 min |\n| B. Extensive (\"high volume\") | 3-5 x 15-20 | \"about 60-70%\" | 2-3 min, \"increasing with increase in load from set to set\" |\n| C. Intensive (\"high intensity\") | 3-5 x 5-8 | \"80-90%\" | 2-5 min, increasing with load |\n\nThe classical regime \"may also be regarded as a particular case of the *plateau system*\". A second classical form raises the load each set so that repetitions fall \"from 10 in the first set to 4-5 repetitions in the last set\", with rest rising as load rises: \"a typical form of the *pyramid system*\". For the intensive regime the authors point to \"Figs 1.8 and 1.9\" (not checked for this entry).\n\n\"Each of the preceding regimes may use\" forced repetitions, cheating, eccentrics (negatives), burning repetitions and ballistics (defined in [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]), plus these \"popularly used\" methods (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397):\n- **Supersets:** set 1 of exercise 1, then set 1 of exercise 2, and so on. \"Since supersets are not intended to develop strength, the athlete allows little or no rest between sets.\" More than two exercises is *multi-setting*; the *giant set* is \"4-6 different exercises performed in a mini-circuit\".\n- **Pre-exhaustion:** a single-joint exercise first \"preferentially exhausts one of the muscle groups that will be used in the following multi-joint exercise\", which also \"compels other muscles to act strongly as accessory muscles\".\n- **Repetitions to failure:** until no further repetition is possible without help; \"often used for muscle endurance and definition training\".\n- **Split routine:** two components trained on successive workouts (upper then lower body; or flexors and adductors one day, extensors and abductors the next); advanced athletes may split within a day. \"A common weekly split routine prescribes split training on Monday-Tuesday, resting on Wednesday, more split training on Thursday-Friday, completed by a weekend of passive or active rest.\"\n- **Zone training** (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398): one body zone per session, so a whole-body regime \"must be spread over several sessions or days\"; split routines are \"one variation of this method\".\n\n**Set against Table 1.1.** The book's own goal chart gives hypertrophy as 60-80% 1RM, 8-15 repetitions, 4-8 sets, 2-5 min rest (PDF p. 22 / printed p. 11, per [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]). The three regimes overlap it on load and rest; the extensive regime's 15-20 repetitions and the intensive regime's 5-8 repetitions fall outside 8-15, and all three use 3-5 sets against 4-8. Inference: these are named variants inside a broad goal chart, not two answers to one question, so this is recorded as a difference, not a contradiction. The submaximal category itself is \"about 60-80% of one's 1RM\" (PDF p. 407), while the intensive regime reaches 90%.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give a reason only for supersets (little rest because they \"are not intended to develop strength\") and for pre-exhaustion (the pre-exhausted prime mover forces accessory muscles to work harder, PDF p. 408). They give no mechanism for the regimes and no study. Chapter 7 places these methods under the structural aim of hypertrophy ([[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]); Chapter 1 sets out the kinds of hypertrophy they produce ([[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]).\n\n**When to apply** — When a hypertrophy or work-capacity phase is planned and the coach wants named, bounded regimes. Eric's baseline (line 180): \"The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power, each with defined intensity/volume bands (e.g., hypertrophy phase 50-75% intensity, 3-6 sets of 5-20 reps ...)\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: the classical and extensive regimes sit inside those course bands; the intensive regime's 80-90% sits above the course's hypertrophy band, in the range the course gives for the strength phase (\"strength phase 80-90% intensity\", same line). Split routines and zone training suit athletes who lift on more days than a whole-body session allows.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not as sport strength methods. The authors say bodybuilding methods \"are of minimal significance for developing most of the specific functional types of strength fitness required in competitive sport\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401). Supersets are, in the authors' words, \"not intended to develop strength\" (PDF p. 408). Eric's baseline (line 104): \"single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: pre-exhaustion and isolation-led splits build a session around single-joint work, which runs against that rule outside a dedicated hypertrophy block. Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**; the regimes' percentages would rest on projected maxes.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: \"if supine dumbbell flyes are performed before a bench press, the pectoral and anterior deltoid muscles are pre-exhausted and the triceps are forced to become more vigorously involved\" (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Table 1.1); [[framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids]] (plateau and pyramid systems); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]; [[framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations]]; [[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]; [[claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load]] (the book's view on how far a single-joint exercise isolates a muscle); [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]] (a two-station circuit is a superset)\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffpozs7XaDffq8Qfr1f5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1c430fbf-e3a1-4bd3-985c-370d73eb69a4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:05:26.971Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1c430fbf-e3a1-4bd3-985c-370d73eb69a4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a2b72ded-2f77-4788-be97-76a7696d93d6","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KhNk6J37B2Jmh3GvogYiNF","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"fcbb2721-e58b-4d55-b9dc-88fd6fa57650","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:05:27.090Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: bodybuilding regimes and set methods\ncategory: [hypertrophy, bodybuilding, sets-and-reps, programming]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Three Bodybuilding Regimes (Classical, Extensive, Intensive) and the Add-on Methods They Use\n\n**What it is** — Under Submaximal Methods the authors give three regimes (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397, checked against the page image):\n\n| Regime | Sets x reps | Load | Rest between sets |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A. Classical | 3-5 x 8-10, same load | not stated | 2-5 min |\n| B. Extensive (\"high volume\") | 3-5 x 15-20 | \"about 60-70%\" | 2-3 min, \"increasing with increase in load from set to set\" |\n| C. Intensive (\"high intensity\") | 3-5 x 5-8 | \"80-90%\" | 2-5 min, increasing with load |\n\nThe classical regime \"may also be regarded as a particular case of the *plateau system*\". A second classical form raises the load each set so that repetitions fall \"from 10 in the first set to 4-5 repetitions in the last set\", with rest rising as load rises: \"a typical form of the *pyramid system*\". For the intensive regime the authors point to \"Figs 1.8 and 1.9\" (not checked for this entry).\n\n\"Each of the preceding regimes may use\" forced repetitions, cheating, eccentrics (negatives), burning repetitions and ballistics (defined in [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]), plus these \"popularly used\" methods (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397):\n- **Supersets:** set 1 of exercise 1, then set 1 of exercise 2, and so on. \"Since supersets are not intended to develop strength, the athlete allows little or no rest between sets.\" More than two exercises is *multi-setting*; the *giant set* is \"4-6 different exercises performed in a mini-circuit\".\n- **Pre-exhaustion:** a single-joint exercise first \"preferentially exhausts one of the muscle groups that will be used in the following multi-joint exercise\", which also \"compels other muscles to act strongly as accessory muscles\".\n- **Repetitions to failure:** until no further repetition is possible without help; \"often used for muscle endurance and definition training\".\n- **Split routine:** two components trained on successive workouts (upper then lower body; or flexors and adductors one day, extensors and abductors the next); advanced athletes may split within a day. \"A common weekly split routine prescribes split training on Monday-Tuesday, resting on Wednesday, more split training on Thursday-Friday, completed by a weekend of passive or active rest.\"\n- **Zone training** (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398): one body zone per session, so a whole-body regime \"must be spread over several sessions or days\"; split routines are \"one variation of this method\".\n\n**Set against Table 1.1.** The book's own goal chart gives hypertrophy as 60-80% 1RM, 8-15 repetitions, 4-8 sets, 2-5 min rest (PDF p. 22 / printed p. 11, per [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]). The three regimes overlap it on load and rest; the extensive regime's 15-20 repetitions and the intensive regime's 5-8 repetitions fall outside 8-15, and all three use 3-5 sets against 4-8. Inference: these are named variants inside a broad goal chart, not two answers to one question, so this is recorded as a difference, not a contradiction. The submaximal category itself is \"about 60-80% of one's 1RM\" (PDF p. 407), while the intensive regime reaches 90%.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give a reason only for supersets (little rest because they \"are not intended to develop strength\") and for pre-exhaustion (the pre-exhausted prime mover forces accessory muscles to work harder, PDF p. 408). They give no mechanism for the regimes and no study. Chapter 7 places these methods under the structural aim of hypertrophy ([[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]); Chapter 1 sets out the kinds of hypertrophy they produce ([[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]).\n\n**When to apply** — When a hypertrophy or work-capacity phase is planned and the coach wants named, bounded regimes. Eric's baseline (line 180): \"The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power, each with defined intensity/volume bands (e.g., hypertrophy phase 50-75% intensity, 3-6 sets of 5-20 reps ...)\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: the classical and extensive regimes sit inside those course bands; the intensive regime's 80-90% sits above the course's hypertrophy band, in the range the course gives for the strength phase (\"strength phase 80-90% intensity\", same line). Split routines and zone training suit athletes who lift on more days than a whole-body session allows.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not as sport strength methods. The authors say bodybuilding methods \"are of minimal significance for developing most of the specific functional types of strength fitness required in competitive sport\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401). Supersets are, in the authors' words, \"not intended to develop strength\" (PDF p. 408). Eric's baseline (line 104): \"single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: pre-exhaustion and isolation-led splits build a session around single-joint work, which runs against that rule outside a dedicated hypertrophy block. Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**; the regimes' percentages would rest on projected maxes.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: \"if supine dumbbell flyes are performed before a bench press, the pectoral and anterior deltoid muscles are pre-exhausted and the triceps are forced to become more vigorously involved\" (PDF p. 408 / printed p. 397).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Table 1.1); [[framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids]] (plateau and pyramid systems); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]; [[framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations]]; [[framework--sarcomere-vs-sarcoplasmic-hypertrophy]]; [[claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load]] (the book's view on how far a single-joint exercise isolates a muscle); [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]] (a two-station circuit is a superset)\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1c430fbf-e3a1-4bd3-985c-370d73eb69a4","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
