{"parentUuid":"dbb631a3-bcbc-4d17-9ae5-419bfa9989b9","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_record","entries":[],"toolInputCopies":[{"id":"toolu_01BjRgf5QLRGvqpnAkir5YpN","copy":"wire"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"f22a0965-9b7d-405e-a273-f0cdf350fd87","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:05:31.045Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f22a0965-9b7d-405e-a273-f0cdf350fd87","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffprK3ZENnkn97EXnHPt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3244,"cache_read_input_tokens":234347,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3244,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":456,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffpr9XoxT1JxReuSD7vF","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"a8c5ebca-a4b4-4b56-a22c-d5627b9496fc","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:05:31.045Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"a8c5ebca-a4b4-4b56-a22c-d5627b9496fc","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffprK3ZENnkn97EXnHPt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01K8cDJ2ycLLJRCNT8g17tnM","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: set structures and pyramids\ncategory: [sets-and-reps, loading, programming, bodybuilding]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Set Structures: Plateau, Step, Stripping, Three Kinds of Pyramid, and the Reverse Pyramid\n\n**What it is** — The authors define six ways of arranging load and repetitions across the sets of one exercise (PDF pp. 408-409 / printed pp. 397-398, checked against the page images):\n\n- **Plateau:** \"After a specific warmup with a light weight, the load remains constant for 3-4 sets (e.g. 50 x 10, 70 x 6, 70 x 6, 70 x 6)\". Used \"during the early stages of training novices or for transitional active rest phases after weeks of heavy training\" (PDF p. 408). The unit of 50 and 70 is not given.\n- **Step:** \"The number of repetitions is kept fixed, while the load is increased after each set until the prescribed number of repetitions can no longer be maintained (e.g. 60 x 3, 70 x 3, 80 x 3, 85 x 3)\" (PDF p. 408).\n- **Stripping (descending set, \"Drop Sets\"):** pyramid up \"until a target load is reached with about 3-4 repetitions\", then \"A partner immediately removes 5-10 kg and the athlete exercises to failure\", repeated \"until the starting weight or less is reached\". Used \"for muscle endurance and definition training\". If the rising pyramid reaches \"a 1RM or 2RM with no more than 6 repetitions for any set, then stripping offers a logical way of developing strength and muscle endurance, since the use of higher repetition sets is too fatiguing to enable one to reach a true 1RM\" (PDF p. 408).\n- **Pyramid (load pyramiding):** \"4-5 sets\" with load rising and repetitions falling. Submaximal example \"10 x 70%.1RM, 8 x 80%.1RM, 6 x 85%.1RM, 4 x 90%.1RM\"; maximal strength example \"6 x 85%.1RM, 4 x 90%.1RM, 2 x 97%.1RM, 1 x 100%.1RM\", with the values stated to be \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (not checked for this entry). Rest \"2-6 minutes, depending on the load used, the aim of the pyramid and the number of the set (the rest increases with load)\" (PDF pp. 408-409).\n- **Repetition pyramiding:** fixed load, repetitions raised \"by one or two repetitions per set (e.g. from an initial 4 to a maximum of 10)\". **Rest pyramiding:** fixed load, rest progressively shortened; load is rarely raised at the same time, and if it is, \"the increments are kept small (2.5-5 kg)\" (PDF p. 409). The DeLorme method and APRE \"involve some form of pyramiding\" (PDF p. 409).\n- **Reverse pyramid:** pyramid up to a final set, then \"work down the pyramid in reverse order using the same number of repetitions for each load\". It is \"very similar to the stripping method, but does not require the athlete to exercise to failure\" (PDF p. 409).\n\nThe authors then add: \"Pyramiding is rarely used by serious athletes today; they find it more beneficial to progress rapidly to the main training load and performing higher repetition work on the way down from a maximum, if absolutely necessary\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398). Their sticking-point list repeats this: \"If you uses pyramids, use fewer repetition sets to reach your maximum, then do your repetition work during a reverse pyramid down from your maximum. This can be especially useful if you wish to concurrently work on strength and hypertrophy\" (item 7, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402).\n\nInference: this sits alongside, not against, the chapter's opening point that pyramiding can serve hypertrophy, strength or endurance \"in the earlier preparatory stages\" (PDF p. 405). One sentence says what pyramiding can do; the other says what serious athletes prefer.\n\n**Why it works** — The one reason the authors give is for stripping and the reverse pyramid: high-repetition sets on the way up are \"too fatiguing to enable one to reach a true 1RM\" (PDF p. 408), so the repetition work goes after the heavy set. They give no mechanism or study for the other structures.\n\n**When to apply** — Plateau sets for novices early on and for active-rest transitions, by the authors' own scope (PDF p. 408). Step sets to find a working load for a fixed repetition target. A short ramp to the top set, then back-off or reverse-pyramid sets, when a session must serve strength and hypertrophy together (PDF pp. 409, 413). Eric's baseline (line 107): \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" **Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)**, kept with %1RM \"on that explicit basis\" of projected maxes (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02). Inference: a pyramid written in %1RM, like the authors' two examples, inherits the projection error of Eric's baseline line 61 (\"No sport performs true 1RM testing\", **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**); the step method finds the day's load from performance instead.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not use a long ascending pyramid to reach a true maximum: the authors say \"higher repetition sets\" on the way up are too fatiguing for that (PDF p. 408), and that serious athletes rarely pyramid (PDF p. 409). Stripping to failure is, in the authors' words, \"commonly used for muscle endurance and definition training\" (PDF p. 408); it is not a maximal-strength method unless the ascent stays at 6 repetitions or fewer. Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases, not for a peaking block.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own maximal-strength pyramid: 6 x 85%, 4 x 90%, 2 x 97%, 1 x 100% of 1RM, with rest lengthening as the load rises, inside 2-6 minutes (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]] (the classical regime as a plateau case, the second classical method as a pyramid); [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]]; [[heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing]]; [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]] (pyramid and stripping circuits); [[heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3244,"cache_read_input_tokens":234347,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3244,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01K8cDJ2ycLLJRCNT8g17tnM":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: set structures and pyramids\ncategory: [sets-and-reps, loading, programming, bodybuilding]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Set Structures: Plateau, Step, Stripping, Three Kinds of Pyramid, and the Reverse Pyramid\n\n**What it is** — The authors define six ways of arranging load and repetitions across the sets of one exercise (PDF pp. 408-409 / printed pp. 397-398, checked against the page images):\n\n- **Plateau:** \"After a specific warmup with a light weight, the load remains constant for 3-4 sets (e.g. 50 x 10, 70 x 6, 70 x 6, 70 x 6)\". Used \"during the early stages of training novices or for transitional active rest phases after weeks of heavy training\" (PDF p. 408). The unit of 50 and 70 is not given.\n- **Step:** \"The number of repetitions is kept fixed, while the load is increased after each set until the prescribed number of repetitions can no longer be maintained (e.g. 60 x 3, 70 x 3, 80 x 3, 85 x 3)\" (PDF p. 408).\n- **Stripping (descending set, \"Drop Sets\"):** pyramid up \"until a target load is reached with about 3-4 repetitions\", then \"A partner immediately removes 5-10 kg and the athlete exercises to failure\", repeated \"until the starting weight or less is reached\". Used \"for muscle endurance and definition training\". If the rising pyramid reaches \"a 1RM or 2RM with no more than 6 repetitions for any set, then stripping offers a logical way of developing strength and muscle endurance, since the use of higher repetition sets is too fatiguing to enable one to reach a true 1RM\" (PDF p. 408).\n- **Pyramid (load pyramiding):** \"4-5 sets\" with load rising and repetitions falling. Submaximal example \"10 x 70%.1RM, 8 x 80%.1RM, 6 x 85%.1RM, 4 x 90%.1RM\"; maximal strength example \"6 x 85%.1RM, 4 x 90%.1RM, 2 x 97%.1RM, 1 x 100%.1RM\", with the values stated to be \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (not checked for this entry). Rest \"2-6 minutes, depending on the load used, the aim of the pyramid and the number of the set (the rest increases with load)\" (PDF pp. 408-409).\n- **Repetition pyramiding:** fixed load, repetitions raised \"by one or two repetitions per set (e.g. from an initial 4 to a maximum of 10)\". **Rest pyramiding:** fixed load, rest progressively shortened; load is rarely raised at the same time, and if it is, \"the increments are kept small (2.5-5 kg)\" (PDF p. 409). The DeLorme method and APRE \"involve some form of pyramiding\" (PDF p. 409).\n- **Reverse pyramid:** pyramid up to a final set, then \"work down the pyramid in reverse order using the same number of repetitions for each load\". It is \"very similar to the stripping method, but does not require the athlete to exercise to failure\" (PDF p. 409).\n\nThe authors then add: \"Pyramiding is rarely used by serious athletes today; they find it more beneficial to progress rapidly to the main training load and performing higher repetition work on the way down from a maximum, if absolutely necessary\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398). Their sticking-point list repeats this: \"If you uses pyramids, use fewer repetition sets to reach your maximum, then do your repetition work during a reverse pyramid down from your maximum. This can be especially useful if you wish to concurrently work on strength and hypertrophy\" (item 7, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402).\n\nInference: this sits alongside, not against, the chapter's opening point that pyramiding can serve hypertrophy, strength or endurance \"in the earlier preparatory stages\" (PDF p. 405). One sentence says what pyramiding can do; the other says what serious athletes prefer.\n\n**Why it works** — The one reason the authors give is for stripping and the reverse pyramid: high-repetition sets on the way up are \"too fatiguing to enable one to reach a true 1RM\" (PDF p. 408), so the repetition work goes after the heavy set. They give no mechanism or study for the other structures.\n\n**When to apply** — Plateau sets for novices early on and for active-rest transitions, by the authors' own scope (PDF p. 408). Step sets to find a working load for a fixed repetition target. A short ramp to the top set, then back-off or reverse-pyramid sets, when a session must serve strength and hypertrophy together (PDF pp. 409, 413). Eric's baseline (line 107): \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" **Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)**, kept with %1RM \"on that explicit basis\" of projected maxes (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02). Inference: a pyramid written in %1RM, like the authors' two examples, inherits the projection error of Eric's baseline line 61 (\"No sport performs true 1RM testing\", **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**); the step method finds the day's load from performance instead.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not use a long ascending pyramid to reach a true maximum: the authors say \"higher repetition sets\" on the way up are too fatiguing for that (PDF p. 408), and that serious athletes rarely pyramid (PDF p. 409). Stripping to failure is, in the authors' words, \"commonly used for muscle endurance and definition training\" (PDF p. 408); it is not a maximal-strength method unless the ascent stays at 6 repetitions or fewer. Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases, not for a peaking block.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own maximal-strength pyramid: 6 x 85%, 4 x 90%, 2 x 97%, 1 x 100% of 1RM, with rest lengthening as the load rises, inside 2-6 minutes (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]] (the classical regime as a plateau case, the second classical method as a pyramid); [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]]; [[heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing]]; [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]] (pyramid and stripping circuits); [[heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffpr9XoxT1JxReuSD7vF","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"640a86d5-d7ec-47f1-b4f8-bb846573ce87","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:05:50.147Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"640a86d5-d7ec-47f1-b4f8-bb846573ce87","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a2b72ded-2f77-4788-be97-76a7696d93d6","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K8cDJ2ycLLJRCNT8g17tnM","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"e420b397-606b-4909-8fc4-f2e5460eebeb","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:05:50.274Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: set structures and pyramids\ncategory: [sets-and-reps, loading, programming, bodybuilding]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Set Structures: Plateau, Step, Stripping, Three Kinds of Pyramid, and the Reverse Pyramid\n\n**What it is** — The authors define six ways of arranging load and repetitions across the sets of one exercise (PDF pp. 408-409 / printed pp. 397-398, checked against the page images):\n\n- **Plateau:** \"After a specific warmup with a light weight, the load remains constant for 3-4 sets (e.g. 50 x 10, 70 x 6, 70 x 6, 70 x 6)\". Used \"during the early stages of training novices or for transitional active rest phases after weeks of heavy training\" (PDF p. 408). The unit of 50 and 70 is not given.\n- **Step:** \"The number of repetitions is kept fixed, while the load is increased after each set until the prescribed number of repetitions can no longer be maintained (e.g. 60 x 3, 70 x 3, 80 x 3, 85 x 3)\" (PDF p. 408).\n- **Stripping (descending set, \"Drop Sets\"):** pyramid up \"until a target load is reached with about 3-4 repetitions\", then \"A partner immediately removes 5-10 kg and the athlete exercises to failure\", repeated \"until the starting weight or less is reached\". Used \"for muscle endurance and definition training\". If the rising pyramid reaches \"a 1RM or 2RM with no more than 6 repetitions for any set, then stripping offers a logical way of developing strength and muscle endurance, since the use of higher repetition sets is too fatiguing to enable one to reach a true 1RM\" (PDF p. 408).\n- **Pyramid (load pyramiding):** \"4-5 sets\" with load rising and repetitions falling. Submaximal example \"10 x 70%.1RM, 8 x 80%.1RM, 6 x 85%.1RM, 4 x 90%.1RM\"; maximal strength example \"6 x 85%.1RM, 4 x 90%.1RM, 2 x 97%.1RM, 1 x 100%.1RM\", with the values stated to be \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (not checked for this entry). Rest \"2-6 minutes, depending on the load used, the aim of the pyramid and the number of the set (the rest increases with load)\" (PDF pp. 408-409).\n- **Repetition pyramiding:** fixed load, repetitions raised \"by one or two repetitions per set (e.g. from an initial 4 to a maximum of 10)\". **Rest pyramiding:** fixed load, rest progressively shortened; load is rarely raised at the same time, and if it is, \"the increments are kept small (2.5-5 kg)\" (PDF p. 409). The DeLorme method and APRE \"involve some form of pyramiding\" (PDF p. 409).\n- **Reverse pyramid:** pyramid up to a final set, then \"work down the pyramid in reverse order using the same number of repetitions for each load\". It is \"very similar to the stripping method, but does not require the athlete to exercise to failure\" (PDF p. 409).\n\nThe authors then add: \"Pyramiding is rarely used by serious athletes today; they find it more beneficial to progress rapidly to the main training load and performing higher repetition work on the way down from a maximum, if absolutely necessary\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398). Their sticking-point list repeats this: \"If you uses pyramids, use fewer repetition sets to reach your maximum, then do your repetition work during a reverse pyramid down from your maximum. This can be especially useful if you wish to concurrently work on strength and hypertrophy\" (item 7, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402).\n\nInference: this sits alongside, not against, the chapter's opening point that pyramiding can serve hypertrophy, strength or endurance \"in the earlier preparatory stages\" (PDF p. 405). One sentence says what pyramiding can do; the other says what serious athletes prefer.\n\n**Why it works** — The one reason the authors give is for stripping and the reverse pyramid: high-repetition sets on the way up are \"too fatiguing to enable one to reach a true 1RM\" (PDF p. 408), so the repetition work goes after the heavy set. They give no mechanism or study for the other structures.\n\n**When to apply** — Plateau sets for novices early on and for active-rest transitions, by the authors' own scope (PDF p. 408). Step sets to find a working load for a fixed repetition target. A short ramp to the top set, then back-off or reverse-pyramid sets, when a session must serve strength and hypertrophy together (PDF pp. 409, 413). Eric's baseline (line 107): \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" **Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)**, kept with %1RM \"on that explicit basis\" of projected maxes (Tension 16, resolved 2026-10-02). Inference: a pyramid written in %1RM, like the authors' two examples, inherits the projection error of Eric's baseline line 61 (\"No sport performs true 1RM testing\", **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**); the step method finds the day's load from performance instead.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not use a long ascending pyramid to reach a true maximum: the authors say \"higher repetition sets\" on the way up are too fatiguing for that (PDF p. 408), and that serious athletes rarely pyramid (PDF p. 409). Stripping to failure is, in the authors' words, \"commonly used for muscle endurance and definition training\" (PDF p. 408); it is not a maximal-strength method unless the ascent stays at 6 repetitions or fewer. Plateau sets are for novices and transition phases, not for a peaking block.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own maximal-strength pyramid: 6 x 85%, 4 x 90%, 2 x 97%, 1 x 100% of 1RM, with rest lengthening as the load rises, inside 2-6 minutes (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]] (the classical regime as a plateau case, the second classical method as a pyramid); [[claim--delorme-oxford-mccloy-progressive-resistance-variants-compared]]; [[framework--apre-autoregulating-progressive-resistance-exercise]]; [[heuristic--apre-skill-learning-6rm-pyramid-test-and-rm-muscle-testing]]; [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]] (pyramid and stripping circuits); [[heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"640a86d5-d7ec-47f1-b4f8-bb846573ce87","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e420b397-606b-4909-8fc4-f2e5460eebeb","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffprK3ZENnkn97EXnHPt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AYuJaoKFLZuMv8HqR78UMb","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: interval resistance and cluster sets\ncategory: [strength-endurance, interval-training, cluster-sets, loading]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Interval Resistance Training (Extensive and Intensive) and Cluster Sets\n\n**What it is** — The authors define interval resistance training by analogy with track: \"the primary aim of interval training is generally to develop strength-endurance by means of systematically alternating work and recovery phases\", with rest \"(preferably active rest)\" too short for full recovery \"so that the athlete is forced to cope with increasing exhaustion\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398, checked against the page image). They split it into two broad categories, \"one to increase strength or power and the other to increase endurance\", and the endurance category into two methods (PDF p. 409):\n\n| | Extensive interval | Intensive interval |\n|---|---|---|\n| Develops | \"medium duration strength-endurance\" and recovery capacity | \"short-duration strength-endurance\" and recovery capacity |\n| Load | \"30-40% of 1RM\" | \"50-60% of 1RM\" |\n| Sets x reps | \"5-6 sets of 20-30 repetitions\" | \"3-6 sets, where each set is performed to failure\" |\n| Rest | \"1-2 minutes\" | \"10-90 seconds\", by exercise, load, fitness and loading duration |\n| Energy | below 40% \"arterial blood flow is not restricted\" and \"'aerobic' energy processes predominate\" | higher loads \"inhibit arterial blood flow and anaerobic energy processes predominate\" |\n| Heart rate | large muscle groups needed to reach the level \"recommended for aerobic conditioning, e.g. 75% of (220-Age)\" | \"can readily increase heart rates to over 180 beats/min\" |\n| Circuits | \"Specially designed circuit training programmes\" can serve | special circuits with \"1-3 minutes\" between circuits |\n\nFor extensive work the authors want \"the larger muscle groups such as the quadriceps, glutei, pectorals and erector spinae\"; \"bicep curls, calf raises and tricep push-downs play a minimal role in elevating the heart rate\" (PDF p. 409).\n\n**Cluster training** is \"another form of interval training\": one or more repetitions \"with a 10-20 second interval between each repetition or cluster of repetitions in an extended set\". \"Characteristically, the minimum load used is one's 5RM and 4-6 sets are performed\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398). *Extensive clustering*: \"4-6 repetitions with one's 4-6RM, with 10 second rest intervals between each cluster\", or stripping-style with the load cut \"by 10-20%\" and a 10 s rest after each set (PDF pp. 409-410). *Intensive clustering*: \"4-6 sets of only one repetition with 75-90% of 1RM, with about a 20 second rest between repetitions for 4-6 repetitions\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, image). That last sentence is unclear as printed: it gives both \"sets of only one repetition\" and \"4-6 repetitions\" without saying how they nest.\n\n**Why `contested`.** The minimum load for cluster training is \"one's 5RM\" (PDF p. 409), but intensive clustering uses \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). Both state the load for the same method. Inference: under the usual relation between repetition maximum and %1RM, a 5RM is well above 75% of 1RM, so the lower end of the intensive range falls below the stated minimum. The book does not reconcile them.\n\nA second internal conflict touches this section: the weightlifting method rests \"1-5 minutes\" between singles (PDF p. 406), while this section says weightlifting and powerlifting rely on near-maximal singles \"with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409); see [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]].\n\n**Different method, not a conflict.** Chapter 5's circuit guidelines give aerobic circuits \"20-40\" repetitions at \"usually not more than 30% of 1RM\" and anaerobic circuits \"3-8\" repetitions at \"usually more than 70% of 1RM\" ([[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). Those are station prescriptions for circuits; the figures here are for interval sets. They differ (for example, 50-60% to failure here against more than 70% for 3-8 reps there) but they answer different questions.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: incomplete rest forces the athlete \"to cope with increasing exhaustion and thereby develop muscular endurance\"; below about 40% of maximum arterial flow is not restricted, so aerobic processes predominate; at 50-60% flow is inhibited and anaerobic processes predominate (PDF p. 409). They give no mechanism for cluster sets beyond classing them as interval training. Chapter 3 adds that a series of single repetitions costs more energy than a continuous set with the same load ([[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]).\n\n**When to apply** — Extensive intervals or circuits for general strength-endurance and recovery capacity in a general preparation phase, built on large muscle groups. Intensive intervals for short-duration strength-endurance. Cluster sets when the aim is more repetitions at a heavy load than one continuous set allows. Eric's baseline (line 182): \"incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: intensive intervals to failure at 50-60% with short rest are close to glycolytic work, so by that rule they would not share a block with a maximal-strength emphasis. The authors' \"75% of (220-Age)\" is an aerobic conditioning target; Eric's baseline (line 119) uses age-based maximum heart rate for the anaerobic threshold at \"about 85% of that\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q20)**. Inference: the two use the same formula for different targets and do not conflict.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Because of increasing fatigue and the greater likelihood of injury under such conditions, exercises that require high levels of skill are not recommended\" (PDF p. 409); keep Olympic lifts and other technical lifts out of endurance intervals. Do not set intensive cluster loads from the book without choosing between its two load statements (5RM minimum against 75-90% of 1RM). Small single-joint exercises will not raise heart rate enough for extensive intervals, by the authors' own example (PDF p. 409).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own extensive interval prescription: 30-40% of 1RM, 5-6 sets of 20-30 repetitions, 1-2 minutes' rest, using exercises for the quadriceps, glutei, pectorals and erector spinae rather than curls, calf raises or push-downs (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]]; [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]; [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (rest-pause and the weightlifting interval set); [[framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids]] (stripping); [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3244,"cache_read_input_tokens":234347,"output_tokens":5383,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":46},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3244},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5383,"cache_read_input_tokens":234347,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3244,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3244,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01AYuJaoKFLZuMv8HqR78UMb":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: interval resistance and cluster sets\ncategory: [strength-endurance, interval-training, cluster-sets, loading]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Interval Resistance Training (Extensive and Intensive) and Cluster Sets\n\n**What it is** — The authors define interval resistance training by analogy with track: \"the primary aim of interval training is generally to develop strength-endurance by means of systematically alternating work and recovery phases\", with rest \"(preferably active rest)\" too short for full recovery \"so that the athlete is forced to cope with increasing exhaustion\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398, checked against the page image). They split it into two broad categories, \"one to increase strength or power and the other to increase endurance\", and the endurance category into two methods (PDF p. 409):\n\n| | Extensive interval | Intensive interval |\n|---|---|---|\n| Develops | \"medium duration strength-endurance\" and recovery capacity | \"short-duration strength-endurance\" and recovery capacity |\n| Load | \"30-40% of 1RM\" | \"50-60% of 1RM\" |\n| Sets x reps | \"5-6 sets of 20-30 repetitions\" | \"3-6 sets, where each set is performed to failure\" |\n| Rest | \"1-2 minutes\" | \"10-90 seconds\", by exercise, load, fitness and loading duration |\n| Energy | below 40% \"arterial blood flow is not restricted\" and \"'aerobic' energy processes predominate\" | higher loads \"inhibit arterial blood flow and anaerobic energy processes predominate\" |\n| Heart rate | large muscle groups needed to reach the level \"recommended for aerobic conditioning, e.g. 75% of (220-Age)\" | \"can readily increase heart rates to over 180 beats/min\" |\n| Circuits | \"Specially designed circuit training programmes\" can serve | special circuits with \"1-3 minutes\" between circuits |\n\nFor extensive work the authors want \"the larger muscle groups such as the quadriceps, glutei, pectorals and erector spinae\"; \"bicep curls, calf raises and tricep push-downs play a minimal role in elevating the heart rate\" (PDF p. 409).\n\n**Cluster training** is \"another form of interval training\": one or more repetitions \"with a 10-20 second interval between each repetition or cluster of repetitions in an extended set\". \"Characteristically, the minimum load used is one's 5RM and 4-6 sets are performed\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398). *Extensive clustering*: \"4-6 repetitions with one's 4-6RM, with 10 second rest intervals between each cluster\", or stripping-style with the load cut \"by 10-20%\" and a 10 s rest after each set (PDF pp. 409-410). *Intensive clustering*: \"4-6 sets of only one repetition with 75-90% of 1RM, with about a 20 second rest between repetitions for 4-6 repetitions\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, image). That last sentence is unclear as printed: it gives both \"sets of only one repetition\" and \"4-6 repetitions\" without saying how they nest.\n\n**Why `contested`.** The minimum load for cluster training is \"one's 5RM\" (PDF p. 409), but intensive clustering uses \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). Both state the load for the same method. Inference: under the usual relation between repetition maximum and %1RM, a 5RM is well above 75% of 1RM, so the lower end of the intensive range falls below the stated minimum. The book does not reconcile them.\n\nA second internal conflict touches this section: the weightlifting method rests \"1-5 minutes\" between singles (PDF p. 406), while this section says weightlifting and powerlifting rely on near-maximal singles \"with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409); see [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]].\n\n**Different method, not a conflict.** Chapter 5's circuit guidelines give aerobic circuits \"20-40\" repetitions at \"usually not more than 30% of 1RM\" and anaerobic circuits \"3-8\" repetitions at \"usually more than 70% of 1RM\" ([[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). Those are station prescriptions for circuits; the figures here are for interval sets. They differ (for example, 50-60% to failure here against more than 70% for 3-8 reps there) but they answer different questions.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: incomplete rest forces the athlete \"to cope with increasing exhaustion and thereby develop muscular endurance\"; below about 40% of maximum arterial flow is not restricted, so aerobic processes predominate; at 50-60% flow is inhibited and anaerobic processes predominate (PDF p. 409). They give no mechanism for cluster sets beyond classing them as interval training. Chapter 3 adds that a series of single repetitions costs more energy than a continuous set with the same load ([[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]).\n\n**When to apply** — Extensive intervals or circuits for general strength-endurance and recovery capacity in a general preparation phase, built on large muscle groups. Intensive intervals for short-duration strength-endurance. Cluster sets when the aim is more repetitions at a heavy load than one continuous set allows. Eric's baseline (line 182): \"incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: intensive intervals to failure at 50-60% with short rest are close to glycolytic work, so by that rule they would not share a block with a maximal-strength emphasis. The authors' \"75% of (220-Age)\" is an aerobic conditioning target; Eric's baseline (line 119) uses age-based maximum heart rate for the anaerobic threshold at \"about 85% of that\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q20)**. Inference: the two use the same formula for different targets and do not conflict.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Because of increasing fatigue and the greater likelihood of injury under such conditions, exercises that require high levels of skill are not recommended\" (PDF p. 409); keep Olympic lifts and other technical lifts out of endurance intervals. Do not set intensive cluster loads from the book without choosing between its two load statements (5RM minimum against 75-90% of 1RM). Small single-joint exercises will not raise heart rate enough for extensive intervals, by the authors' own example (PDF p. 409).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own extensive interval prescription: 30-40% of 1RM, 5-6 sets of 20-30 repetitions, 1-2 minutes' rest, using exercises for the quadriceps, glutei, pectorals and erector spinae rather than curls, calf raises or push-downs (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]]; [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]; [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (rest-pause and the weightlifting interval set); [[framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids]] (stripping); [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffpr9XoxT1JxReuSD7vF","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"2200536d-6a07-4309-af35-db45589df3cd","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:06:12.884Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"2200536d-6a07-4309-af35-db45589df3cd","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a2b72ded-2f77-4788-be97-76a7696d93d6","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AYuJaoKFLZuMv8HqR78UMb","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"adf997fd-b05c-4edf-b9ec-b8e5c43e1ac6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:06:12.985Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: interval resistance and cluster sets\ncategory: [strength-endurance, interval-training, cluster-sets, loading]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Interval Resistance Training (Extensive and Intensive) and Cluster Sets\n\n**What it is** — The authors define interval resistance training by analogy with track: \"the primary aim of interval training is generally to develop strength-endurance by means of systematically alternating work and recovery phases\", with rest \"(preferably active rest)\" too short for full recovery \"so that the athlete is forced to cope with increasing exhaustion\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398, checked against the page image). They split it into two broad categories, \"one to increase strength or power and the other to increase endurance\", and the endurance category into two methods (PDF p. 409):\n\n| | Extensive interval | Intensive interval |\n|---|---|---|\n| Develops | \"medium duration strength-endurance\" and recovery capacity | \"short-duration strength-endurance\" and recovery capacity |\n| Load | \"30-40% of 1RM\" | \"50-60% of 1RM\" |\n| Sets x reps | \"5-6 sets of 20-30 repetitions\" | \"3-6 sets, where each set is performed to failure\" |\n| Rest | \"1-2 minutes\" | \"10-90 seconds\", by exercise, load, fitness and loading duration |\n| Energy | below 40% \"arterial blood flow is not restricted\" and \"'aerobic' energy processes predominate\" | higher loads \"inhibit arterial blood flow and anaerobic energy processes predominate\" |\n| Heart rate | large muscle groups needed to reach the level \"recommended for aerobic conditioning, e.g. 75% of (220-Age)\" | \"can readily increase heart rates to over 180 beats/min\" |\n| Circuits | \"Specially designed circuit training programmes\" can serve | special circuits with \"1-3 minutes\" between circuits |\n\nFor extensive work the authors want \"the larger muscle groups such as the quadriceps, glutei, pectorals and erector spinae\"; \"bicep curls, calf raises and tricep push-downs play a minimal role in elevating the heart rate\" (PDF p. 409).\n\n**Cluster training** is \"another form of interval training\": one or more repetitions \"with a 10-20 second interval between each repetition or cluster of repetitions in an extended set\". \"Characteristically, the minimum load used is one's 5RM and 4-6 sets are performed\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398). *Extensive clustering*: \"4-6 repetitions with one's 4-6RM, with 10 second rest intervals between each cluster\", or stripping-style with the load cut \"by 10-20%\" and a 10 s rest after each set (PDF pp. 409-410). *Intensive clustering*: \"4-6 sets of only one repetition with 75-90% of 1RM, with about a 20 second rest between repetitions for 4-6 repetitions\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, image). That last sentence is unclear as printed: it gives both \"sets of only one repetition\" and \"4-6 repetitions\" without saying how they nest.\n\n**Why `contested`.** The minimum load for cluster training is \"one's 5RM\" (PDF p. 409), but intensive clustering uses \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). Both state the load for the same method. Inference: under the usual relation between repetition maximum and %1RM, a 5RM is well above 75% of 1RM, so the lower end of the intensive range falls below the stated minimum. The book does not reconcile them.\n\nA second internal conflict touches this section: the weightlifting method rests \"1-5 minutes\" between singles (PDF p. 406), while this section says weightlifting and powerlifting rely on near-maximal singles \"with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409); see [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]].\n\n**Different method, not a conflict.** Chapter 5's circuit guidelines give aerobic circuits \"20-40\" repetitions at \"usually not more than 30% of 1RM\" and anaerobic circuits \"3-8\" repetitions at \"usually more than 70% of 1RM\" ([[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). Those are station prescriptions for circuits; the figures here are for interval sets. They differ (for example, 50-60% to failure here against more than 70% for 3-8 reps there) but they answer different questions.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: incomplete rest forces the athlete \"to cope with increasing exhaustion and thereby develop muscular endurance\"; below about 40% of maximum arterial flow is not restricted, so aerobic processes predominate; at 50-60% flow is inhibited and anaerobic processes predominate (PDF p. 409). They give no mechanism for cluster sets beyond classing them as interval training. Chapter 3 adds that a series of single repetitions costs more energy than a continuous set with the same load ([[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]).\n\n**When to apply** — Extensive intervals or circuits for general strength-endurance and recovery capacity in a general preparation phase, built on large muscle groups. Intensive intervals for short-duration strength-endurance. Cluster sets when the aim is more repetitions at a heavy load than one continuous set allows. Eric's baseline (line 182): \"incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: intensive intervals to failure at 50-60% with short rest are close to glycolytic work, so by that rule they would not share a block with a maximal-strength emphasis. The authors' \"75% of (220-Age)\" is an aerobic conditioning target; Eric's baseline (line 119) uses age-based maximum heart rate for the anaerobic threshold at \"about 85% of that\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q20)**. Inference: the two use the same formula for different targets and do not conflict.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Because of increasing fatigue and the greater likelihood of injury under such conditions, exercises that require high levels of skill are not recommended\" (PDF p. 409); keep Olympic lifts and other technical lifts out of endurance intervals. Do not set intensive cluster loads from the book without choosing between its two load statements (5RM minimum against 75-90% of 1RM). Small single-joint exercises will not raise heart rate enough for extensive intervals, by the authors' own example (PDF p. 409).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own extensive interval prescription: 30-40% of 1RM, 5-6 sets of 20-30 repetitions, 1-2 minutes' rest, using exercises for the quadriceps, glutei, pectorals and erector spinae rather than curls, calf raises or push-downs (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]]; [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]; [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (rest-pause and the weightlifting interval set); [[framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids]] (stripping); [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2200536d-6a07-4309-af35-db45589df3cd","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"adf997fd-b05c-4edf-b9ec-b8e5c43e1ac6","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14757024 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"38232f02-f313-4066-b6c1-200df754ca35","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:06:12.995Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14757024 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"38232f02-f313-4066-b6c1-200df754ca35","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpuqaBftaTX3FpvJs3e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5649,"cache_read_input_tokens":237591,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5649,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1258,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffpuXfRTi849ygzHNW7p","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"0fee18ed-a092-4bf5-8708-617e01fb7a1e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:06:19.734Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"0fee18ed-a092-4bf5-8708-617e01fb7a1e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpuqaBftaTX3FpvJs3e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014LomS7pYDXHKBx1CfeTVLx","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: hybrid exercises and sets\ncategory: [exercise-selection, complexes, weightlifting, programming]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Hybrid Training: Several Movements in One Repetition, or a Different Movement Within One Set\n\n**What it is** — \"Hybrid Training ... refers to exercise in which each repetition consists of two or more separate movements between the starting and end points\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, checked against the page image). The clean-and-jerk, \"a pull from the ground, with a front squat, followed by a thrust overhead\", is \"probably the best known hybrid exercise\". The method also covers \"the *hybrid set*, which comprises a different movement for each repetition of the set\" (PDF p. 410).\n\nThe authors' examples (PDF p. 410):\n- Hybrid repetitions: barbell or dumbbell curl + overhead standing press; back or front squat + press (or push press or jerk); squat + good morning; dumbbell lateral raise (palms pronated) + upright row; dumbbell bench press + bent-elbow pullover (or flye).\n- Hybrid sets: reps 1-5 dumbbell press, 6-10 dumbbell curl, 11-15 lateral raise; reps 1-5 narrow-grip bench press, 6-10 wide-grip; reps 1-5 squat, 6-15 calf raise; reps 1-5 squat, 6-10 overhead press or push press; reps 1-5 dumbbell bench press, 6-10 bent-elbow flye, 11-15 bent-elbow pullover.\n- Unequal counts are allowed: \"Reps 1-3: Power clean from the hang (3 reps); Reps 4-8: front squat (5 reps); Reps 9-10: push press (2 reps)\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). Using \"the recording convention in 6.6.2\", this is written as 80 with a superscript 3 over \"3+5+2\", \"for a bar loaded to 80kg, for a set of 3 repetitions\" (PDF p. 411, image). Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions, so the \"3\" probably refers to something else (the superscript may be a set count), and the text does not say.\n\nGuidelines \"to avoid injury for this demanding type of training\" (PDF p. 411): \"The load must be selected so that it can be performed without excessive strain in the weakest movement of the hybrid\", and \"The order of movements should always be arranged to permit their safe and controlled execution without causing exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading.\" A larger list of hybrid exercises is promised in Ch 8.4.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons: the clean-and-jerk \"exercises an extensive variety of muscle groups\", and \"It is doubtful if any manoeuvre in any other sport rivals the clean-and-jerk or clean-and-push-press in recruiting maximal or near maximal contraction in so many muscle groups in such a short time\". They cite their own Table 3.12 for the clean-and-jerk costing \"over 60kJ per maximal lift, higher than all other lifts\" (book's cross-reference, not checked for this entry). They attribute this to hybrid exercises involving \"more joints of the body moving through a greater number of degrees of freedom\", which gives \"a more concentrated form of circuit training\": \"Instead of doing three separate exercises at three different stations, one can do all of them in a single repetition or at a single station\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399).\n\n**When to apply** — When session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar; when a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors' use). Eric's baseline (line 104): \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: clean-based hybrids such as hang power clean, front squat and push press fit that rule; the curl and lateral-raise hybrids do not.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — When the load is set by the strongest movement: the authors require it to suit \"the weakest movement of the hybrid\" (PDF p. 411), so the stronger movements are under-loaded. Inference: that makes hybrids a poor choice when the aim is maximal load in one of the movements. Not in an order that brings a technical movement after a fatiguing one; the authors rule out orders that cause \"exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading\" (PDF p. 411). Do not cite the 60 kJ figure from this entry; it is the book's pointer to Table 3.12, not checked here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own unequal hybrid set: hang power clean for 3, front squat for 5, push press for 2, with an 80 kg bar (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). The load is limited by the weakest of the three movements, by the authors' rule.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]] (hybrids under Multi-purpose Training); [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]; [[heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5649,"cache_read_input_tokens":237591,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5649,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_014LomS7pYDXHKBx1CfeTVLx":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: hybrid exercises and sets\ncategory: [exercise-selection, complexes, weightlifting, programming]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Hybrid Training: Several Movements in One Repetition, or a Different Movement Within One Set\n\n**What it is** — \"Hybrid Training ... refers to exercise in which each repetition consists of two or more separate movements between the starting and end points\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, checked against the page image). The clean-and-jerk, \"a pull from the ground, with a front squat, followed by a thrust overhead\", is \"probably the best known hybrid exercise\". The method also covers \"the *hybrid set*, which comprises a different movement for each repetition of the set\" (PDF p. 410).\n\nThe authors' examples (PDF p. 410):\n- Hybrid repetitions: barbell or dumbbell curl + overhead standing press; back or front squat + press (or push press or jerk); squat + good morning; dumbbell lateral raise (palms pronated) + upright row; dumbbell bench press + bent-elbow pullover (or flye).\n- Hybrid sets: reps 1-5 dumbbell press, 6-10 dumbbell curl, 11-15 lateral raise; reps 1-5 narrow-grip bench press, 6-10 wide-grip; reps 1-5 squat, 6-15 calf raise; reps 1-5 squat, 6-10 overhead press or push press; reps 1-5 dumbbell bench press, 6-10 bent-elbow flye, 11-15 bent-elbow pullover.\n- Unequal counts are allowed: \"Reps 1-3: Power clean from the hang (3 reps); Reps 4-8: front squat (5 reps); Reps 9-10: push press (2 reps)\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). Using \"the recording convention in 6.6.2\", this is written as 80 with a superscript 3 over \"3+5+2\", \"for a bar loaded to 80kg, for a set of 3 repetitions\" (PDF p. 411, image). Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions, so the \"3\" probably refers to something else (the superscript may be a set count), and the text does not say.\n\nGuidelines \"to avoid injury for this demanding type of training\" (PDF p. 411): \"The load must be selected so that it can be performed without excessive strain in the weakest movement of the hybrid\", and \"The order of movements should always be arranged to permit their safe and controlled execution without causing exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading.\" A larger list of hybrid exercises is promised in Ch 8.4.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons: the clean-and-jerk \"exercises an extensive variety of muscle groups\", and \"It is doubtful if any manoeuvre in any other sport rivals the clean-and-jerk or clean-and-push-press in recruiting maximal or near maximal contraction in so many muscle groups in such a short time\". They cite their own Table 3.12 for the clean-and-jerk costing \"over 60kJ per maximal lift, higher than all other lifts\" (book's cross-reference, not checked for this entry). They attribute this to hybrid exercises involving \"more joints of the body moving through a greater number of degrees of freedom\", which gives \"a more concentrated form of circuit training\": \"Instead of doing three separate exercises at three different stations, one can do all of them in a single repetition or at a single station\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399).\n\n**When to apply** — When session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar; when a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors' use). Eric's baseline (line 104): \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: clean-based hybrids such as hang power clean, front squat and push press fit that rule; the curl and lateral-raise hybrids do not.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — When the load is set by the strongest movement: the authors require it to suit \"the weakest movement of the hybrid\" (PDF p. 411), so the stronger movements are under-loaded. Inference: that makes hybrids a poor choice when the aim is maximal load in one of the movements. Not in an order that brings a technical movement after a fatiguing one; the authors rule out orders that cause \"exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading\" (PDF p. 411). Do not cite the 60 kJ figure from this entry; it is the book's pointer to Table 3.12, not checked here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own unequal hybrid set: hang power clean for 3, front squat for 5, push press for 2, with an 80 kg bar (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). The load is limited by the weakest of the three movements, by the authors' rule.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]] (hybrids under Multi-purpose Training); [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]; [[heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffpuXfRTi849ygzHNW7p","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d6a69113-ddcf-4bbb-95ea-7c1e62150633","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:06:35.461Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d6a69113-ddcf-4bbb-95ea-7c1e62150633","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a2b72ded-2f77-4788-be97-76a7696d93d6","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014LomS7pYDXHKBx1CfeTVLx","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"eebf1252-8f5e-4b46-9cca-4868ed45321d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:06:35.576Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: hybrid exercises and sets\ncategory: [exercise-selection, complexes, weightlifting, programming]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Hybrid Training: Several Movements in One Repetition, or a Different Movement Within One Set\n\n**What it is** — \"Hybrid Training ... refers to exercise in which each repetition consists of two or more separate movements between the starting and end points\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, checked against the page image). The clean-and-jerk, \"a pull from the ground, with a front squat, followed by a thrust overhead\", is \"probably the best known hybrid exercise\". The method also covers \"the *hybrid set*, which comprises a different movement for each repetition of the set\" (PDF p. 410).\n\nThe authors' examples (PDF p. 410):\n- Hybrid repetitions: barbell or dumbbell curl + overhead standing press; back or front squat + press (or push press or jerk); squat + good morning; dumbbell lateral raise (palms pronated) + upright row; dumbbell bench press + bent-elbow pullover (or flye).\n- Hybrid sets: reps 1-5 dumbbell press, 6-10 dumbbell curl, 11-15 lateral raise; reps 1-5 narrow-grip bench press, 6-10 wide-grip; reps 1-5 squat, 6-15 calf raise; reps 1-5 squat, 6-10 overhead press or push press; reps 1-5 dumbbell bench press, 6-10 bent-elbow flye, 11-15 bent-elbow pullover.\n- Unequal counts are allowed: \"Reps 1-3: Power clean from the hang (3 reps); Reps 4-8: front squat (5 reps); Reps 9-10: push press (2 reps)\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). Using \"the recording convention in 6.6.2\", this is written as 80 with a superscript 3 over \"3+5+2\", \"for a bar loaded to 80kg, for a set of 3 repetitions\" (PDF p. 411, image). Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions, so the \"3\" probably refers to something else (the superscript may be a set count), and the text does not say.\n\nGuidelines \"to avoid injury for this demanding type of training\" (PDF p. 411): \"The load must be selected so that it can be performed without excessive strain in the weakest movement of the hybrid\", and \"The order of movements should always be arranged to permit their safe and controlled execution without causing exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading.\" A larger list of hybrid exercises is promised in Ch 8.4.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons: the clean-and-jerk \"exercises an extensive variety of muscle groups\", and \"It is doubtful if any manoeuvre in any other sport rivals the clean-and-jerk or clean-and-push-press in recruiting maximal or near maximal contraction in so many muscle groups in such a short time\". They cite their own Table 3.12 for the clean-and-jerk costing \"over 60kJ per maximal lift, higher than all other lifts\" (book's cross-reference, not checked for this entry). They attribute this to hybrid exercises involving \"more joints of the body moving through a greater number of degrees of freedom\", which gives \"a more concentrated form of circuit training\": \"Instead of doing three separate exercises at three different stations, one can do all of them in a single repetition or at a single station\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399).\n\n**When to apply** — When session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar; when a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors' use). Eric's baseline (line 104): \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: clean-based hybrids such as hang power clean, front squat and push press fit that rule; the curl and lateral-raise hybrids do not.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — When the load is set by the strongest movement: the authors require it to suit \"the weakest movement of the hybrid\" (PDF p. 411), so the stronger movements are under-loaded. Inference: that makes hybrids a poor choice when the aim is maximal load in one of the movements. Not in an order that brings a technical movement after a fatiguing one; the authors rule out orders that cause \"exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading\" (PDF p. 411). Do not cite the 60 kJ figure from this entry; it is the book's pointer to Table 3.12, not checked here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own unequal hybrid set: hang power clean for 3, front squat for 5, push press for 2, with an 80 kg bar (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). The load is limited by the weakest of the three movements, by the authors' rule.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]] (hybrids under Multi-purpose Training); [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]; [[heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d6a69113-ddcf-4bbb-95ea-7c1e62150633","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"eebf1252-8f5e-4b46-9cca-4868ed45321d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpuqaBftaTX3FpvJs3e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CyhnWJXt9SMqE7g9DiMYTW","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: segmental range training\ncategory: [partial-range, weightlifting, exercise-variation, technique]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Progressive and Random Segmental Training: Work the Range in Segments, as Rigert Did for the Pull\n\n**What it is** — *Progressive Segmental Training* divides \"the range of movement into 2-4 segments, then performing 3-7 repetitions in each successive segment\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, checked against the page image). The bodybuilding form is \"Twenty-Ones\": 7 repetitions over each third of a curl's range, \"(or frequently, over the full range of movement)\" for the last seven. The authors' weightlifting example: the Russian middle-heavyweight Rigert \"used this method for training the pull in the clean and the snatch\". In the clean pull \"the bar first was raised to just below the knees for 1-3 repetitions, next the bar was moved for 1-3 repetitions between this point and mid-thigh, and, finally, 1-3 repetitions were performed between mid-thigh and waist level with full shoulder shrug and extension onto the toes. Frequently, the set was completed with a full clean pull from the ground\" (PDF p. 410).\n\nA bodybuilding variant exercises the segments \"in a different or random order or pattern every set or workout to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime, which can diminish the training effect\"; the authors name it \"Random Segmental Training\" \"For want of a better term\" (PDF p. 410).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give a reason only for the random variant: varying the order limits \"early accommodation to a predictable regime, which can diminish the training effect\" (PDF p. 410). For the progressive form they give no mechanism. Inference: each segment of the pull can be practised with repetitions before the whole pull is joined up, which is how Rigert's set ends. Zatsiorsky's library entry on accommodation states the general law the random variant appeals to ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]).\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: for a lifter whose pull breaks down at one segment (for example below the knee or at mid-thigh), as segment practice that ends in the full lift, which is the structure of the authors' Rigert example. The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled. Eric's baseline (line 103) judges transfer by Dynamic Correspondence, including \"amplitude/direction of movement\" and \"region of force production\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**. Inference: segment work trains force in a chosen region of the range, so it fits that rule when the segment is the region the sport or lift needs.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Inference: not as the only pull work. Rigert's set \"Frequently\" ended with a full clean pull (PDF p. 410), and the book's Chapter 4 material warns that joint-angle-specific strength transfers narrowly ([[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]]). The authors give no evidence that segmental work improves the full lift; the Rigert account is an anecdote with no outcome reported.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Rigert's clean pull in three segments (floor to just below the knee, knee to mid-thigh, mid-thigh to waist with shrug and rise onto the toes), 1-3 repetitions each, often finished with a full pull from the ground (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (restricted range repetitions); [[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]]; [[heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]; [[framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5649,"cache_read_input_tokens":237591,"output_tokens":3722,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":193},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5649},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3722,"cache_read_input_tokens":237591,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5649,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5649,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01CyhnWJXt9SMqE7g9DiMYTW":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: segmental range training\ncategory: [partial-range, weightlifting, exercise-variation, technique]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Progressive and Random Segmental Training: Work the Range in Segments, as Rigert Did for the Pull\n\n**What it is** — *Progressive Segmental Training* divides \"the range of movement into 2-4 segments, then performing 3-7 repetitions in each successive segment\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, checked against the page image). The bodybuilding form is \"Twenty-Ones\": 7 repetitions over each third of a curl's range, \"(or frequently, over the full range of movement)\" for the last seven. The authors' weightlifting example: the Russian middle-heavyweight Rigert \"used this method for training the pull in the clean and the snatch\". In the clean pull \"the bar first was raised to just below the knees for 1-3 repetitions, next the bar was moved for 1-3 repetitions between this point and mid-thigh, and, finally, 1-3 repetitions were performed between mid-thigh and waist level with full shoulder shrug and extension onto the toes. Frequently, the set was completed with a full clean pull from the ground\" (PDF p. 410).\n\nA bodybuilding variant exercises the segments \"in a different or random order or pattern every set or workout to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime, which can diminish the training effect\"; the authors name it \"Random Segmental Training\" \"For want of a better term\" (PDF p. 410).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give a reason only for the random variant: varying the order limits \"early accommodation to a predictable regime, which can diminish the training effect\" (PDF p. 410). For the progressive form they give no mechanism. Inference: each segment of the pull can be practised with repetitions before the whole pull is joined up, which is how Rigert's set ends. Zatsiorsky's library entry on accommodation states the general law the random variant appeals to ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]).\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: for a lifter whose pull breaks down at one segment (for example below the knee or at mid-thigh), as segment practice that ends in the full lift, which is the structure of the authors' Rigert example. The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled. Eric's baseline (line 103) judges transfer by Dynamic Correspondence, including \"amplitude/direction of movement\" and \"region of force production\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**. Inference: segment work trains force in a chosen region of the range, so it fits that rule when the segment is the region the sport or lift needs.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Inference: not as the only pull work. Rigert's set \"Frequently\" ended with a full clean pull (PDF p. 410), and the book's Chapter 4 material warns that joint-angle-specific strength transfers narrowly ([[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]]). The authors give no evidence that segmental work improves the full lift; the Rigert account is an anecdote with no outcome reported.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Rigert's clean pull in three segments (floor to just below the knee, knee to mid-thigh, mid-thigh to waist with shrug and rise onto the toes), 1-3 repetitions each, often finished with a full pull from the ground (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (restricted range repetitions); [[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]]; [[heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]; [[framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffpuXfRTi849ygzHNW7p","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1d9cb0ee-46a5-4e7e-bb6b-c17fdd7e2196","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:06:48.609Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","