between mid-thigh and waist level with full shoulder shrug and extension onto the toes. Frequently, the set was completed with a full clean pull from the ground\" (PDF p. 410).\n\nA bodybuilding variant exercises the segments \"in a different or random order or pattern every set or workout to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime, which can diminish the training effect\"; the authors name it \"Random Segmental Training\" \"For want of a better term\" (PDF p. 410).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give a reason only for the random variant: varying the order limits \"early accommodation to a predictable regime, which can diminish the training effect\" (PDF p. 410). For the progressive form they give no mechanism. Inference: each segment of the pull can be practised with repetitions before the whole pull is joined up, which is how Rigert's set ends. Zatsiorsky's library entry on accommodation states the general law the random variant appeals to ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]).\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: for a lifter whose pull breaks down at one segment (for example below the knee or at mid-thigh), as segment practice that ends in the full lift, which is the structure of the authors' Rigert example. The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled. Eric's baseline (line 103) judges transfer by Dynamic Correspondence, including \"amplitude/direction of movement\" and \"region of force production\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**. Inference: segment work trains force in a chosen region of the range, so it fits that rule when the segment is the region the sport or lift needs.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Inference: not as the only pull work. Rigert's set \"Frequently\" ended with a full clean pull (PDF p. 410), and the book's Chapter 4 material warns that joint-angle-specific strength transfers narrowly ([[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]]). The authors give no evidence that segmental work improves the full lift; the Rigert account is an anecdote with no outcome reported.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Rigert's clean pull in three segments (floor to just below the knee, knee to mid-thigh, mid-thigh to waist with shrug and rise onto the toes), 1-3 repetitions each, often finished with a full pull from the ground (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (restricted range repetitions); [[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]]; [[heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]; [[framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1d9cb0ee-46a5-4e7e-bb6b-c17fdd7e2196","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
