{"parentUuid":"fd0dc0e2-bbc6-4b31-b5ce-4bcd66938f91","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a2b72ded-2f77-4788-be97-76a7696d93d6","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CuHouSryB5yf7aJfwNSxFd","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"c9ca7103-84cf-4456-8fee-64100f788dc1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:00.250Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training.md","oldString":"and the book's Chapter 4 material warns","newString":"and the book's Chapter 2 material warns","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: segmental range training\ncategory: [partial-range, weightlifting, exercise-variation, technique]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Progressive and Random Segmental Training: Work the Range in Segments, as Rigert Did for the Pull\n\n**What it is** — *Progressive Segmental Training* divides \"the range of movement into 2-4 segments, then performing 3-7 repetitions in each successive segment\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, checked against the page image). The bodybuilding form is \"Twenty-Ones\": 7 repetitions over each third of a curl's range, \"(or frequently, over the full range of movement)\" for the last seven. The authors' weightlifting example: the Russian middle-heavyweight Rigert \"used this method for training the pull in the clean and the snatch\". In the clean pull \"the bar first was raised to just below the knees for 1-3 repetitions, next the bar was moved for 1-3 repetitions between this point and mid-thigh, and, finally, 1-3 repetitions were performed between mid-thigh and waist level with full shoulder shrug and extension onto the toes. Frequently, the set was completed with a full clean pull from the ground\" (PDF p. 410).\n\nA bodybuilding variant exercises the segments \"in a different or random order or pattern every set or workout to minimise the possibility of early accommodation to a predictable regime, which can diminish the training effect\"; the authors name it \"Random Segmental Training\" \"For want of a better term\" (PDF p. 410).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give a reason only for the random variant: varying the order limits \"early accommodation to a predictable regime, which can diminish the training effect\" (PDF p. 410). For the progressive form they give no mechanism. Inference: each segment of the pull can be practised with repetitions before the whole pull is joined up, which is how Rigert's set ends. Zatsiorsky's library entry on accommodation states the general law the random variant appeals to ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]).\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: for a lifter whose pull breaks down at one segment (for example below the knee or at mid-thigh), as segment practice that ends in the full lift, which is the structure of the authors' Rigert example. The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled. Eric's baseline (line 103) judges transfer by Dynamic Correspondence, including \"amplitude/direction of movement\" and \"region of force production\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**. Inference: segment work trains force in a chosen region of the range, so it fits that rule when the segment is the region the sport or lift needs.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Inference: not as the only pull work. Rigert's set \"Frequently\" ended with a full clean pull (PDF p. 410), and the book's Chapter 4 material warns that joint-angle-specific strength transfers narrowly ([[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]]). The authors give no evidence that segmental work improves the full lift; the Rigert account is an anecdote with no outcome reported.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Rigert's clean pull in three segments (floor to just below the knee, knee to mid-thigh, mid-thigh to waist with shrug and rise onto the toes), 1-3 repetitions each, often finished with a full pull from the ground (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (restricted range repetitions); [[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]]; [[heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]; [[framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":21,"oldLines":7,"newStart":21,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Inference: for a lifter whose pull breaks down at one segment (for example below the knee or at mid-thigh), as segment practice that ends in the full lift, which is the structure of the authors' Rigert example. The random variant when a long block of one exercise has stalled. Eric's baseline (line 103) judges transfer by Dynamic Correspondence, including \"amplitude/direction of movement\" and \"region of force production\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**. Inference: segment work trains force in a chosen region of the range, so it fits that rule when the segment is the region the sport or lift needs."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Inference: not as the only pull work. Rigert's set \"Frequently\" ended with a full clean pull (PDF p. 410), and the book's Chapter 4 material warns that joint-angle-specific strength transfers narrowly ([[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]]). The authors give no evidence that segmental work improves the full lift; the Rigert account is an anecdote with no outcome reported.","+**When NOT to apply** — Inference: not as the only pull work. Rigert's set \"Frequently\" ended with a full clean pull (PDF p. 410), and the book's Chapter 2 material warns that joint-angle-specific strength transfers narrowly ([[claim--joint-angle-specific-strength-training-narrows-transfer]]). The authors give no evidence that segmental work improves the full lift; the Rigert account is an anecdote with no outcome reported."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: Rigert's clean pull in three segments (floor to just below the knee, knee to mid-thigh, mid-thigh to waist with shrug and rise onto the toes), 1-3 repetitions each, often finished with a full pull from the ground (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fd0dc0e2-bbc6-4b31-b5ce-4bcd66938f91","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c9ca7103-84cf-4456-8fee-64100f788dc1","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpxqLPLEtHCkVNsyXPr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0182N4Xxf73HEAkvPKyR2jy9","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: reactive contrast methods\ncategory: [power, explosive-strength, contrast-training, potentiation]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Contrast Methods: Load Release, Pneumatics and the Heavy-Light Set\n\n**What it is** — \"Reactive methods are intended to develop power and explosive strength by imposing specific demands on nervous processes.\" Besides plyometrics (5.2.4), the category includes the *Contrast Methods* (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400, checked against the page image):\n\n- **Load release:** perform an exercise with an added load, \"then releasing the load suddenly during the lift, so that the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\". The stripping method \"offers a non-explosive variation of this method\", and the term also covers the sudden release of a parachute towed by a runner, swimmer or cyclist.\n- **Pneumatics:** \"much the same as the preceding contrast method\", on a compressed-air machine whose pressure can be \"quickly increased or decreased at any stage of a movement at the touch of a button\", so resistance drops \"fairly rapidly, though not as rapidly as with the load release method\" (Keiser machines are named).\n- **Heavy-light set:** \"performing a low repetition heavy set, decreasing the load by about 20%, taking a rest of 10-20 seconds, then executing the lighter set as rapidly as possible for 3-5 repetitions.\"\n- **Static-dynamic method:** preliminary isometric tension raises the dynamic and explosive strength of the following action through the after-effect (3.3.1, 3.4.2); its size depends on \"the duration of static loading, muscular fatigue and the interval between the static stimulus and the subsequent action\". This method is atomized from Chapter 4 in [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]] and not repeated here.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: for load release, \"the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\"; for the heavy-light set, \"The nervous system remains briefly adapted to the heavy load if the rest interval is brief and the muscles are temporarily able to operate in this super-stimulated condition to produce a very powerful and rapid contraction with the lightened load\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). No study is cited on this page.\n\n**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lamber, 1965), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, text layer). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]].\n\n**When to apply** — For power and explosive-strength work in athletes with a strength base, when the aim is a faster lighter effort straight after a heavier one. The heavy-light set needs only a bar and a quick load change. The authors also list \"Use any of the contrast training methods discussed earlier\" as a way through a sticking point (item 9, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402). Inference: in Eric's in-season week (baseline line 186: \"Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**), a heavy-light pairing is a low-volume way to keep a fast element in the lighter Wednesday lift; this is not something the authors or Eric propose.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not expect a reliable acute boost: the book's own cited Western studies found none in several tasks (PDF p. 175). The heavy-light set depends on the rest staying brief (10-20 s, PDF p. 411); with longer rest the authors' stated mechanism no longer applies. Do not use the stripping method as a power method; the authors call it the \"non-explosive variation\" (PDF p. 411). Chapter 5 classes contrast methods among shock methods for experienced athletes, used only after overtraining is ruled out ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: an athlete does a dip or pull-up \"as powerfully as possible with a load grasped between the legs, then release[s] the load early during the next repetition and accelerate[s] rapidly upwards for the remainder of the movement\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]; [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]]; [[heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release]] (parachute release as a contrast method); [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":653,"cache_read_input_tokens":247224,"output_tokens":2286,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":653},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2286,"cache_read_input_tokens":247224,"cache_creation_input_tokens":653,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":653,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0182N4Xxf73HEAkvPKyR2jy9":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: reactive contrast methods\ncategory: [power, explosive-strength, contrast-training, potentiation]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Contrast Methods: Load Release, Pneumatics and the Heavy-Light Set\n\n**What it is** — \"Reactive methods are intended to develop power and explosive strength by imposing specific demands on nervous processes.\" Besides plyometrics (5.2.4), the category includes the *Contrast Methods* (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400, checked against the page image):\n\n- **Load release:** perform an exercise with an added load, \"then releasing the load suddenly during the lift, so that the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\". The stripping method \"offers a non-explosive variation of this method\", and the term also covers the sudden release of a parachute towed by a runner, swimmer or cyclist.\n- **Pneumatics:** \"much the same as the preceding contrast method\", on a compressed-air machine whose pressure can be \"quickly increased or decreased at any stage of a movement at the touch of a button\", so resistance drops \"fairly rapidly, though not as rapidly as with the load release method\" (Keiser machines are named).\n- **Heavy-light set:** \"performing a low repetition heavy set, decreasing the load by about 20%, taking a rest of 10-20 seconds, then executing the lighter set as rapidly as possible for 3-5 repetitions.\"\n- **Static-dynamic method:** preliminary isometric tension raises the dynamic and explosive strength of the following action through the after-effect (3.3.1, 3.4.2); its size depends on \"the duration of static loading, muscular fatigue and the interval between the static stimulus and the subsequent action\". This method is atomized from Chapter 4 in [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]] and not repeated here.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: for load release, \"the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\"; for the heavy-light set, \"The nervous system remains briefly adapted to the heavy load if the rest interval is brief and the muscles are temporarily able to operate in this super-stimulated condition to produce a very powerful and rapid contraction with the lightened load\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). No study is cited on this page.\n\n**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lamber, 1965), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, text layer). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]].\n\n**When to apply** — For power and explosive-strength work in athletes with a strength base, when the aim is a faster lighter effort straight after a heavier one. The heavy-light set needs only a bar and a quick load change. The authors also list \"Use any of the contrast training methods discussed earlier\" as a way through a sticking point (item 9, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402). Inference: in Eric's in-season week (baseline line 186: \"Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**), a heavy-light pairing is a low-volume way to keep a fast element in the lighter Wednesday lift; this is not something the authors or Eric propose.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not expect a reliable acute boost: the book's own cited Western studies found none in several tasks (PDF p. 175). The heavy-light set depends on the rest staying brief (10-20 s, PDF p. 411); with longer rest the authors' stated mechanism no longer applies. Do not use the stripping method as a power method; the authors call it the \"non-explosive variation\" (PDF p. 411). Chapter 5 classes contrast methods among shock methods for experienced athletes, used only after overtraining is ruled out ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: an athlete does a dip or pull-up \"as powerfully as possible with a load grasped between the legs, then release[s] the load early during the next repetition and accelerate[s] rapidly upwards for the remainder of the movement\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]; [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]]; [[heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release]] (parachute release as a contrast method); [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffpxdvHX8nLrJ8YDAPwX","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"69793d7a-2a4e-465b-9dcf-5a51bd9cba55","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:16.548Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"69793d7a-2a4e-465b-9dcf-5a51bd9cba55","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a2b72ded-2f77-4788-be97-76a7696d93d6","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0182N4Xxf73HEAkvPKyR2jy9","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"87102b3d-3a37-4b79-b061-cdbf77c523c6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:16.697Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: reactive contrast methods\ncategory: [power, explosive-strength, contrast-training, potentiation]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Contrast Methods: Load Release, Pneumatics and the Heavy-Light Set\n\n**What it is** — \"Reactive methods are intended to develop power and explosive strength by imposing specific demands on nervous processes.\" Besides plyometrics (5.2.4), the category includes the *Contrast Methods* (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400, checked against the page image):\n\n- **Load release:** perform an exercise with an added load, \"then releasing the load suddenly during the lift, so that the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\". The stripping method \"offers a non-explosive variation of this method\", and the term also covers the sudden release of a parachute towed by a runner, swimmer or cyclist.\n- **Pneumatics:** \"much the same as the preceding contrast method\", on a compressed-air machine whose pressure can be \"quickly increased or decreased at any stage of a movement at the touch of a button\", so resistance drops \"fairly rapidly, though not as rapidly as with the load release method\" (Keiser machines are named).\n- **Heavy-light set:** \"performing a low repetition heavy set, decreasing the load by about 20%, taking a rest of 10-20 seconds, then executing the lighter set as rapidly as possible for 3-5 repetitions.\"\n- **Static-dynamic method:** preliminary isometric tension raises the dynamic and explosive strength of the following action through the after-effect (3.3.1, 3.4.2); its size depends on \"the duration of static loading, muscular fatigue and the interval between the static stimulus and the subsequent action\". This method is atomized from Chapter 4 in [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]] and not repeated here.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: for load release, \"the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\"; for the heavy-light set, \"The nervous system remains briefly adapted to the heavy load if the rest interval is brief and the muscles are temporarily able to operate in this super-stimulated condition to produce a very powerful and rapid contraction with the lightened load\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). No study is cited on this page.\n\n**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lamber, 1965), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, text layer). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]].\n\n**When to apply** — For power and explosive-strength work in athletes with a strength base, when the aim is a faster lighter effort straight after a heavier one. The heavy-light set needs only a bar and a quick load change. The authors also list \"Use any of the contrast training methods discussed earlier\" as a way through a sticking point (item 9, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402). Inference: in Eric's in-season week (baseline line 186: \"Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**), a heavy-light pairing is a low-volume way to keep a fast element in the lighter Wednesday lift; this is not something the authors or Eric propose.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not expect a reliable acute boost: the book's own cited Western studies found none in several tasks (PDF p. 175). The heavy-light set depends on the rest staying brief (10-20 s, PDF p. 411); with longer rest the authors' stated mechanism no longer applies. Do not use the stripping method as a power method; the authors call it the \"non-explosive variation\" (PDF p. 411). Chapter 5 classes contrast methods among shock methods for experienced athletes, used only after overtraining is ruled out ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: an athlete does a dip or pull-up \"as powerfully as possible with a load grasped between the legs, then release[s] the load early during the next repetition and accelerate[s] rapidly upwards for the remainder of the movement\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]; [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]]; [[heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release]] (parachute release as a contrast method); [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"69793d7a-2a4e-465b-9dcf-5a51bd9cba55","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
