{"parentUuid":"0f1d3c0c-2b99-4a76-92a0-26b23fbe1a38","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_record","entries":[],"toolInputCopies":[{"id":"toolu_01CuHouSryB5yf7aJfwNSxFd","copy":"wire"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"fe5b146e-fb0c-40a1-b6d8-22d81dd04e8e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:21.191Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"fe5b146e-fb0c-40a1-b6d8-22d81dd04e8e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpzPQxfUZ2LZrJACMJd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2557,"cache_read_input_tokens":247877,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2557,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1060,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffpzEHXu8Sv7m5RyASCq","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"3eca0e8b-997a-484c-a10c-a4dfe8e5599a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:21.190Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"3eca0e8b-997a-484c-a10c-a4dfe8e5599a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpzPQxfUZ2LZrJACMJd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014oVyrnVFfqPzwVzB7EgDRK","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: bodybuilding set variations\ncategory: [bodybuilding, hypertrophy, exercise-order, variation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Miscellaneous Bodybuilding Set Variations, Framed as Novelty for a Hypertrophy Phase\n\n**What it is** — The authors introduce a group of \"Miscellaneous Methods\" with a frame: bodybuilding methods \"are of minimal significance for developing most of the specific functional types of strength fitness required in competitive sport\", so they give \"only a brief summary ... to provide the coach with working definitions of methods that he may wish to apply in an hypertrophy phase\". \"The intention of most of these methods is to offer a novel variation to shock the body or particular muscle group out of any prevailing state of habituation or stagnation\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401, checked against the page image). The methods (PDF pp. 410, 412-413 / printed pp. 399, 401-402):\n\n- **Strict sets:** precise form with no momentum or cheating; \"It is advisable that all beginners learn first with strict sets to create the appropriate neuromuscular patterns of control before moving to other variations.\"\n- **Muscle priority sets:** the \"most undeveloped muscle groups are exercised first\" while energy and motivation are highest.\n- **Major to minor muscle sets:** largest muscles first, smaller later. \"Instructors often are taught that this is the most effective way to train and this is generally acceptable for the beginner in the general preparation phase, but it should not be stipulated as general principle for all training.\" Reasons to deviate: pre-exhaustion of \"stubborn muscles\", muscle priority, recovery from a previous workout on the same muscles, two or more workouts a day, and \"principles of *functional priority* discussed earlier in this text (i.e. technical skills > speed-strength > strength-speed > strength > strength-endurance > general endurance)\".\n- **Alternating sets:** staggered (major-minor) supersets that use the long rests of major-muscle sets; upper-lower body sets; agonist-antagonist sets (for example triceps then biceps).\n- **Tempo changing sets:** vary one repetition's speed, or the relative durations of the concentric, isometric and eccentric phases; \"like any other forms that stimulate the body in an unexpected way\", of possible value against \"habituation and stagnation (see Fig 1.32)\".\n- **Diminishing sets:** \"usually 100\" repetitions with one load in as few sets as possible, for example \"30-25-20-15-10\"; a newcomer may need \"10-12 sets\"; add load once 100 repetitions take \"4-5 sets\".\n- **Rep pausing sets:** a short break inside a set to recover, ease effort or restore motivation.\n- **Holding sets:** a static hold during or at the end of a movement, for muscles, \"tendons and joint capsules\", and balance in overhead lifts.\n- **Cumulative sets:** start with one repetition and add one each set until a set to failure; \"Usually ... one's 10RM, with 10 second rest intervals\".\n- **Peak contraction sets:** finish each repetition \"at the point of maximal muscle contraction (see Figs 2.8, 2.9) and not at the end of range when the force has already diminished significantly\", with intense isometric or quasi-isometric effort there.\n- **Continuous tension sets** (PDF p. 410): smooth repetitions without bounce, cheating or pause, joints never fully locked; cycling and arm ergometry give continuous tension naturally.\n\n**The PHA claim.** Upper-lower body sets are \"sometimes inappropriately called peripheral heart action (PHA) sets\". The switch to heavy activation of one body part \"tends to promote reflexive relaxation of another distant body part, a process first described by Setchinov in the early 1900s\". Proponents believe PHA \"avoids engorging one muscle part with blood and encourages the development of circulatory fitness\". The authors reply: \"The fact is that all human resisted movement and not just PHA encourages peripheral circulation and PHA offers no superior cardio-circulatory benefits compared with other systems of resistance training\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401). They cite no study for this. In 5.6 the same Setchinov effect is used for restoration between circuit stations rather than relaxation ([[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]); the two descriptions differ in emphasis, not in direction.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' general mechanism is habituation: a method that \"stimulate[s] the body in an unexpected way\" may break \"habituation and stagnation\" after long training (PDF p. 412). Specific reasons: strict sets build \"neuromuscular patterns of control\" in beginners; muscle priority puts the weakest group where energy is highest; staggered sets use otherwise idle rest; peak contraction sets avoid ending repetitions where force \"has already diminished significantly\" (PDF pp. 412-413). No study is cited for any of them.\n\n**When to apply** — In a dedicated hypertrophy phase, as variation once ordinary sets have stalled (the authors' stated use). Strict sets first for every beginner. Eric's baseline (line 41): \"teach basic movement skills and basic lifts before anything advanced\" **Inferred (transcript + Week 1 slides)**, which matches the authors' strict-sets-first rule. Use the functional-priority order, not size of muscle, when a sport session mixes qualities: technical skills, then speed-strength, strength-speed, strength, strength-endurance, general endurance (PDF p. 412).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not as sport strength methods: the authors' own frame calls them \"of minimal significance\" for most sport-specific strength (PDF p. 412). Do not treat major-to-minor order as a rule beyond beginners in general preparation (PDF p. 412). Do not choose upper-lower alternation for a cardiovascular advantage; the authors deny one, though without evidence. Eric's baseline (line 109): \"prioritize movement quality and confidence-through-competence over chasing hypertrophy or 1RM numbers, especially early in a training cycle\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: novelty methods aimed at a stalled hypertrophy block belong later in a cycle, not early.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own diminishing set: 100 repetitions with one load as 30-25-20-15-10, with the load raised once the 100 take 4-5 sets (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]]; [[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]; [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[heuristic--plan-variation-to-avoid-restrictive-neural-patterns]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]; [[framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology]] (quasi-isometric)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2557,"cache_read_input_tokens":247877,"output_tokens":2759,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":176},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2557},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2759,"cache_read_input_tokens":247877,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2557,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2557,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_014oVyrnVFfqPzwVzB7EgDRK":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: bodybuilding set variations\ncategory: [bodybuilding, hypertrophy, exercise-order, variation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Miscellaneous Bodybuilding Set Variations, Framed as Novelty for a Hypertrophy Phase\n\n**What it is** — The authors introduce a group of \"Miscellaneous Methods\" with a frame: bodybuilding methods \"are of minimal significance for developing most of the specific functional types of strength fitness required in competitive sport\", so they give \"only a brief summary ... to provide the coach with working definitions of methods that he may wish to apply in an hypertrophy phase\". \"The intention of most of these methods is to offer a novel variation to shock the body or particular muscle group out of any prevailing state of habituation or stagnation\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401, checked against the page image). The methods (PDF pp. 410, 412-413 / printed pp. 399, 401-402):\n\n- **Strict sets:** precise form with no momentum or cheating; \"It is advisable that all beginners learn first with strict sets to create the appropriate neuromuscular patterns of control before moving to other variations.\"\n- **Muscle priority sets:** the \"most undeveloped muscle groups are exercised first\" while energy and motivation are highest.\n- **Major to minor muscle sets:** largest muscles first, smaller later. \"Instructors often are taught that this is the most effective way to train and this is generally acceptable for the beginner in the general preparation phase, but it should not be stipulated as general principle for all training.\" Reasons to deviate: pre-exhaustion of \"stubborn muscles\", muscle priority, recovery from a previous workout on the same muscles, two or more workouts a day, and \"principles of *functional priority* discussed earlier in this text (i.e. technical skills > speed-strength > strength-speed > strength > strength-endurance > general endurance)\".\n- **Alternating sets:** staggered (major-minor) supersets that use the long rests of major-muscle sets; upper-lower body sets; agonist-antagonist sets (for example triceps then biceps).\n- **Tempo changing sets:** vary one repetition's speed, or the relative durations of the concentric, isometric and eccentric phases; \"like any other forms that stimulate the body in an unexpected way\", of possible value against \"habituation and stagnation (see Fig 1.32)\".\n- **Diminishing sets:** \"usually 100\" repetitions with one load in as few sets as possible, for example \"30-25-20-15-10\"; a newcomer may need \"10-12 sets\"; add load once 100 repetitions take \"4-5 sets\".\n- **Rep pausing sets:** a short break inside a set to recover, ease effort or restore motivation.\n- **Holding sets:** a static hold during or at the end of a movement, for muscles, \"tendons and joint capsules\", and balance in overhead lifts.\n- **Cumulative sets:** start with one repetition and add one each set until a set to failure; \"Usually ... one's 10RM, with 10 second rest intervals\".\n- **Peak contraction sets:** finish each repetition \"at the point of maximal muscle contraction (see Figs 2.8, 2.9) and not at the end of range when the force has already diminished significantly\", with intense isometric or quasi-isometric effort there.\n- **Continuous tension sets** (PDF p. 410): smooth repetitions without bounce, cheating or pause, joints never fully locked; cycling and arm ergometry give continuous tension naturally.\n\n**The PHA claim.** Upper-lower body sets are \"sometimes inappropriately called peripheral heart action (PHA) sets\". The switch to heavy activation of one body part \"tends to promote reflexive relaxation of another distant body part, a process first described by Setchinov in the early 1900s\". Proponents believe PHA \"avoids engorging one muscle part with blood and encourages the development of circulatory fitness\". The authors reply: \"The fact is that all human resisted movement and not just PHA encourages peripheral circulation and PHA offers no superior cardio-circulatory benefits compared with other systems of resistance training\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401). They cite no study for this. In 5.6 the same Setchinov effect is used for restoration between circuit stations rather than relaxation ([[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]); the two descriptions differ in emphasis, not in direction.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' general mechanism is habituation: a method that \"stimulate[s] the body in an unexpected way\" may break \"habituation and stagnation\" after long training (PDF p. 412). Specific reasons: strict sets build \"neuromuscular patterns of control\" in beginners; muscle priority puts the weakest group where energy is highest; staggered sets use otherwise idle rest; peak contraction sets avoid ending repetitions where force \"has already diminished significantly\" (PDF pp. 412-413). No study is cited for any of them.\n\n**When to apply** — In a dedicated hypertrophy phase, as variation once ordinary sets have stalled (the authors' stated use). Strict sets first for every beginner. Eric's baseline (line 41): \"teach basic movement skills and basic lifts before anything advanced\" **Inferred (transcript + Week 1 slides)**, which matches the authors' strict-sets-first rule. Use the functional-priority order, not size of muscle, when a sport session mixes qualities: technical skills, then speed-strength, strength-speed, strength, strength-endurance, general endurance (PDF p. 412).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not as sport strength methods: the authors' own frame calls them \"of minimal significance\" for most sport-specific strength (PDF p. 412). Do not treat major-to-minor order as a rule beyond beginners in general preparation (PDF p. 412). Do not choose upper-lower alternation for a cardiovascular advantage; the authors deny one, though without evidence. Eric's baseline (line 109): \"prioritize movement quality and confidence-through-competence over chasing hypertrophy or 1RM numbers, especially early in a training cycle\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: novelty methods aimed at a stalled hypertrophy block belong later in a cycle, not early.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own diminishing set: 100 repetitions with one load as 30-25-20-15-10, with the load raised once the 100 take 4-5 sets (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]]; [[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]; [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[heuristic--plan-variation-to-avoid-restrictive-neural-patterns]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]; [[framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology]] (quasi-isometric)\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffpzEHXu8Sv7m5RyASCq","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"e2c4f0f5-f510-4767-8dc7-218d22233b2b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:41.676Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845