{"parentUuid":"e0b067f5-74d1-4fc0-b148-c31c47a60962","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffq2EqLFWBdZ9ww1oxYf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Xr4EtqQBMbpATSpMCmmuF1","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md","old_string":"([[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]); the two descriptions differ","new_string":"([[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]). Inference: the two descriptions differ"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2881,"cache_read_input_tokens":250434,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2881,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Xr4EtqQBMbpATSpMCmmuF1":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md","old_string":"([[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]); the two descriptions differ","new_string":"([[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]). Inference: the two descriptions differ"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffq25C9Egkz9u5oMMaVf","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c3f34fb8-8f57-4a4e-9339-ff95f764a867","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:47.681Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c3f34fb8-8f57-4a4e-9339-ff95f764a867","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"725cf014-e8e6-4e21-907d-d54c6c265ee5","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xr4EtqQBMbpATSpMCmmuF1","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"ec6e1c1c-116b-42d5-9ec7-e29cf55ec6ec","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:07:47.767Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md","oldString":"([[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]); the two descriptions differ","newString":"([[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]). Inference: the two descriptions differ","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: bodybuilding set variations\ncategory: [bodybuilding, hypertrophy, exercise-order, variation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Miscellaneous Bodybuilding Set Variations, Framed as Novelty for a Hypertrophy Phase\n\n**What it is** — The authors introduce a group of \"Miscellaneous Methods\" with a frame: bodybuilding methods \"are of minimal significance for developing most of the specific functional types of strength fitness required in competitive sport\", so they give \"only a brief summary ... to provide the coach with working definitions of methods that he may wish to apply in an hypertrophy phase\". \"The intention of most of these methods is to offer a novel variation to shock the body or particular muscle group out of any prevailing state of habituation or stagnation\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401, checked against the page image). The methods (PDF pp. 410, 412-413 / printed pp. 399, 401-402):\n\n- **Strict sets:** precise form with no momentum or cheating; \"It is advisable that all beginners learn first with strict sets to create the appropriate neuromuscular patterns of control before moving to other variations.\"\n- **Muscle priority sets:** the \"most undeveloped muscle groups are exercised first\" while energy and motivation are highest.\n- **Major to minor muscle sets:** largest muscles first, smaller later. \"Instructors often are taught that this is the most effective way to train and this is generally acceptable for the beginner in the general preparation phase, but it should not be stipulated as general principle for all training.\" Reasons to deviate: pre-exhaustion of \"stubborn muscles\", muscle priority, recovery from a previous workout on the same muscles, two or more workouts a day, and \"principles of *functional priority* discussed earlier in this text (i.e. technical skills > speed-strength > strength-speed > strength > strength-endurance > general endurance)\".\n- **Alternating sets:** staggered (major-minor) supersets that use the long rests of major-muscle sets; upper-lower body sets; agonist-antagonist sets (for example triceps then biceps).\n- **Tempo changing sets:** vary one repetition's speed, or the relative durations of the concentric, isometric and eccentric phases; \"like any other forms that stimulate the body in an unexpected way\", of possible value against \"habituation and stagnation (see Fig 1.32)\".\n- **Diminishing sets:** \"usually 100\" repetitions with one load in as few sets as possible, for example \"30-25-20-15-10\"; a newcomer may need \"10-12 sets\"; add load once 100 repetitions take \"4-5 sets\".\n- **Rep pausing sets:** a short break inside a set to recover, ease effort or restore motivation.\n- **Holding sets:** a static hold during or at the end of a movement, for muscles, \"tendons and joint capsules\", and balance in overhead lifts.\n- **Cumulative sets:** start with one repetition and add one each set until a set to failure; \"Usually ... one's 10RM, with 10 second rest intervals\".\n- **Peak contraction sets:** finish each repetition \"at the point of maximal muscle contraction (see Figs 2.8, 2.9) and not at the end of range when the force has already diminished significantly\", with intense isometric or quasi-isometric effort there.\n- **Continuous tension sets** (PDF p. 410): smooth repetitions without bounce, cheating or pause, joints never fully locked; cycling and arm ergometry give continuous tension naturally.\n\n**The PHA claim.** Upper-lower body sets are \"sometimes inappropriately called peripheral heart action (PHA) sets\". The switch to heavy activation of one body part \"tends to promote reflexive relaxation of another distant body part, a process first described by Setchinov in the early 1900s\". Proponents believe PHA \"avoids engorging one muscle part with blood and encourages the development of circulatory fitness\". The authors reply: \"The fact is that all human resisted movement and not just PHA encourages peripheral circulation and PHA offers no superior cardio-circulatory benefits compared with other systems of resistance training\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401). They cite no study for this. In 5.6 the same Setchinov effect is used for restoration between circuit stations rather than relaxation ([[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]); the two descriptions differ in emphasis, not in direction.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' general mechanism is habituation: a method that \"stimulate[s] the body in an unexpected way\" may break \"habituation and stagnation\" after long training (PDF p. 412). Specific reasons: strict sets build \"neuromuscular patterns of control\" in beginners; muscle priority puts the weakest group where energy is highest; staggered sets use otherwise idle rest; peak contraction sets avoid ending repetitions where force \"has already diminished significantly\" (PDF pp. 412-413). No study is cited for any of them.\n\n**When to apply** — In a dedicated hypertrophy phase, as variation once ordinary sets have stalled (the authors' stated use). Strict sets first for every beginner. Eric's baseline (line 41): \"teach basic movement skills and basic lifts before anything advanced\" **Inferred (transcript + Week 1 slides)**, which matches the authors' strict-sets-first rule. Use the functional-priority order, not size of muscle, when a sport session mixes qualities: technical skills, then speed-strength, strength-speed, strength, strength-endurance, general endurance (PDF p. 412).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not as sport strength methods: the authors' own frame calls them \"of minimal significance\" for most sport-specific strength (PDF p. 412). Do not treat major-to-minor order as a rule beyond beginners in general preparation (PDF p. 412). Do not choose upper-lower alternation for a cardiovascular advantage; the authors deny one, though without evidence. Eric's baseline (line 109): \"prioritize movement quality and confidence-through-competence over chasing hypertrophy or 1RM numbers, especially early in a training cycle\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: novelty methods aimed at a stalled hypertrophy block belong later in a cycle, not early.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own diminishing set: 100 repetitions with one load as 30-25-20-15-10, with the load raised once the 100 take 4-5 sets (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]]; [[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]; [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[heuristic--plan-variation-to-avoid-restrictive-neural-patterns]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]; [[framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology]] (quasi-isometric)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":27,"oldLines":7,"newStart":27,"newLines":7,"lines":[" - **Peak contraction sets:** finish each repetition \"at the point of maximal muscle contraction (see Figs 2.8, 2.9) and not at the end of range when the force has already diminished significantly\", with intense isometric or quasi-isometric effort there."," - **Continuous tension sets** (PDF p. 410): smooth repetitions without bounce, cheating or pause, joints never fully locked; cycling and arm ergometry give continuous tension naturally."," ","-**The PHA claim.** Upper-lower body sets are \"sometimes inappropriately called peripheral heart action (PHA) sets\". The switch to heavy activation of one body part \"tends to promote reflexive relaxation of another distant body part, a process first described by Setchinov in the early 1900s\". Proponents believe PHA \"avoids engorging one muscle part with blood and encourages the development of circulatory fitness\". The authors reply: \"The fact is that all human resisted movement and not just PHA encourages peripheral circulation and PHA offers no superior cardio-circulatory benefits compared with other systems of resistance training\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401). They cite no study for this. In 5.6 the same Setchinov effect is used for restoration between circuit stations rather than relaxation ([[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]); the two descriptions differ in emphasis, not in direction.","+**The PHA claim.** Upper-lower body sets are \"sometimes inappropriately called peripheral heart action (PHA) sets\". The switch to heavy activation of one body part \"tends to promote reflexive relaxation of another distant body part, a process first described by Setchinov in the early 1900s\". Proponents believe PHA \"avoids engorging one muscle part with blood and encourages the development of circulatory fitness\". The authors reply: \"The fact is that all human resisted movement and not just PHA encourages peripheral circulation and PHA offers no superior cardio-circulatory benefits compared with other systems of resistance training\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401). They cite no study for this. In 5.6 the same Setchinov effect is used for restoration between circuit stations rather than relaxation ([[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]). Inference: the two descriptions differ in emphasis, not in direction."," "," **Why it works** — The authors' general mechanism is habituation: a method that \"stimulate[s] the body in an unexpected way\" may break \"habituation and stagnation\" after long training (PDF p. 412). Specific reasons: strict sets build \"neuromuscular patterns of control\" in beginners; muscle priority puts the weakest group where energy is highest; staggered sets use otherwise idle rest; peak contraction sets avoid ending repetitions where force \"has already diminished significantly\" (PDF pp. 412-413). No study is cited for any of them."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c3f34fb8-8f57-4a4e-9339-ff95f764a867","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ec6e1c1c-116b-42d5-9ec7-e29cf55ec6ec","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffq2EqLFWBdZ9ww1oxYf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013cuf6Xgc4JNcbzzMTEpw1J","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: breaking strength plateaus\ncategory: [plateaus, loading, programming, motivation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Breaking a Sticking Point: The Authors' Repertoire of 27 Strategies\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Methods for Overcoming Barriers\" the authors call reaching a \"sticking point\" \"one of single most frustrating experiences in the life of any athlete\", which \"may lead to loss of form, loss of interest, decrease in motivation, the unnecessary or premature reliance on anabolic substances, an endless search for plausible ergogenic aids, injury or even the end of one's sporting career\", and offer \"a repertoire of various strategies\" (PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402, checked against the page image). The list is numbered 1 to 28 but has no item 19 (PDF p. 414 / printed p. 403, image), so it holds 27 strategies. Grouped here by kind (item numbers are the book's):\n\n- **Change the repetition and load pattern:** raise repetitions with near-maximal loads, \"try to increase your 2RM to a 3RM or your 3RM to a 5RM\" (1); less high-repetition work, more 1RM, 2RM or 3RM work (2); CAT \"with lighter loads\" (3); reach the maximum with fewer repetition sets and do the repetition work in a reverse pyramid (7).\n- **Change the increments:** \"Increase loads by unfamiliar increments, since sticking points often relate to the numerical value of the load which one associates with one's current 1RM\", for example 80-92.5-97.5-102.5 kg instead of 80-90-95-100 kg (4); bigger jumps between sets after an adequate warm-up, for example 60-80-100 kg instead of 60-70-80-90-100 kg, adding heavier sets or a plateau at 100 kg to restore volume (6); \"minimal increments\" of \"0.5 - 1.25kg\" added without counting them, sometimes slipped \"between the larger plates if you wish to help a colleague break through some largely psychological barrier\" (8).\n- **Change the warm-up and rests:** a \"less demanding warm-up which conserves energy for the heaviest lifts\" (5); rests that are \"optimal\", found by experiment or tests such as heart rate or motor control, while also training \"with different rest intervals\" because competition may not allow optimal rest (13).\n- **Use other methods:** contrast methods (9); supramaximal adaptation training such as supporting a load \"significantly heavier than your 1RM\", lock-outs or end-of-range repetitions (11); ballistic methods \"to move through a sticking point\" (20); loaded isometric holds \"at crucial points\" (21); assistance exercises for the weak area (26); forced repetitions (27); \"free weights combined with elastics or chains\" (28).\n- **Change the session or the programme:** change exercise order or duration (10); fewer exercises (15); more variation (16); \"shorter, more frequent workouts\" (17); modules of a few exercises, \"two or more modules per day\" (18); a basic periodisation programme if none is followed (25).\n- **Technique, rest and the mind:** alter technique with \"a skilled coach, together with video analysis\", but \"It is unwise to attempt any changes in technique close to any competitions, unless there is something seriously inefficient or unsafe\" (12); a short break from training (14) or from the problem exercise (24); formal restoration (22); \"appropriate mental strategies by consulting qualified specialists\" (23).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give reasons for a few items only: unfamiliar increments, because \"sticking points often relate to the numerical value of the load which one associates with one's current 1RM\" (4); minimal increments, for a \"largely psychological barrier\" (8); a short break, \"to overcome mental stagnation and facilitate restoration\" (14); rest experiments, because rests \"either too short or too long both may disrupt your ability to perform optimally\" (13); technique, because \"Sometimes your sticking point is due to imperfect technique\" (12) (PDF pp. 413-414). The list cites no study.\n\n**When to apply** — When a lift has stalled for weeks in a lifter with a training history, as a checklist to pick one or two changes at a time. Eric's baseline (line 30): \"Data collection has a cost in time, athlete compliance, and staff attention.\" **Explicit**. Inference: item 13's heart-rate or motor-control tests for rest length are a data cost of that kind, worth paying only for a lifter whose rests are a real question. Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**. Inference: items 1, 2, 4 and 8 are written around a known 1RM; with projected maxes the \"numerical value\" the athlete associates with a max may be a number they have never lifted.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not before ruling out overtraining. This list does not say so, but the book's Chapter 5 says lack of progress \"may be due to overtraining rather than long-term stagnation\", in which case a shock method is \"potentially harmful\" ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]); several items here (forced repetitions, supramaximal holds, contrast and ballistic methods) are on that shock list. Not technique changes near competition (item 12's own limit). Eric's baseline (line 142): \"Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design; abrupt, large changes are treated as a direct injury-risk driver\" **Inferred (transcript)**. Inference: item 6 (bigger jumps between sets) and item 11 (loads well above 1RM) are the kind of abrupt change that rule warns against, so in a team setting they need a strength base and close supervision.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: instead of working up to a 100 kg best via 80-90-95-100 kg, use 80-92.5-97.5-102.5 kg, so the athlete is not stopped by the familiar number (item 4, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]] (item 3); [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]] (item 9); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (items 11, 20, 27); [[framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids]] (item 7); [[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]] (item 28); [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]] (a different use of \"sticking point\": the weakest joint angle within one lift)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2881,"cache_read_input_tokens":250434,"output_tokens":2847,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":109},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2881},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2847,"cache_read_input_tokens":250434,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2881,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2881,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_013cuf6Xgc4JNcbzzMTEpw1J":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: breaking strength plateaus\ncategory: [plateaus, loading, programming, motivation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Breaking a Sticking Point: The Authors' Repertoire of 27 Strategies\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Methods for Overcoming Barriers\" the authors call reaching a \"sticking point\" \"one of single most frustrating experiences in the life of any athlete\", which \"may lead to loss of form, loss of interest, decrease in motivation, the unnecessary or premature reliance on anabolic substances, an endless search for plausible ergogenic aids, injury or even the end of one's sporting career\", and offer \"a repertoire of various strategies\" (PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402, checked against the page image). The list is numbered 1 to 28 but has no item 19 (PDF p. 414 / printed p. 403, image), so it holds 27 strategies. Grouped here by kind (item numbers are the book's):\n\n- **Change the repetition and load pattern:** raise repetitions with near-maximal loads, \"try to increase your 2RM to a 3RM or your 3RM to a 5RM\" (1); less high-repetition work, more 1RM, 2RM or 3RM work (2); CAT \"with lighter loads\" (3); reach the maximum with fewer repetition sets and do the repetition work in a reverse pyramid (7).\n- **Change the increments:** \"Increase loads by unfamiliar increments, since sticking points often relate to the numerical value of the load which one associates with one's current 1RM\", for example 80-92.5-97.5-102.5 kg instead of 80-90-95-100 kg (4); bigger jumps between sets after an adequate warm-up, for example 60-80-100 kg instead of 60-70-80-90-100 kg, adding heavier sets or a plateau at 100 kg to restore volume (6); \"minimal increments\" of \"0.5 - 1.25kg\" added without counting them, sometimes slipped \"between the larger plates if you wish to help a colleague break through some largely psychological barrier\" (8).\n- **Change the warm-up and rests:** a \"less demanding warm-up which conserves energy for the heaviest lifts\" (5); rests that are \"optimal\", found by experiment or tests such as heart rate or motor control, while also training \"with different rest intervals\" because competition may not allow optimal rest (13).\n- **Use other methods:** contrast methods (9); supramaximal adaptation training such as supporting a load \"significantly heavier than your 1RM\", lock-outs or end-of-range repetitions (11); ballistic methods \"to move through a sticking point\" (20); loaded isometric holds \"at crucial points\" (21); assistance exercises for the weak area (26); forced repetitions (27); \"free weights combined with elastics or chains\" (28).\n- **Change the session or the programme:** change exercise order or duration (10); fewer exercises (15); more variation (16); \"shorter, more frequent workouts\" (17); modules of a few exercises, \"two or more modules per day\" (18); a basic periodisation programme if none is followed (25).\n- **Technique, rest and the mind:** alter technique with \"a skilled coach, together with video analysis\", but \"It is unwise to attempt any changes in technique close to any competitions, unless there is something seriously inefficient or unsafe\" (12); a short break from training (14) or from the problem exercise (24); formal restoration (22); \"appropriate mental strategies by consulting qualified specialists\" (23).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give reasons for a few items only: unfamiliar increments, because \"sticking points often relate to the numerical value of the load which one associates with one's current 1RM\" (4); minimal increments, for a \"largely psychological barrier\" (8); a short break, \"to overcome mental stagnation and facilitate restoration\" (14); rest experiments, because rests \"either too short or too long both may disrupt your ability to perform optimally\" (13); technique, because \"Sometimes your sticking point is due to imperfect technique\" (12) (PDF pp. 413-414). The list cites no study.\n\n**When to apply** — When a lift has stalled for weeks in a lifter with a training history, as a checklist to pick one or two changes at a time. Eric's baseline (line 30): \"Data collection has a cost in time, athlete compliance, and staff attention.\" **Explicit**. Inference: item 13's heart-rate or motor-control tests for rest length are a data cost of that kind, worth paying only for a lifter whose rests are a real question. Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**. Inference: items 1, 2, 4 and 8 are written around a known 1RM; with projected maxes the \"numerical value\" the athlete associates with a max may be a number they have never lifted.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not before ruling out overtraining. This list does not say so, but the book's Chapter 5 says lack of progress \"may be due to overtraining rather than long-term stagnation\", in which case a shock method is \"potentially harmful\" ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]); several items here (forced repetitions, supramaximal holds, contrast and ballistic methods) are on that shock list. Not technique changes near competition (item 12's own limit). Eric's baseline (line 142): \"Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design; abrupt, large changes are treated as a direct injury-risk driver\" **Inferred (transcript)**. Inference: item 6 (bigger jumps between sets) and item 11 (loads well above 1RM) are the kind of abrupt change that rule warns against, so in a team setting they need a strength base and close supervision.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: instead of working up to a 100 kg best via 80-90-95-100 kg, use 80-92.5-97.5-102.5 kg, so the athlete is not stopped by the familiar number (item 4, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]] (item 3); [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]] (item 9); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (items 11, 20, 27); [[framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids]] (item 7); [[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]] (item 28); [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]] (a different use of \"sticking point\": the weakest joint angle within one lift)\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffq25C9Egkz9u5oMMaVf","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"dd397c2c-e59b-4e0b-9157-744978278d6a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:08:08.165Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"dd397c2c-e59b-4e0b-9157-744978278d6a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013cuf6Xgc4JNcbzzMTEpw1J","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"d8a47077-b499-49a0-940b-c23b341e1dfc","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:08:08.266Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--overcoming-sticking-points-repertoire-of-27-strategies.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: breaking strength plateaus\ncategory: [plateaus, loading, programming, motivation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Breaking a Sticking Point: The Authors' Repertoire of 27 Strategies\n\n**What it is** — Under \"Methods for Overcoming Barriers\" the authors call reaching a \"sticking point\" \"one of single most frustrating experiences in the life of any athlete\", which \"may lead to loss of form, loss of interest, decrease in motivation, the unnecessary or premature reliance on anabolic substances, an endless search for plausible ergogenic aids, injury or even the end of one's sporting career\", and offer \"a repertoire of various strategies\" (PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402, checked against the page image). The list is numbered 1 to 28 but has no item 19 (PDF p. 414 / printed p. 403, image), so it holds 27 strategies. Grouped here by kind (item numbers are the book's):\n\n- **Change the repetition and load pattern:** raise repetitions with near-maximal loads, \"try to increase your 2RM to a 3RM or your 3RM to a 5RM\" (1); less high-repetition work, more 1RM, 2RM or 3RM work (2); CAT \"with lighter loads\" (3); reach the maximum with fewer repetition sets and do the repetition work in a reverse pyramid (7).\n- **Change the increments:** \"Increase loads by unfamiliar increments, since sticking points often relate to the numerical value of the load which one associates with one's current 1RM\", for example 80-92.5-97.5-102.5 kg instead of 80-90-95-100 kg (4); bigger jumps between sets after an adequate warm-up, for example 60-80-100 kg instead of 60-70-80-90-100 kg, adding heavier sets or a plateau at 100 kg to restore volume (6); \"minimal increments\" of \"0.5 - 1.25kg\" added without counting them, sometimes slipped \"between the larger plates if you wish to help a colleague break through some largely psychological barrier\" (8).\n- **Change the warm-up and rests:** a \"less demanding warm-up which conserves energy for the heaviest lifts\" (5); rests that are \"optimal\", found by experiment or tests such as heart rate or motor control, while also training \"with different rest intervals\" because competition may not allow optimal rest (13).\n- **Use other methods:** contrast methods (9); supramaximal adaptation training such as supporting a load \"significantly heavier than your 1RM\", lock-outs or end-of-range repetitions (11); ballistic methods \"to move through a sticking point\" (20); loaded isometric holds \"at crucial points\" (21); assistance exercises for the weak area (26); forced repetitions (27); \"free weights combined with elastics or chains\" (28).\n- **Change the session or the programme:** change exercise order or duration (10); fewer exercises (15); more variation (16); \"shorter, more frequent workouts\" (17); modules of a few exercises, \"two or more modules per day\" (18); a basic periodisation programme if none is followed (25).\n- **Technique, rest and the mind:** alter technique with \"a skilled coach, together with video analysis\", but \"It is unwise to attempt any changes in technique close to any competitions, unless there is something seriously inefficient or unsafe\" (12); a short break from training (14) or from the problem exercise (24); formal restoration (22); \"appropriate mental strategies by consulting qualified specialists\" (23).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give reasons for a few items only: unfamiliar increments, because \"sticking points often relate to the numerical value of the load which one associates with one's current 1RM\" (4); minimal increments, for a \"largely psychological barrier\" (8); a short break, \"to overcome mental stagnation and facilitate restoration\" (14); rest experiments, because rests \"either too short or too long both may disrupt your ability to perform optimally\" (13); technique, because \"Sometimes your sticking point is due to imperfect technique\" (12) (PDF pp. 413-414). The list cites no study.\n\n**When to apply** — When a lift has stalled for weeks in a lifter with a training history, as a checklist to pick one or two changes at a time. Eric's baseline (line 30): \"Data collection has a cost in time, athlete compliance, and staff attention.\" **Explicit**. Inference: item 13's heart-rate or motor-control tests for rest length are a data cost of that kind, worth paying only for a lifter whose rests are a real question. Eric's baseline (line 61): \"No sport performs true 1RM testing.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q10)**. Inference: items 1, 2, 4 and 8 are written around a known 1RM; with projected maxes the \"numerical value\" the athlete associates with a max may be a number they have never lifted.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not before ruling out overtraining. This list does not say so, but the book's Chapter 5 says lack of progress \"may be due to overtraining rather than long-term stagnation\", in which case a shock method is \"potentially harmful\" ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]); several items here (forced repetitions, supramaximal holds, contrast and ballistic methods) are on that shock list. Not technique changes near competition (item 12's own limit). Eric's baseline (line 142): \"Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design; abrupt, large changes are treated as a direct injury-risk driver\" **Inferred (transcript)**. Inference: item 6 (bigger jumps between sets) and item 11 (loads well above 1RM) are the kind of abrupt change that rule warns against, so in a team setting they need a strength base and close supervision.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: instead of working up to a 100 kg best via 80-90-95-100 kg, use 80-92.5-97.5-102.5 kg, so the athlete is not stopped by the familiar number (item 4, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]] (item 3); [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]] (item 9); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (items 11, 20, 27); [[framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids]] (item 7); [[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]] (item 28); [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]] (a different use of \"sticking point\": the weakest joint angle within one lift)\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"dd397c2c-e59b-4e0b-9157-744978278d6a","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
