{"parentUuid":"d14d8950-749f-4595-972f-d26d2e2f6a9b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jm7sDkRSeDtuDhvSjq4WKE","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"ce877781-e1e4-4232-90a0-8f22eddb1090","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:12.309Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md","oldString":"- Categories: reactive, supramaximal, maximal, circa-maximal, submaximal.","newString":"- Categories: reactive, supramaximal, maximal, circa-maximal, submaximal. Reactive Methods connects to Plyometrics and Contrast Methods; Supramaximal Methods connects straight to its box of variations; Maximal, Circa-maximal and Submaximal Methods have no drawn connector to any method.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7\"\ntopic: classifying strength training methods\ncategory: [strength, programming, methods, classification]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Strength Training Methods Sorted by Structural or Functional Aim (Figs 7.1 and 7.2)\n\n**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 7 by restating that \"all strength training depends on two dominant factors ... namely structural conditioning and functional (central nervous and neuromuscular) conditioning\", so training \"may be categorised according to its major structural and functional aims relative to the importance of nervous system training methods (Fig 7.1)\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). Fig 7.1 (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394, read from the page image) splits Strength Training into Structural (Connective Tissue Hypertrophy and Strength; Muscle Hypertrophy) and Functional (Muscle Endurance; Maximal Strength; Power), with an arrow under the five boxes labelled \"Increasing importance of nervous system training\", running from connective tissue on the left to power on the right. The caption says power \"refers to the qualities of strength-speed and speed-strength\".\n\nFig 7.2 (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395, read from the page image and 300 dpi crops) is a four-column chart: Fitness Quality, Category of Method, Methods, Variations. The authors chose a \"loosely interconnected array of columns and rows, showing only some of the more obvious relationships\" because a single flowchart would be \"logically tedious, unattractive and largely unusable\" given the overlap between methods (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). What the image shows:\n- Fitness qualities: explosive strength, maximal strength, strength-speed, speed-strength, strength-endurance, speed-endurance, flexibility, hypertrophy. Only three carry a drawn connector: explosive strength to Reactive Methods, flexibility to a box of static, passive, active, ballistic and PNF, and hypertrophy to Bodybuilding Methods. The other five quality boxes have no drawn link.\n- Categories: reactive, supramaximal, maximal, circa-maximal, submaximal.\n- Methods with their variations: Plyometrics; Contrast Methods (load release, heavy-light set, stripping, pneumatics, static-dynamic); supramaximal variations (forced reps, restricted range, eccentrics, cheating, ballistics); Weightlifting Method (single rep sets, few rep sets); Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics; an Oxford Method box with no drawn link; Endurance Methods; Pyramiding (load pyramiding, rest pyramiding, stripping, reverse pyramid, APRE, DeLorme method); Bodybuilding Methods (plateau, step, multi-sets, supersets, split routine, continuous sets, progressive segments, pre-exhaustion, reps to failure, alternating sets, miscellaneous); Circuit Training (interval, continuous); Multi-purpose Training (isokinetics, loadless training, zone training, hybrids, interval sets, cluster training).\n\nStripping appears twice (under Contrast Methods and under Pyramiding) and APRE appears twice (under Isometrics and under Pyramiding). The authors give the reason: \"some of the training methods may be used to achieve several different aims by altering variables such as the load, number of repetitions and rest intervals\"; pyramiding \"should not be regarded merely as a bodybuilding method\" because it can serve hypertrophy, strength and endurance \"depending on the load and number of repetitions (which are inversely related)\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394). Likewise cheating and restricted range repetitions, \"although classified as supramaximal methods, may also be used submaximally or circamaximally\", and it is \"the role of the coach to choose a given method according to the intended training goal\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394).\n\n**Choosing between hypertrophy and nervous system work.** On the same page the authors say the choice \"may be based on performance in tests of explosive strength and speed-strength (e.g. vertical jump), as well as on the magnitude of the strength deficit discussed early in Chapter 1. If this deficit at any stage is large, then it would be appropriate to concentrate more on weightlifting and reactive methods, whereas, if the deficit is small, submaximal bodybuilding methods should be relied upon\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394). This is the direction of Chapter 1's \"In general\" rule (\"if the strength deficit is large ... weightlifting or plyometric methods\"; \"If the strength deficit is small, hypertrophy must be induced by submaximal loading methods\", PDF p. 21 / printed p. 10). The jump-test paragraph on that same Chapter 1 page pairs a large deficit with \"strength and hypertrophy training with 5RM to 8RM ... loads\" instead (PDF p. 21 / printed p. 10). Chapter 7 names jump performance as a basis but gives a direction only for the deficit, so it does not settle which reading applies to a jump-test deficit. The rule and its internal conflict are rated `contested` in [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]].\n\nThe authors also point to Table 1.1, \"based largely on the practical experience of generations of bodybuilders and weightlifters\", as \"an initial guide\" read alongside Fig 7.1 (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394; see [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' basis is the two-factor view of strength (structural and functional) stressed \"throughout this text\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393), set out in Chapter 1 ([[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]]). They give no evidence for the ordering of the five aims along the nervous-system arrow. Inference: the arrow is a teaching device that places each aim by how much of its gain the authors attribute to neural change; it is not a measured scale.\n\n**When to apply** — As a menu when building a block: start from the fitness quality the block targets, then pick a category and method, and set load, repetitions and rest to fit the aim, since the same method can serve several aims (PDF pp. 404-405). Eric's baseline (line 103): \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky) ... governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**. Inference: the chart picks a method for a quality; dynamic correspondence still decides which exercise the method is applied to. Eric's baseline (line 76) uses the dynamic strength index \"to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)**, tagged in the baseline as contested on the evidence. Inference: that is the same kind of decision the authors make from a jump test and the strength deficit, made with a different measure.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not read Fig 7.2 as a decision tree. The authors say it shows \"only some of the more obvious relationships\" (PDF p. 404), and five of its eight quality boxes have no drawn link to any method. Do not read the method headings as fixed: a method's aim moves with its load, repetitions and rest (PDF pp. 404-405). The authors' concluding remark for 7.1 also warns that \"no methods operate in isolation of one another; each method has immediate and delayed training effects which have significant effects on other methods that follow hours, days or even weeks later\" (PDF p. 414 / printed p. 403), so a method chosen from the chart still has to be placed in a sequence. Do not use the hypertrophy-or-nervous-system rule on a jump-test deficit without reading the conflict recorded in [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example: pyramiding is placed in its own row because, by changing the load and number of repetitions, it can be used \"in the earlier preparatory stages to meet several general training aims such as increased muscle hypertrophy, strength and endurance\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]] (Chapter 1 source of the structural and functional split); [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]] (the deficit rule; Chapter 7 restates its \"In general\" direction); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Table 1.1); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]; [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]]; [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]]; [[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]] (why APRE sits under Isometrics)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":7,"newStart":17,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," Fig 7.2 (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395, read from the page image and 300 dpi crops) is a four-column chart: Fitness Quality, Category of Method, Methods, Variations. The authors chose a \"loosely interconnected array of columns and rows, showing only some of the more obvious relationships\" because a single flowchart would be \"logically tedious, unattractive and largely unusable\" given the overlap between methods (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). What the image shows:"," - Fitness qualities: explosive strength, maximal strength, strength-speed, speed-strength, strength-endurance, speed-endurance, flexibility, hypertrophy. Only three carry a drawn connector: explosive strength to Reactive Methods, flexibility to a box of static, passive, active, ballistic and PNF, and hypertrophy to Bodybuilding Methods. The other five quality boxes have no drawn link.","-- Categories: reactive, supramaximal, maximal, circa-maximal, submaximal.","+- Categories: reactive, supramaximal, maximal, circa-maximal, submaximal. Reactive Methods connects to Plyometrics and Contrast Methods; Supramaximal Methods connects straight to its box of variations; Maximal, Circa-maximal and Submaximal Methods have no drawn connector to any method."," - Methods with their variations: Plyometrics; Contrast Methods (load release, heavy-light set, stripping, pneumatics, static-dynamic); supramaximal variations (forced reps, restricted range, eccentrics, cheating, ballistics); Weightlifting Method (single rep sets, few rep sets); Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics; an Oxford Method box with no drawn link; Endurance Methods; Pyramiding (load pyramiding, rest pyramiding, stripping, reverse pyramid, APRE, DeLorme method); Bodybuilding Methods (plateau, step, multi-sets, supersets, split routine, continuous sets, progressive segments, pre-exhaustion, reps to failure, alternating sets, miscellaneous); Circuit Training (interval, continuous); Multi-purpose Training (isokinetics, loadless training, zone training, hybrids, interval sets, cluster training)."," "," Stripping appears twice (under Contrast Methods and under Pyramiding) and APRE appears twice (under Isometrics and under Pyramiding). The authors give the reason: \"some of the training methods may be used to achieve several different aims by altering variables such as the load, number of repetitions and rest intervals\"; pyramiding \"should not be regarded merely as a bodybuilding method\" because it can serve hypertrophy, strength and endurance \"depending on the load and number of repetitions (which are inversely related)\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394). Likewise cheating and restricted range repetitions, \"although classified as supramaximal methods, may also be used submaximally or circamaximally\", and it is \"the role of the coach to choose a given method according to the intended training goal\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394)."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d14d8950-749f-4595-972f-d26d2e2f6a9b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ce877781-e1e4-4232-90a0-8f22eddb1090","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqH8RYBJxKfAYkP4ejj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UJaZkFGd1sLKCBzW4jv6gN","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md","old_string":"Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics; an Oxford Method box with no drawn link; Endurance Methods;","new_string":"Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics and Endurance Methods, both with no drawn variations; an Oxford Method box in the variations column with no drawn link;"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1945,"cache_read_input_tokens":268824,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1945,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01UJaZkFGd1sLKCBzW4jv6gN":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md","old_string":"Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics; an Oxford Method box with no drawn link; Endurance Methods;","new_string":"Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics and Endurance Methods, both with no drawn variations; an Oxford Method box in the variations column with no drawn link;"}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011CffqGwQFdiWpasC1VDCG3","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f38498a4-17ec-447f-bc88-c1c2e92b9686","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:14.343Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f38498a4-17ec-447f-bc88-c1c2e92b9686","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UJaZkFGd1sLKCBzW4jv6gN","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"ed8cfdee-14a1-42b0-875a-00a6ec8c68d7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:14.423Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2.md","oldString":"Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics; an Oxford Method box with no drawn link; Endurance Methods;","newString":"Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics and Endurance Methods, both with no drawn variations; an Oxford Method box in the variations column with no drawn link;","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7\"\ntopic: classifying strength training methods\ncategory: [strength, programming, methods, classification]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Strength Training Methods Sorted by Structural or Functional Aim (Figs 7.1 and 7.2)\n\n**What it is** — The authors open Chapter 7 by restating that \"all strength training depends on two dominant factors ... namely structural conditioning and functional (central nervous and neuromuscular) conditioning\", so training \"may be categorised according to its major structural and functional aims relative to the importance of nervous system training methods (Fig 7.1)\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). Fig 7.1 (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394, read from the page image) splits Strength Training into Structural (Connective Tissue Hypertrophy and Strength; Muscle Hypertrophy) and Functional (Muscle Endurance; Maximal Strength; Power), with an arrow under the five boxes labelled \"Increasing importance of nervous system training\", running from connective tissue on the left to power on the right. The caption says power \"refers to the qualities of strength-speed and speed-strength\".\n\nFig 7.2 (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395, read from the page image and 300 dpi crops) is a four-column chart: Fitness Quality, Category of Method, Methods, Variations. The authors chose a \"loosely interconnected array of columns and rows, showing only some of the more obvious relationships\" because a single flowchart would be \"logically tedious, unattractive and largely unusable\" given the overlap between methods (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). What the image shows:\n- Fitness qualities: explosive strength, maximal strength, strength-speed, speed-strength, strength-endurance, speed-endurance, flexibility, hypertrophy. Only three carry a drawn connector: explosive strength to Reactive Methods, flexibility to a box of static, passive, active, ballistic and PNF, and hypertrophy to Bodybuilding Methods. The other five quality boxes have no drawn link.\n- Categories: reactive, supramaximal, maximal, circa-maximal, submaximal. Reactive Methods connects to Plyometrics and Contrast Methods; Supramaximal Methods connects straight to its box of variations; Maximal, Circa-maximal and Submaximal Methods have no drawn connector to any method.\n- Methods with their variations: Plyometrics; Contrast Methods (load release, heavy-light set, stripping, pneumatics, static-dynamic); supramaximal variations (forced reps, restricted range, eccentrics, cheating, ballistics); Weightlifting Method (single rep sets, few rep sets); Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics; an Oxford Method box with no drawn link; Endurance Methods; Pyramiding (load pyramiding, rest pyramiding, stripping, reverse pyramid, APRE, DeLorme method); Bodybuilding Methods (plateau, step, multi-sets, supersets, split routine, continuous sets, progressive segments, pre-exhaustion, reps to failure, alternating sets, miscellaneous); Circuit Training (interval, continuous); Multi-purpose Training (isokinetics, loadless training, zone training, hybrids, interval sets, cluster training).\n\nStripping appears twice (under Contrast Methods and under Pyramiding) and APRE appears twice (under Isometrics and under Pyramiding). The authors give the reason: \"some of the training methods may be used to achieve several different aims by altering variables such as the load, number of repetitions and rest intervals\"; pyramiding \"should not be regarded merely as a bodybuilding method\" because it can serve hypertrophy, strength and endurance \"depending on the load and number of repetitions (which are inversely related)\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394). Likewise cheating and restricted range repetitions, \"although classified as supramaximal methods, may also be used submaximally or circamaximally\", and it is \"the role of the coach to choose a given method according to the intended training goal\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394).\n\n**Choosing between hypertrophy and nervous system work.** On the same page the authors say the choice \"may be based on performance in tests of explosive strength and speed-strength (e.g. vertical jump), as well as on the magnitude of the strength deficit discussed early in Chapter 1. If this deficit at any stage is large, then it would be appropriate to concentrate more on weightlifting and reactive methods, whereas, if the deficit is small, submaximal bodybuilding methods should be relied upon\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394). This is the direction of Chapter 1's \"In general\" rule (\"if the strength deficit is large ... weightlifting or plyometric methods\"; \"If the strength deficit is small, hypertrophy must be induced by submaximal loading methods\", PDF p. 21 / printed p. 10). The jump-test paragraph on that same Chapter 1 page pairs a large deficit with \"strength and hypertrophy training with 5RM to 8RM ... loads\" instead (PDF p. 21 / printed p. 10). Chapter 7 names jump performance as a basis but gives a direction only for the deficit, so it does not settle which reading applies to a jump-test deficit. The rule and its internal conflict are rated `contested` in [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]].\n\nThe authors also point to Table 1.1, \"based largely on the practical experience of generations of bodybuilders and weightlifters\", as \"an initial guide\" read alongside Fig 7.1 (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394; see [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' basis is the two-factor view of strength (structural and functional) stressed \"throughout this text\" (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393), set out in Chapter 1 ([[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]]). They give no evidence for the ordering of the five aims along the nervous-system arrow. Inference: the arrow is a teaching device that places each aim by how much of its gain the authors attribute to neural change; it is not a measured scale.\n\n**When to apply** — As a menu when building a block: start from the fitness quality the block targets, then pick a category and method, and set load, repetitions and rest to fit the aim, since the same method can serve several aims (PDF pp. 404-405). Eric's baseline (line 103): \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky) ... governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**. Inference: the chart picks a method for a quality; dynamic correspondence still decides which exercise the method is applied to. Eric's baseline (line 76) uses the dynamic strength index \"to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)**, tagged in the baseline as contested on the evidence. Inference: that is the same kind of decision the authors make from a jump test and the strength deficit, made with a different measure.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not read Fig 7.2 as a decision tree. The authors say it shows \"only some of the more obvious relationships\" (PDF p. 404), and five of its eight quality boxes have no drawn link to any method. Do not read the method headings as fixed: a method's aim moves with its load, repetitions and rest (PDF pp. 404-405). The authors' concluding remark for 7.1 also warns that \"no methods operate in isolation of one another; each method has immediate and delayed training effects which have significant effects on other methods that follow hours, days or even weeks later\" (PDF p. 414 / printed p. 403), so a method chosen from the chart still has to be placed in a sequence. Do not use the hypertrophy-or-nervous-system rule on a jump-test deficit without reading the conflict recorded in [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own example: pyramiding is placed in its own row because, by changing the load and number of repetitions, it can be used \"in the earlier preparatory stages to meet several general training aims such as increased muscle hypertrophy, strength and endurance\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--functional-vs-structural-resistance-training]] (Chapter 1 source of the structural and functional split); [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]] (the deficit rule; Chapter 7 restates its \"In general\" direction); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Table 1.1); [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]; [[framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set]]; [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]]; [[heuristic--isometric-apre-six-second-maximum-protocol]] (why APRE sits under Isometrics)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":18,"oldLines":7,"newStart":18,"newLines":7,"lines":[" Fig 7.2 (PDF p. 406 / printed p. 395, read from the page image and 300 dpi crops) is a four-column chart: Fitness Quality, Category of Method, Methods, Variations. The authors chose a \"loosely interconnected array of columns and rows, showing only some of the more obvious relationships\" because a single flowchart would be \"logically tedious, unattractive and largely unusable\" given the overlap between methods (PDF p. 404 / printed p. 393). What the image shows:"," - Fitness qualities: explosive strength, maximal strength, strength-speed, speed-strength, strength-endurance, speed-endurance, flexibility, hypertrophy. Only three carry a drawn connector: explosive strength to Reactive Methods, flexibility to a box of static, passive, active, ballistic and PNF, and hypertrophy to Bodybuilding Methods. The other five quality boxes have no drawn link."," - Categories: reactive, supramaximal, maximal, circa-maximal, submaximal. Reactive Methods connects to Plyometrics and Contrast Methods; Supramaximal Methods connects straight to its box of variations; Maximal, Circa-maximal and Submaximal Methods have no drawn connector to any method.","-- Methods with their variations: Plyometrics; Contrast Methods (load release, heavy-light set, stripping, pneumatics, static-dynamic); supramaximal variations (forced reps, restricted range, eccentrics, cheating, ballistics); Weightlifting Method (single rep sets, few rep sets); Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics; an Oxford Method box with no drawn link; Endurance Methods; Pyramiding (load pyramiding, rest pyramiding, stripping, reverse pyramid, APRE, DeLorme method); Bodybuilding Methods (plateau, step, multi-sets, supersets, split routine, continuous sets, progressive segments, pre-exhaustion, reps to failure, alternating sets, miscellaneous); Circuit Training (interval, continuous); Multi-purpose Training (isokinetics, loadless training, zone training, hybrids, interval sets, cluster training).","+- Methods with their variations: Plyometrics; Contrast Methods (load release, heavy-light set, stripping, pneumatics, static-dynamic); supramaximal variations (forced reps, restricted range, eccentrics, cheating, ballistics); Weightlifting Method (single rep sets, few rep sets); Isometrics (traditional, explosive, oscillatory, APRE); Quasi-isometrics and Endurance Methods, both with no drawn variations; an Oxford Method box in the variations column with no drawn link; Pyramiding (load pyramiding, rest pyramiding, stripping, reverse pyramid, APRE, DeLorme method); Bodybuilding Methods (plateau, step, multi-sets, supersets, split routine, continuous sets, progressive segments, pre-exhaustion, reps to failure, alternating sets, miscellaneous); Circuit Training (interval, continuous); Multi-purpose Training (isokinetics, loadless training, zone training, hybrids, interval sets, cluster training)."," "," Stripping appears twice (under Contrast Methods and under Pyramiding) and APRE appears twice (under Isometrics and under Pyramiding). The authors give the reason: \"some of the training methods may be used to achieve several different aims by altering variables such as the load, number of repetitions and rest intervals\"; pyramiding \"should not be regarded merely as a bodybuilding method\" because it can serve hypertrophy, strength and endurance \"depending on the load and number of repetitions (which are inversely related)\" (PDF pp. 404-405 / printed pp. 393-394). Likewise cheating and restricted range repetitions, \"although classified as supramaximal methods, may also be used submaximally or circamaximally\", and it is \"the role of the coach to choose a given method according to the intended training goal\" (PDF p. 405 / printed p. 394)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f38498a4-17ec-447f-bc88-c1c2e92b9686","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ed8cfdee-14a1-42b0-875a-00a6ec8c68d7","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqH8RYBJxKfAYkP4ejj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MVE2khXpaXPDVW76C286QZ","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md","old_string":"(Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lamber, 1965)","new_string":"(Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lambert, 1965; printed \"Lamber\" in the text layer)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1945,"cache_read_input_tokens":268824,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1945,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01MVE2khXpaXPDVW76C286QZ":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md","old_string":"(Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lamber, 1965)","new_string":"(Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lambert, 1965; printed \"Lamber\" in the text layer)"}},"apiBlockIndex":4,"requestId":"req_011CffqGwQFdiWpasC1VDCG3","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d016599c-c9d8-4a51-8333-29e51d9680e3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:15.847Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d016599c-c9d8-4a51-8333-29e51d9680e3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MVE2khXpaXPDVW76C286QZ","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"81e3c171-ff4d-4d5f-b75f-a25b85dd3539","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:15.960Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md","oldString":"(Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lamber, 1965)","newString":"(Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lambert, 1965; printed \"Lamber\" in the text layer)","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: reactive contrast methods\ncategory: [power, explosive-strength, contrast-training, potentiation]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Contrast Methods: Load Release, Pneumatics and the Heavy-Light Set\n\n**What it is** — \"Reactive methods are intended to develop power and explosive strength by imposing specific demands on nervous processes.\" Besides plyometrics (5.2.4), the category includes the *Contrast Methods* (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400, checked against the page image):\n\n- **Load release:** perform an exercise with an added load, \"then releasing the load suddenly during the lift, so that the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\". The stripping method \"offers a non-explosive variation of this method\", and the term also covers the sudden release of a parachute towed by a runner, swimmer or cyclist.\n- **Pneumatics:** \"much the same as the preceding contrast method\", on a compressed-air machine whose pressure can be \"quickly increased or decreased at any stage of a movement at the touch of a button\", so resistance drops \"fairly rapidly, though not as rapidly as with the load release method\" (Keiser machines are named).\n- **Heavy-light set:** \"performing a low repetition heavy set, decreasing the load by about 20%, taking a rest of 10-20 seconds, then executing the lighter set as rapidly as possible for 3-5 repetitions.\"\n- **Static-dynamic method:** preliminary isometric tension raises the dynamic and explosive strength of the following action through the after-effect (3.3.1, 3.4.2); its size depends on \"the duration of static loading, muscular fatigue and the interval between the static stimulus and the subsequent action\". This method is atomized from Chapter 4 in [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]] and not repeated here.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: for load release, \"the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\"; for the heavy-light set, \"The nervous system remains briefly adapted to the heavy load if the rest interval is brief and the muscles are temporarily able to operate in this super-stimulated condition to produce a very powerful and rapid contraction with the lightened load\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). No study is cited on this page.\n\n**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lamber, 1965), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, text layer). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]].\n\n**When to apply** — For power and explosive-strength work in athletes with a strength base, when the aim is a faster lighter effort straight after a heavier one. The heavy-light set needs only a bar and a quick load change. The authors also list \"Use any of the contrast training methods discussed earlier\" as a way through a sticking point (item 9, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402). Inference: in Eric's in-season week (baseline line 186: \"Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**), a heavy-light pairing is a low-volume way to keep a fast element in the lighter Wednesday lift; this is not something the authors or Eric propose.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not expect a reliable acute boost: the book's own cited Western studies found none in several tasks (PDF p. 175). The heavy-light set depends on the rest staying brief (10-20 s, PDF p. 411); with longer rest the authors' stated mechanism no longer applies. Do not use the stripping method as a power method; the authors call it the \"non-explosive variation\" (PDF p. 411). Chapter 5 classes contrast methods among shock methods for experienced athletes, used only after overtraining is ruled out ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: an athlete does a dip or pull-up \"as powerfully as possible with a load grasped between the legs, then release[s] the load early during the next repetition and accelerate[s] rapidly upwards for the remainder of the movement\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]; [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]]; [[heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release]] (parachute release as a contrast method); [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":22,"oldLines":7,"newStart":22,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: for load release, \"the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\"; for the heavy-light set, \"The nervous system remains briefly adapted to the heavy load if the rest interval is brief and the muscles are temporarily able to operate in this super-stimulated condition to produce a very powerful and rapid contraction with the lightened load\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). No study is cited on this page."," ","-**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lamber, 1965), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, text layer). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]].","+**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lambert, 1965; printed \"Lamber\" in the text layer), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, text layer). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]."," "," **When to apply** — For power and explosive-strength work in athletes with a strength base, when the aim is a faster lighter effort straight after a heavier one. The heavy-light set needs only a bar and a quick load change. The authors also list \"Use any of the contrast training methods discussed earlier\" as a way through a sticking point (item 9, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402). Inference: in Eric's in-season week (baseline line 186: \"Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**), a heavy-light pairing is a low-volume way to keep a fast element in the lighter Wednesday lift; this is not something the authors or Eric propose."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d016599c-c9d8-4a51-8333-29e51d9680e3","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"81e3c171-ff4d-4d5f-b75f-a25b85dd3539","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqH8RYBJxKfAYkP4ejj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01M78Hgui8GbJyysdo5jbdsC","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md","old_string":"Inference: under the usual relation between repetition maximum and %1RM, a 5RM is well above 75% of 1RM, so the lower end of the intensive range falls below the stated minimum.","new_string":"Inference: the authors' own maximal pyramid puts 6 repetitions at 85% of 1RM, with values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF p. 409), so by the book's own figures a 5RM sits above 85%, and the lower end of the intensive range (75%) falls below the stated minimum."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1945,"cache_read_input_tokens":268824,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1945,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01M78Hgui8GbJyysdo5jbdsC":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md","old_string":"Inference: under the usual relation between repetition maximum and %1RM, a 5RM is well above 75% of 1RM, so the lower end of the intensive range falls below the stated minimum.","new_string":"Inference: the authors' own maximal pyramid puts 6 repetitions at 85% of 1RM, with values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF p. 409), so by the book's own figures a 5RM sits above 85%, and the lower end of the intensive range (75%) falls below the stated minimum."}},"apiBlockIndex":5,"requestId":"req_011CffqGwQFdiWpasC1VDCG3","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"97abd61a-f357-4126-9777-a3747c39802b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:18.060Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"97abd61a-f357-4126-9777-a3747c39802b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M78Hgui8GbJyysdo5jbdsC","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"5f622496-7cb0-4ee3-bc4e-41d634d5ed08","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:18.175Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--interval-resistance-training-and-cluster-sets.md","oldString":"Inference: under the usual relation between repetition maximum and %1RM, a 5RM is well above 75% of 1RM, so the lower end of the intensive range falls below the stated minimum.","newString":"Inference: the authors' own maximal pyramid puts 6 repetitions at 85% of 1RM, with values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF p. 409), so by the book's own figures a 5RM sits above 85%, and the lower end of the intensive range (75%) falls below the stated minimum.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: interval resistance and cluster sets\ncategory: [strength-endurance, interval-training, cluster-sets, loading]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Interval Resistance Training (Extensive and Intensive) and Cluster Sets\n\n**What it is** — The authors define interval resistance training by analogy with track: \"the primary aim of interval training is generally to develop strength-endurance by means of systematically alternating work and recovery phases\", with rest \"(preferably active rest)\" too short for full recovery \"so that the athlete is forced to cope with increasing exhaustion\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398, checked against the page image). They split it into two broad categories, \"one to increase strength or power and the other to increase endurance\", and the endurance category into two methods (PDF p. 409):\n\n| | Extensive interval | Intensive interval |\n|---|---|---|\n| Develops | \"medium duration strength-endurance\" and recovery capacity | \"short-duration strength-endurance\" and recovery capacity |\n| Load | \"30-40% of 1RM\" | \"50-60% of 1RM\" |\n| Sets x reps | \"5-6 sets of 20-30 repetitions\" | \"3-6 sets, where each set is performed to failure\" |\n| Rest | \"1-2 minutes\" | \"10-90 seconds\", by exercise, load, fitness and loading duration |\n| Energy | below 40% \"arterial blood flow is not restricted\" and \"'aerobic' energy processes predominate\" | higher loads \"inhibit arterial blood flow and anaerobic energy processes predominate\" |\n| Heart rate | large muscle groups needed to reach the level \"recommended for aerobic conditioning, e.g. 75% of (220-Age)\" | \"can readily increase heart rates to over 180 beats/min\" |\n| Circuits | \"Specially designed circuit training programmes\" can serve | special circuits with \"1-3 minutes\" between circuits |\n\nFor extensive work the authors want \"the larger muscle groups such as the quadriceps, glutei, pectorals and erector spinae\"; \"bicep curls, calf raises and tricep push-downs play a minimal role in elevating the heart rate\" (PDF p. 409).\n\n**Cluster training** is \"another form of interval training\": one or more repetitions \"with a 10-20 second interval between each repetition or cluster of repetitions in an extended set\". \"Characteristically, the minimum load used is one's 5RM and 4-6 sets are performed\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398). *Extensive clustering*: \"4-6 repetitions with one's 4-6RM, with 10 second rest intervals between each cluster\", or stripping-style with the load cut \"by 10-20%\" and a 10 s rest after each set (PDF pp. 409-410). *Intensive clustering*: \"4-6 sets of only one repetition with 75-90% of 1RM, with about a 20 second rest between repetitions for 4-6 repetitions\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, image). That last sentence is unclear as printed: it gives both \"sets of only one repetition\" and \"4-6 repetitions\" without saying how they nest.\n\n**Why `contested`.** The minimum load for cluster training is \"one's 5RM\" (PDF p. 409), but intensive clustering uses \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). Both state the load for the same method. Inference: under the usual relation between repetition maximum and %1RM, a 5RM is well above 75% of 1RM, so the lower end of the intensive range falls below the stated minimum. The book does not reconcile them.\n\nA second internal conflict touches this section: the weightlifting method rests \"1-5 minutes\" between singles (PDF p. 406), while this section says weightlifting and powerlifting rely on near-maximal singles \"with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409); see [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]].\n\n**Different method, not a conflict.** Chapter 5's circuit guidelines give aerobic circuits \"20-40\" repetitions at \"usually not more than 30% of 1RM\" and anaerobic circuits \"3-8\" repetitions at \"usually more than 70% of 1RM\" ([[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). Those are station prescriptions for circuits; the figures here are for interval sets. They differ (for example, 50-60% to failure here against more than 70% for 3-8 reps there) but they answer different questions.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: incomplete rest forces the athlete \"to cope with increasing exhaustion and thereby develop muscular endurance\"; below about 40% of maximum arterial flow is not restricted, so aerobic processes predominate; at 50-60% flow is inhibited and anaerobic processes predominate (PDF p. 409). They give no mechanism for cluster sets beyond classing them as interval training. Chapter 3 adds that a series of single repetitions costs more energy than a continuous set with the same load ([[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]).\n\n**When to apply** — Extensive intervals or circuits for general strength-endurance and recovery capacity in a general preparation phase, built on large muscle groups. Intensive intervals for short-duration strength-endurance. Cluster sets when the aim is more repetitions at a heavy load than one continuous set allows. Eric's baseline (line 182): \"incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: intensive intervals to failure at 50-60% with short rest are close to glycolytic work, so by that rule they would not share a block with a maximal-strength emphasis. The authors' \"75% of (220-Age)\" is an aerobic conditioning target; Eric's baseline (line 119) uses age-based maximum heart rate for the anaerobic threshold at \"about 85% of that\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q20)**. Inference: the two use the same formula for different targets and do not conflict.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Because of increasing fatigue and the greater likelihood of injury under such conditions, exercises that require high levels of skill are not recommended\" (PDF p. 409); keep Olympic lifts and other technical lifts out of endurance intervals. Do not set intensive cluster loads from the book without choosing between its two load statements (5RM minimum against 75-90% of 1RM). Small single-joint exercises will not raise heart rate enough for extensive intervals, by the authors' own example (PDF p. 409).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own extensive interval prescription: 30-40% of 1RM, 5-6 sets of 20-30 repetitions, 1-2 minutes' rest, using exercises for the quadriceps, glutei, pectorals and erector spinae rather than curls, calf raises or push-downs (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]]; [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]; [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]] (rest-pause and the weightlifting interval set); [[framework--set-structures-plateau-step-stripping-and-pyramids]] (stripping); [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":29,"oldLines":7,"newStart":29,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Cluster training** is \"another form of interval training\": one or more repetitions \"with a 10-20 second interval between each repetition or cluster of repetitions in an extended set\". \"Characteristically, the minimum load used is one's 5RM and 4-6 sets are performed\" (PDF p. 409 / printed p. 398). *Extensive clustering*: \"4-6 repetitions with one's 4-6RM, with 10 second rest intervals between each cluster\", or stripping-style with the load cut \"by 10-20%\" and a 10 s rest after each set (PDF pp. 409-410). *Intensive clustering*: \"4-6 sets of only one repetition with 75-90% of 1RM, with about a 20 second rest between repetitions for 4-6 repetitions\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, image). That last sentence is unclear as printed: it gives both \"sets of only one repetition\" and \"4-6 repetitions\" without saying how they nest."," ","-**Why `contested`.** The minimum load for cluster training is \"one's 5RM\" (PDF p. 409), but intensive clustering uses \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). Both state the load for the same method. Inference: under the usual relation between repetition maximum and %1RM, a 5RM is well above 75% of 1RM, so the lower end of the intensive range falls below the stated minimum. The book does not reconcile them.","+**Why `contested`.** The minimum load for cluster training is \"one's 5RM\" (PDF p. 409), but intensive clustering uses \"75-90% of 1RM\" (PDF p. 410). Both state the load for the same method. Inference: the authors' own maximal pyramid puts 6 repetitions at 85% of 1RM, with values \"derived from the equations associated with Fig 1.9\" (PDF p. 409), so by the book's own figures a 5RM sits above 85%, and the lower end of the intensive range (75%) falls below the stated minimum. The book does not reconcile them."," "," A second internal conflict touches this section: the weightlifting method rests \"1-5 minutes\" between singles (PDF p. 406), while this section says weightlifting and powerlifting rely on near-maximal singles \"with a few seconds of rest between repetitions\" (PDF p. 409); see [[framework--maximal-supramaximal-circa-maximal-and-submaximal-methods]]."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"97abd61a-f357-4126-9777-a3747c39802b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5f622496-7cb0-4ee3-bc4e-41d634d5ed08","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqH8RYBJxKfAYkP4ejj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LR2gLRQHMHGGRDoaLhp4R6","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md","old_string":"Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions, so the \"3\" probably refers to something else (the superscript may be a set count), and the text does not say.","new_string":"Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions and the text does not say what the \"3\" counts. Inference: the superscript may be a set count."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1945,"cache_read_input_tokens":268824,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1945,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01LR2gLRQHMHGGRDoaLhp4R6":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md","old_string":"Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions, so the \"3\" probably refers to something else (the superscript may be a set count), and the text does not say.","new_string":"Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions and the text does not say what the \"3\" counts. Inference: the superscript may be a set count."}},"apiBlockIndex":6,"requestId":"req_011CffqGwQFdiWpasC1VDCG3","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f37fc399-c3b8-487b-93d4-6502cafd6b91","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:19.848Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f37fc399-c3b8-487b-93d4-6502cafd6b91","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LR2gLRQHMHGGRDoaLhp4R6","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"8176b15d-3747-410c-9f29-99ee85aa9796","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:19.948Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--hybrid-repetitions-and-hybrid-sets.md","oldString":"Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions, so the \"3\" probably refers to something else (the superscript may be a set count), and the text does not say.","newString":"Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions and the text does not say what the \"3\" counts. Inference: the superscript may be a set count.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: hybrid exercises and sets\ncategory: [exercise-selection, complexes, weightlifting, programming]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Hybrid Training: Several Movements in One Repetition, or a Different Movement Within One Set\n\n**What it is** — \"Hybrid Training ... refers to exercise in which each repetition consists of two or more separate movements between the starting and end points\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399, checked against the page image). The clean-and-jerk, \"a pull from the ground, with a front squat, followed by a thrust overhead\", is \"probably the best known hybrid exercise\". The method also covers \"the *hybrid set*, which comprises a different movement for each repetition of the set\" (PDF p. 410).\n\nThe authors' examples (PDF p. 410):\n- Hybrid repetitions: barbell or dumbbell curl + overhead standing press; back or front squat + press (or push press or jerk); squat + good morning; dumbbell lateral raise (palms pronated) + upright row; dumbbell bench press + bent-elbow pullover (or flye).\n- Hybrid sets: reps 1-5 dumbbell press, 6-10 dumbbell curl, 11-15 lateral raise; reps 1-5 narrow-grip bench press, 6-10 wide-grip; reps 1-5 squat, 6-15 calf raise; reps 1-5 squat, 6-10 overhead press or push press; reps 1-5 dumbbell bench press, 6-10 bent-elbow flye, 11-15 bent-elbow pullover.\n- Unequal counts are allowed: \"Reps 1-3: Power clean from the hang (3 reps); Reps 4-8: front squat (5 reps); Reps 9-10: push press (2 reps)\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). Using \"the recording convention in 6.6.2\", this is written as 80 with a superscript 3 over \"3+5+2\", \"for a bar loaded to 80kg, for a set of 3 repetitions\" (PDF p. 411, image). Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions, so the \"3\" probably refers to something else (the superscript may be a set count), and the text does not say.\n\nGuidelines \"to avoid injury for this demanding type of training\" (PDF p. 411): \"The load must be selected so that it can be performed without excessive strain in the weakest movement of the hybrid\", and \"The order of movements should always be arranged to permit their safe and controlled execution without causing exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading.\" A larger list of hybrid exercises is promised in Ch 8.4.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons: the clean-and-jerk \"exercises an extensive variety of muscle groups\", and \"It is doubtful if any manoeuvre in any other sport rivals the clean-and-jerk or clean-and-push-press in recruiting maximal or near maximal contraction in so many muscle groups in such a short time\". They cite their own Table 3.12 for the clean-and-jerk costing \"over 60kJ per maximal lift, higher than all other lifts\" (book's cross-reference, not checked for this entry). They attribute this to hybrid exercises involving \"more joints of the body moving through a greater number of degrees of freedom\", which gives \"a more concentrated form of circuit training\": \"Instead of doing three separate exercises at three different stations, one can do all of them in a single repetition or at a single station\" (PDF p. 410 / printed p. 399).\n\n**When to apply** — When session time or space is short and several movements can be loaded by the same bar; when a circuit is to be condensed into one station (the authors' use). Eric's baseline (line 104): \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: clean-based hybrids such as hang power clean, front squat and push press fit that rule; the curl and lateral-raise hybrids do not.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — When the load is set by the strongest movement: the authors require it to suit \"the weakest movement of the hybrid\" (PDF p. 411), so the stronger movements are under-loaded. Inference: that makes hybrids a poor choice when the aim is maximal load in one of the movements. Not in an order that brings a technical movement after a fatiguing one; the authors rule out orders that cause \"exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading\" (PDF p. 411). Do not cite the 60 kJ figure from this entry; it is the book's pointer to Table 3.12, not checked here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own unequal hybrid set: hang power clean for 3, front squat for 5, push press for 2, with an 80 kg bar (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). The load is limited by the weakest of the three movements, by the authors' rule.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]] (hybrids under Multi-purpose Training); [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]; [[heuristic--progressive-and-random-segmental-training]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":18,"oldLines":7,"newStart":18,"newLines":7,"lines":[" The authors' examples (PDF p. 410):"," - Hybrid repetitions: barbell or dumbbell curl + overhead standing press; back or front squat + press (or push press or jerk); squat + good morning; dumbbell lateral raise (palms pronated) + upright row; dumbbell bench press + bent-elbow pullover (or flye)."," - Hybrid sets: reps 1-5 dumbbell press, 6-10 dumbbell curl, 11-15 lateral raise; reps 1-5 narrow-grip bench press, 6-10 wide-grip; reps 1-5 squat, 6-15 calf raise; reps 1-5 squat, 6-10 overhead press or push press; reps 1-5 dumbbell bench press, 6-10 bent-elbow flye, 11-15 bent-elbow pullover.","-- Unequal counts are allowed: \"Reps 1-3: Power clean from the hang (3 reps); Reps 4-8: front squat (5 reps); Reps 9-10: push press (2 reps)\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). Using \"the recording convention in 6.6.2\", this is written as 80 with a superscript 3 over \"3+5+2\", \"for a bar loaded to 80kg, for a set of 3 repetitions\" (PDF p. 411, image). Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions, so the \"3\" probably refers to something else (the superscript may be a set count), and the text does not say.","+- Unequal counts are allowed: \"Reps 1-3: Power clean from the hang (3 reps); Reps 4-8: front squat (5 reps); Reps 9-10: push press (2 reps)\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). Using \"the recording convention in 6.6.2\", this is written as 80 with a superscript 3 over \"3+5+2\", \"for a bar loaded to 80kg, for a set of 3 repetitions\" (PDF p. 411, image). Unclear as printed: the set contains 10 repetitions and the text does not say what the \"3\" counts. Inference: the superscript may be a set count."," "," Guidelines \"to avoid injury for this demanding type of training\" (PDF p. 411): \"The load must be selected so that it can be performed without excessive strain in the weakest movement of the hybrid\", and \"The order of movements should always be arranged to permit their safe and controlled execution without causing exhaustion, impaired technique or overloading.\" A larger list of hybrid exercises is promised in Ch 8.4."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f37fc399-c3b8-487b-93d4-6502cafd6b91","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8176b15d-3747-410c-9f29-99ee85aa9796","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqH8RYBJxKfAYkP4ejj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015SUSEZJ35TwFkjhyPQVp6R","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md","old_string":"which matches the authors' strict-sets-first rule. Use the functional-priority order","new_string":"which matches the authors' strict-sets-first rule. Inference: use the functional-priority order"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1945,"cache_read_input_tokens":268824,"output_tokens":1999,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":128},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1945},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1999,"cache_read_input_tokens":268824,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1945,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1945,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_015SUSEZJ35TwFkjhyPQVp6R":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md","old_string":"which matches the authors' strict-sets-first rule. Use the functional-priority order","new_string":"which matches the authors' strict-sets-first rule. Inference: use the functional-priority order"}},"apiBlockIndex":7,"requestId":"req_011CffqGwQFdiWpasC1VDCG3","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"92c30b0c-4c17-4787-95cb-b65fee16cc49","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:21.254Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"92c30b0c-4c17-4787-95cb-b65fee16cc49","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015SUSEZJ35TwFkjhyPQVp6R","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"5b8e1549-5e8d-4aae-a2ba-3252eb1a46f7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:21.355Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--miscellaneous-bodybuilding-set-variations.md","oldString":"which matches the authors' strict-sets-first rule. Use the functional-priority order","newString":"which matches the authors' strict-sets-first rule. Inference: use the functional-priority order","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: bodybuilding set variations\ncategory: [bodybuilding, hypertrophy, exercise-order, variation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Miscellaneous Bodybuilding Set Variations, Framed as Novelty for a Hypertrophy Phase\n\n**What it is** — The authors introduce a group of \"Miscellaneous Methods\" with a frame: bodybuilding methods \"are of minimal significance for developing most of the specific functional types of strength fitness required in competitive sport\", so they give \"only a brief summary ... to provide the coach with working definitions of methods that he may wish to apply in an hypertrophy phase\". \"The intention of most of these methods is to offer a novel variation to shock the body or particular muscle group out of any prevailing state of habituation or stagnation\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401, checked against the page image). The methods (PDF pp. 410, 412-413 / printed pp. 399, 401-402):\n\n- **Strict sets:** precise form with no momentum or cheating; \"It is advisable that all beginners learn first with strict sets to create the appropriate neuromuscular patterns of control before moving to other variations.\"\n- **Muscle priority sets:** the \"most undeveloped muscle groups are exercised first\" while energy and motivation are highest.\n- **Major to minor muscle sets:** largest muscles first, smaller later. \"Instructors often are taught that this is the most effective way to train and this is generally acceptable for the beginner in the general preparation phase, but it should not be stipulated as general principle for all training.\" Reasons to deviate: pre-exhaustion of \"stubborn muscles\", muscle priority, recovery from a previous workout on the same muscles, two or more workouts a day, and \"principles of *functional priority* discussed earlier in this text (i.e. technical skills > speed-strength > strength-speed > strength > strength-endurance > general endurance)\".\n- **Alternating sets:** staggered (major-minor) supersets that use the long rests of major-muscle sets; upper-lower body sets; agonist-antagonist sets (for example triceps then biceps).\n- **Tempo changing sets:** vary one repetition's speed, or the relative durations of the concentric, isometric and eccentric phases; \"like any other forms that stimulate the body in an unexpected way\", of possible value against \"habituation and stagnation (see Fig 1.32)\".\n- **Diminishing sets:** \"usually 100\" repetitions with one load in as few sets as possible, for example \"30-25-20-15-10\"; a newcomer may need \"10-12 sets\"; add load once 100 repetitions take \"4-5 sets\".\n- **Rep pausing sets:** a short break inside a set to recover, ease effort or restore motivation.\n- **Holding sets:** a static hold during or at the end of a movement, for muscles, \"tendons and joint capsules\", and balance in overhead lifts.\n- **Cumulative sets:** start with one repetition and add one each set until a set to failure; \"Usually ... one's 10RM, with 10 second rest intervals\".\n- **Peak contraction sets:** finish each repetition \"at the point of maximal muscle contraction (see Figs 2.8, 2.9) and not at the end of range when the force has already diminished significantly\", with intense isometric or quasi-isometric effort there.\n- **Continuous tension sets** (PDF p. 410): smooth repetitions without bounce, cheating or pause, joints never fully locked; cycling and arm ergometry give continuous tension naturally.\n\n**The PHA claim.** Upper-lower body sets are \"sometimes inappropriately called peripheral heart action (PHA) sets\". The switch to heavy activation of one body part \"tends to promote reflexive relaxation of another distant body part, a process first described by Setchinov in the early 1900s\". Proponents believe PHA \"avoids engorging one muscle part with blood and encourages the development of circulatory fitness\". The authors reply: \"The fact is that all human resisted movement and not just PHA encourages peripheral circulation and PHA offers no superior cardio-circulatory benefits compared with other systems of resistance training\" (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401). They cite no study for this. In 5.6 the same Setchinov effect is used for restoration between circuit stations rather than relaxation ([[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]). Inference: the two descriptions differ in emphasis, not in direction.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' general mechanism is habituation: a method that \"stimulate[s] the body in an unexpected way\" may break \"habituation and stagnation\" after long training (PDF p. 412). Specific reasons: strict sets build \"neuromuscular patterns of control\" in beginners; muscle priority puts the weakest group where energy is highest; staggered sets use otherwise idle rest; peak contraction sets avoid ending repetitions where force \"has already diminished significantly\" (PDF pp. 412-413). No study is cited for any of them.\n\n**When to apply** — In a dedicated hypertrophy phase, as variation once ordinary sets have stalled (the authors' stated use). Strict sets first for every beginner. Eric's baseline (line 41): \"teach basic movement skills and basic lifts before anything advanced\" **Inferred (transcript + Week 1 slides)**, which matches the authors' strict-sets-first rule. Use the functional-priority order, not size of muscle, when a sport session mixes qualities: technical skills, then speed-strength, strength-speed, strength, strength-endurance, general endurance (PDF p. 412).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not as sport strength methods: the authors' own frame calls them \"of minimal significance\" for most sport-specific strength (PDF p. 412). Do not treat major-to-minor order as a rule beyond beginners in general preparation (PDF p. 412). Do not choose upper-lower alternation for a cardiovascular advantage; the authors deny one, though without evidence. Eric's baseline (line 109): \"prioritize movement quality and confidence-through-competence over chasing hypertrophy or 1RM numbers, especially early in a training cycle\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: novelty methods aimed at a stalled hypertrophy block belong later in a cycle, not early.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own diminishing set: 100 repetitions with one load as 30-25-20-15-10, with the load raised once the 100 take 4-5 sets (PDF p. 412 / printed p. 401).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-bodybuilding-regimes-and-add-on-methods]]; [[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]; [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[heuristic--plan-variation-to-avoid-restrictive-neural-patterns]]; [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]; [[framework--precise-muscle-action-terminology]] (quasi-isometric)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":31,"oldLines":7,"newStart":31,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' general mechanism is habituation: a method that \"stimulate[s] the body in an unexpected way\" may break \"habituation and stagnation\" after long training (PDF p. 412). Specific reasons: strict sets build \"neuromuscular patterns of control\" in beginners; muscle priority puts the weakest group where energy is highest; staggered sets use otherwise idle rest; peak contraction sets avoid ending repetitions where force \"has already diminished significantly\" (PDF pp. 412-413). No study is cited for any of them."," ","-**When to apply** — In a dedicated hypertrophy phase, as variation once ordinary sets have stalled (the authors' stated use). Strict sets first for every beginner. Eric's baseline (line 41): \"teach basic movement skills and basic lifts before anything advanced\" **Inferred (transcript + Week 1 slides)**, which matches the authors' strict-sets-first rule. Use the functional-priority order, not size of muscle, when a sport session mixes qualities: technical skills, then speed-strength, strength-speed, strength, strength-endurance, general endurance (PDF p. 412).","+**When to apply** — In a dedicated hypertrophy phase, as variation once ordinary sets have stalled (the authors' stated use). Strict sets first for every beginner. Eric's baseline (line 41): \"teach basic movement skills and basic lifts before anything advanced\" **Inferred (transcript + Week 1 slides)**, which matches the authors' strict-sets-first rule. Inference: use the functional-priority order, not size of muscle, when a sport session mixes qualities: technical skills, then speed-strength, strength-speed, strength, strength-endurance, general endurance (PDF p. 412)."," "," **When NOT to apply** — Not as sport strength methods: the authors' own frame calls them \"of minimal significance\" for most sport-specific strength (PDF p. 412). Do not treat major-to-minor order as a rule beyond beginners in general preparation (PDF p. 412). Do not choose upper-lower alternation for a cardiovascular advantage; the authors deny one, though without evidence. Eric's baseline (line 109): \"prioritize movement quality and confidence-through-competence over chasing hypertrophy or 1RM numbers, especially early in a training cycle\" **Inferred (transcript + slides)**. Inference: novelty methods aimed at a stalled hypertrophy block belong later in a cycle, not early."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"92c30b0c-4c17-4787-95cb-b65fee16cc49","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
