{"parentUuid":"1d6de35d-6289-4bdb-bb0b-29c1b57d925d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqKh68SbvKdLbutZz88","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The page prints \"Lamber\", so I'll quote it as printed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1486,"cache_read_input_tokens":274168,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1486,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":40,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffqKR1FHq5ozPGdKjb3P","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5773e49d-b01a-45da-9381-1729d5fff43d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:41.822Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5773e49d-b01a-45da-9381-1729d5fff43d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqKh68SbvKdLbutZz88","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018aNHWSQagWWB2acNNEsREx","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md","old_string":"(Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lambert, 1965; printed \"Lamber\" in the text layer)","new_string":"(Nofsinger, 1963; \"Nelson & Lamber, 1965\", as printed)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1486,"cache_read_input_tokens":274168,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1486,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":40,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018aNHWSQagWWB2acNNEsREx":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md","old_string":"(Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lambert, 1965; printed \"Lamber\" in the text layer)","new_string":"(Nofsinger, 1963; \"Nelson & Lamber, 1965\", as printed)"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffqKR1FHq5ozPGdKjb3P","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"a0f7a825-e046-4b57-a840-d5618eb67d3b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:43.461Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"a0f7a825-e046-4b57-a840-d5618eb67d3b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018aNHWSQagWWB2acNNEsREx","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"255ff2ea-46a5-432c-b4e7-5a49fbfc33ec","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:43.548Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md","oldString":"(Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lambert, 1965; printed \"Lamber\" in the text layer)","newString":"(Nofsinger, 1963; \"Nelson & Lamber, 1965\", as printed)","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: reactive contrast methods\ncategory: [power, explosive-strength, contrast-training, potentiation]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Contrast Methods: Load Release, Pneumatics and the Heavy-Light Set\n\n**What it is** — \"Reactive methods are intended to develop power and explosive strength by imposing specific demands on nervous processes.\" Besides plyometrics (5.2.4), the category includes the *Contrast Methods* (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400, checked against the page image):\n\n- **Load release:** perform an exercise with an added load, \"then releasing the load suddenly during the lift, so that the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\". The stripping method \"offers a non-explosive variation of this method\", and the term also covers the sudden release of a parachute towed by a runner, swimmer or cyclist.\n- **Pneumatics:** \"much the same as the preceding contrast method\", on a compressed-air machine whose pressure can be \"quickly increased or decreased at any stage of a movement at the touch of a button\", so resistance drops \"fairly rapidly, though not as rapidly as with the load release method\" (Keiser machines are named).\n- **Heavy-light set:** \"performing a low repetition heavy set, decreasing the load by about 20%, taking a rest of 10-20 seconds, then executing the lighter set as rapidly as possible for 3-5 repetitions.\"\n- **Static-dynamic method:** preliminary isometric tension raises the dynamic and explosive strength of the following action through the after-effect (3.3.1, 3.4.2); its size depends on \"the duration of static loading, muscular fatigue and the interval between the static stimulus and the subsequent action\". This method is atomized from Chapter 4 in [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]] and not repeated here.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: for load release, \"the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\"; for the heavy-light set, \"The nervous system remains briefly adapted to the heavy load if the rest interval is brief and the muscles are temporarily able to operate in this super-stimulated condition to produce a very powerful and rapid contraction with the lightened load\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). No study is cited on this page.\n\n**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lambert, 1965; printed \"Lamber\" in the text layer), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, text layer). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]].\n\n**When to apply** — For power and explosive-strength work in athletes with a strength base, when the aim is a faster lighter effort straight after a heavier one. The heavy-light set needs only a bar and a quick load change. The authors also list \"Use any of the contrast training methods discussed earlier\" as a way through a sticking point (item 9, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402). Inference: in Eric's in-season week (baseline line 186: \"Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**), a heavy-light pairing is a low-volume way to keep a fast element in the lighter Wednesday lift; this is not something the authors or Eric propose.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not expect a reliable acute boost: the book's own cited Western studies found none in several tasks (PDF p. 175). The heavy-light set depends on the rest staying brief (10-20 s, PDF p. 411); with longer rest the authors' stated mechanism no longer applies. Do not use the stripping method as a power method; the authors call it the \"non-explosive variation\" (PDF p. 411). Chapter 5 classes contrast methods among shock methods for experienced athletes, used only after overtraining is ruled out ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: an athlete does a dip or pull-up \"as powerfully as possible with a load grasped between the legs, then release[s] the load early during the next repetition and accelerate[s] rapidly upwards for the remainder of the movement\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]; [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]]; [[heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release]] (parachute release as a contrast method); [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":22,"oldLines":7,"newStart":22,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: for load release, \"the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\"; for the heavy-light set, \"The nervous system remains briefly adapted to the heavy load if the rest interval is brief and the muscles are temporarily able to operate in this super-stimulated condition to produce a very powerful and rapid contraction with the lightened load\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). No study is cited on this page."," ","-**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; Nelson & Lambert, 1965; printed \"Lamber\" in the text layer), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, text layer). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]].","+**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; \"Nelson & Lamber, 1965\", as printed), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, text layer). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]."," "," **When to apply** — For power and explosive-strength work in athletes with a strength base, when the aim is a faster lighter effort straight after a heavier one. The heavy-light set needs only a bar and a quick load change. The authors also list \"Use any of the contrast training methods discussed earlier\" as a way through a sticking point (item 9, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402). Inference: in Eric's in-season week (baseline line 186: \"Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**), a heavy-light pairing is a low-volume way to keep a fast element in the lighter Wednesday lift; this is not something the authors or Eric propose."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a0f7a825-e046-4b57-a840-d5618eb67d3b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"255ff2ea-46a5-432c-b4e7-5a49fbfc33ec","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqKh68SbvKdLbutZz88","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ba8bvbsBQhaM6qUvakJzUA","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md","old_string":"(PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, text layer)","new_string":"(PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, checked against a crop of the page image)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1486,"cache_read_input_tokens":274168,"output_tokens":473,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1486},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":473,"cache_read_input_tokens":274168,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1486,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1486,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Ba8bvbsBQhaM6qUvakJzUA":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md","old_string":"(PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, text layer)","new_string":"(PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, checked against a crop of the page image)"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffqKR1FHq5ozPGdKjb3P","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4191d8ee-e3de-41c9-bacb-63880166fa28","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:44.761Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4191d8ee-e3de-41c9-bacb-63880166fa28","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ce914f6b-f81b-42fd-a40b-4c5550786125","agentId":"adfe1d740cda6c713","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ba8bvbsBQhaM6qUvakJzUA","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"4f3f6e67-fa7b-4842-ba9c-6519ef141766","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:11:44.844Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--contrast-methods-load-release-pneumatics-heavy-light-set.md","oldString":"(PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, text layer)","newString":"(PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, checked against a crop of the page image)","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.1\"\ntopic: reactive contrast methods\ncategory: [power, explosive-strength, contrast-training, potentiation]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Contrast Methods: Load Release, Pneumatics and the Heavy-Light Set\n\n**What it is** — \"Reactive methods are intended to develop power and explosive strength by imposing specific demands on nervous processes.\" Besides plyometrics (5.2.4), the category includes the *Contrast Methods* (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400, checked against the page image):\n\n- **Load release:** perform an exercise with an added load, \"then releasing the load suddenly during the lift, so that the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\". The stripping method \"offers a non-explosive variation of this method\", and the term also covers the sudden release of a parachute towed by a runner, swimmer or cyclist.\n- **Pneumatics:** \"much the same as the preceding contrast method\", on a compressed-air machine whose pressure can be \"quickly increased or decreased at any stage of a movement at the touch of a button\", so resistance drops \"fairly rapidly, though not as rapidly as with the load release method\" (Keiser machines are named).\n- **Heavy-light set:** \"performing a low repetition heavy set, decreasing the load by about 20%, taking a rest of 10-20 seconds, then executing the lighter set as rapidly as possible for 3-5 repetitions.\"\n- **Static-dynamic method:** preliminary isometric tension raises the dynamic and explosive strength of the following action through the after-effect (3.3.1, 3.4.2); its size depends on \"the duration of static loading, muscular fatigue and the interval between the static stimulus and the subsequent action\". This method is atomized from Chapter 4 in [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]] and not repeated here.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: for load release, \"the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\"; for the heavy-light set, \"The nervous system remains briefly adapted to the heavy load if the rest interval is brief and the muscles are temporarily able to operate in this super-stimulated condition to produce a very powerful and rapid contraction with the lightened load\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). No study is cited on this page.\n\n**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; \"Nelson & Lamber, 1965\", as printed), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, text layer). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]].\n\n**When to apply** — For power and explosive-strength work in athletes with a strength base, when the aim is a faster lighter effort straight after a heavier one. The heavy-light set needs only a bar and a quick load change. The authors also list \"Use any of the contrast training methods discussed earlier\" as a way through a sticking point (item 9, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402). Inference: in Eric's in-season week (baseline line 186: \"Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**), a heavy-light pairing is a low-volume way to keep a fast element in the lighter Wednesday lift; this is not something the authors or Eric propose.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Do not expect a reliable acute boost: the book's own cited Western studies found none in several tasks (PDF p. 175). The heavy-light set depends on the rest staying brief (10-20 s, PDF p. 411); with longer rest the authors' stated mechanism no longer applies. Do not use the stripping method as a power method; the authors call it the \"non-explosive variation\" (PDF p. 411). Chapter 5 classes contrast methods among shock methods for experienced athletes, used only after overtraining is ruled out ([[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: an athlete does a dip or pull-up \"as powerfully as possible with a load grasped between the legs, then release[s] the load early during the next repetition and accelerate[s] rapidly upwards for the remainder of the movement\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]; [[claim--static-dynamic-combined-isometric-concentric-method]]; [[heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release]] (parachute release as a contrast method); [[framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation]]; [[framework--strength-training-aims-and-method-chart-figs-7-1-and-7-2]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":22,"oldLines":7,"newStart":22,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' mechanisms: for load release, \"the contrast in stimulus received by the nervous system produces a rapid acceleration\"; for the heavy-light set, \"The nervous system remains briefly adapted to the heavy load if the rest interval is brief and the muscles are temporarily able to operate in this super-stimulated condition to produce a very powerful and rapid contraction with the lightened load\" (PDF p. 411 / printed p. 400). No study is cited on this page."," ","-**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; \"Nelson & Lamber, 1965\", as printed), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, text layer). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]].","+**Why `contested`.** The book's own 3.4.2 section reports that research on this question \"is contradictory, particularly in Western literature\": a faster unloaded movement directly after the same movement with a weight (Murray, 1959; Van Huss et al, 1962), but no such effect in other studies \"despite subjective claims by the athletes that their movements felt quicker after using loads\" (Nofsinger, 1963; \"Nelson & Lamber, 1965\", as printed), no vertical jump increase shortly after resistive exercise (Stockholm & Nelson, 1965), and worse shot-put results after throws with a heavier shot (Bischke & Morehouse, 1950) (PDF p. 175 / printed p. 164, checked against a crop of the page image). The heavy-light set and load release rest on exactly the effect those studies dispute: that a heavier load makes the following lighter effort faster. See [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]] and, for the Russian findings on load, timing and sequence, [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]."," "," **When to apply** — For power and explosive-strength work in athletes with a strength base, when the aim is a faster lighter effort straight after a heavier one. The heavy-light set needs only a bar and a quick load change. The authors also list \"Use any of the contrast training methods discussed earlier\" as a way through a sticking point (item 9, PDF p. 413 / printed p. 402). Inference: in Eric's in-season week (baseline line 186: \"Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**), a heavy-light pairing is a low-volume way to keep a fast element in the lighter Wednesday lift; this is not something the authors or Eric propose."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4191d8ee-e3de-41c9-bacb-63880166fa28","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
